\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Comments on Celestial CFT and 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 String Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Igor Mol
\end_layout

\begin_layout Affiliation
Federal University of Juiz de Fora,
 Minas Gerais,
 Brazil
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author Email
igormol@ime.unicamp.br
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
14 October 2026
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract
In a recent work,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ogawa2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 proposed a model for celestial conformal field theory (CFT) based on the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-Wess-Zumino-Novikov-Witten (WZNW) model.
 In this paper,
 we extend the model advanced by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ogawa2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 demonstrating how it can holographically generate tree-level MHV scattering amplitudes for both gluons and gravitons when analytically continued to the ultra-hyperbolic four-dimensional Klein space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{K}\cong\mathbf{R}^{2,2}$
\end_inset

,
 thereby offering an alternative to celestial Liouville theory.
 We construct a holographic dictionary in which vertex operators and conformal primaries in celestial CFT are derived from their worldsheet counterparts in Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}$
\end_inset

 (bosonic) string theory.
 Within this dictionary,
 we derive the celestial stress-energy tensor,
 compute the two- and three-point functions,
 and determine the celestial operator product expansion (OPE).
 Additionally,
 we derive a system of partial differential equations that characterises the celestial amplitudes of our model,
 utilising the Knizhnik–Zamolodchikov (KZ) equations and worldsheet Ward identities.
 In the Appendix,
 we provide a concise introduction to the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model,
 with emphasis on its connection to Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}$
\end_inset

 string theory.
\end_layout

