\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It has been recently observed in a series of works by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "stieberger2023yang,stieberger2023celestial,giribet2024remarks,melton2024celestial,mol2024holographic"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 within the framework of what may be termed ``celestial Liouville theory,'' that one can holographically derive the celestial amplitudes for gluons in pure Yang-Mills theory and for gravitons in General Relativity (GR),
 formulated perturbatively on a flat spacetime background,
 from the correlation functions of Liouville vertex operators in the semiclassical limit 
\begin_inset Formula $b\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

 of Liouville field theory.
 These Liouville vertex operators are appropriately dressed by an 
\begin_inset Formula $SO\left(2N\right)$
\end_inset

 level-one Kac-Moody current algebra on the celestial sphere,
 with the holographic correspondence understood in the large-
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 limit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 drawing upon the work of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "teschner1997conformal,teschner1999mini,teschner1999structure,teschner2000operator"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ribault2005h3+"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 it is well known that the semiclassical limit of Liouville field theory correlation functions coincides with the mini-superspace limit of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model.
 In the context of celestial conformal field theory (CFT),
 this raises the question of whether this coincidence hints at a deeper connection or whether there exists a more compelling model for celestial CFT based on the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model instead of Liouville theory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The purpose of this work is to explore this question,
 and we shall affirmatively demonstrate that the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model provides a more robust foundation for celestial CFT.
 Building on the insights of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "de1999string,giveon1998comments"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and interpreting the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model as the Euclidean continuation of 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 (bosonic) string theory,
 we propose a holographic dictionary wherein the vertex operators,
 correlation functions,
 and stress-energy tensor of celestial CFT are unambiguously derived from their counterparts in Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 string theory.
 We will demonstrate in detail how,
 via this holographic dictionary,
 one can derive the tree-level celestial amplitudes for the scattering of MHV gluons and gravitons,
 analytically continued to the ultra-hyperbolic Klein space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}^{2}_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 within the framework of celestial leaf amplitudes as introduced by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "casali2022celestial,melton2023celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset


\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
A point requiring special attention is the incorporation of helicity.
 We will show that the leading-trace,
 large-level limit of the correlators of vertex operators,
 constructed by dressing the scalar conformal primaries of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model with a level-one 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SO}(N)$
\end_inset

 Kac-Moody current algebra,
 reproduces a scalar 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}$
\end_inset

 Feynman-Witten contact diagram multiplied by the cyclic Parke-Taylor denominator.
 However,
 to account for the helicity numerator,
 we follow a suggestion of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "nair2005note"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and further dress these vertex operators with 
\emph on
auxiliary anti-commuting helicity variables
\emph default
.
 Acting on the resulting leading-trace,
 large-level limit of the correlation functions with a 
\emph on
helicity projector
\emph default
 then yields the desired tree-level MHV celestial leaf amplitudes for gluons and gravitons.
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Furthermore,
 as an application of our dictionary,
 we shall investigate the exact celestial two- and three-point functions,
 as well as the structure constants of the celestial operator product expansion (OPE).
 Additionally,
 we will derive a system of partial differential equations (PDEs) characterising the celestial amplitudes in the frequency-momentum representation,
 following from our dictionary via the Knizhnik–Zamolodchikov (KZ) equations and worldsheet Ward identities.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is worth noting that a similar model based on the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model was recently proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ogawa2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and we have benefited greatly from their insights.
 However,
 there are significant differences between their model and the one we present.
 Specifically,
 the hyperboloid 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ogawa2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

's work corresponds to the hyperbolic foliation of momentum space,
 whereas in our construction,
 the hyperboloid associated with the string target space is identified with the hyperbolic foliation of Klein space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}^{2}_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 as discussed in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "casali2022celestial,melton2023celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This distinction introduces a crucial difference:
 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ogawa2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

's model,
 the authors were compelled to define the celestial conformal primaries such that the celestial three-point amplitude vanishes,
 a constraint imposed by Minkowski-space kinematics.
 In contrast,
 since we work in the split signature of Klein space,
 we are not subject to this vanishing constraint,
 and indeed,
 we shall analyse the three-point function in our celestial CFT and its relationship to the structure constants of the celestial OPE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Another notable distinction is that 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ogawa2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 omitted consideration of the worldsheet variables in their construction of the final form of the correlation functions.
 In contrast,
 our holographic dictionary prescribes that one must integrate over the worldsheet coordinates to obtain a celestial amplitude from the correlation functions of 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 string theory.
 As we shall demonstrate in detail,
 the factors involving products of gamma functions,
 arising from worldsheet integrals,
 play a key role in determining the structure constants of the celestial OPE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This paper is structured as follows.
 In Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Holographic-Reconstruction-of"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 we begin by stating the postulates of our holographic dictionary,
 and move to the derivation of its consequences,
 starting with the holographic derivation of tree-level MHV amplitudes for both pure Yang-Mills theory and Einstein's gravity.
 In Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Correlation-Functions.-Operator"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 we will demonstrate how our construction uniquely determines the two- and three-point functions as well as the celestial OPE.
 Additionally,
 we will examine a system of partial differential equations characterising the celestial amplitudes,
 derived from our holographic dictionary in frequency space,
 and,
 in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Discussion"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 we summarise our findings and discuss potential avenues for further research stemming from this work.
\end_layout

