\begin_layout Standard
After the first version of the present manuscript had been completed,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "donnay2025parafermions"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 proposed an alternative formulation to generate the tree-level MHV leaf amplitudes for gluons utilising non-compact parafermions appertaining to the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbf{R})/\mathrm{U}(1)$
\end_inset

 coset model,
 dressed by a level-one 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SO}(N_{c})$
\end_inset

 Kac-Moody current algebra.
 In the semiclassical,
 large-
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c}$
\end_inset

 limits,
 the construction proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "donnay2025parafermions"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 reproduces the dressed-Liouville theory description by means of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

/Liouville theory correspondence,
 where the maximal spectral-flow violation plays a key role in their construction.
 The gluon helicities are represented with the same current algebra,
 without the need to introduce additional negative-weight fermions,
 as used in the dressed-Liouville theory model for celestial CFT.
 Therefore,
 the proposal of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "donnay2025parafermions"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 gives a complementary construction of the MHV leaf amplitudes in the framework provided by the WZNW model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Outline of the Holographic Dictionary
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
According to the holographic dictionary proposed in the present manuscript,
 the translation between the observables belonging to gauge theory and general relativity,
 defined perturbatively on four-dimensional Minkowski spacetime,
 and the observables constructed from the data provided by the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model,
 inspired by Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}$
\end_inset

 string theory,
 is accomplished in two parts.
 The first part concerns the application of the formalism of celestial leaf amplitudes to recast the entries of the four-dimensional 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

-matrix into a form that can be reproduced as a certain limit (which we shall precisely specify in the subsequent discussion) from the correlators of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model.
 The second part consists in identifying the celestial vertex operators for gluons and gravitons in the perturbative celestial CFT conjecturally dual to gauge theory and general relativity at tree level from the objects naturally defined from the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model,
 such as its conformal primary fields and boundary Kac-Moody current algebras.
 The principal claim of the present manuscript is a proposed correspondence between a subset of the observables of the celestial CFT and the minisuperspace limit of the helicity-projected correlators of appropriately dressed,
 worldsheet-integrated vertex operators constructed from the conformal primaries of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us begin by specifying the first part of the dictionary.
 The initial data consists of four-dimensional scattering amplitudes in the MHV sub-sectors of gauge theory and general relativity at tree level in Minkowski spacetime 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{M}\cong\mathbf{R}^{1,3}$
\end_inset

.
 We then translate these amplitudes from their momentum-space representation to the representation spanned by the conformal primary basis,
 which,
 for the scattering of massless particles,
 takes the form of a Mellin transform with respect to the frequencies of the scattered particles.
 We then perform the analytic continuation from Lorentzian signature to neutral,
 or Kleinian,
 signature,
 thereby arriving at Kleinian celestial amplitudes.
 We subsequently foliate the timelike 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{W}^{\mathrm{T}}$
\end_inset

 and spacelike 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{W}^{\mathrm{S}}$
\end_inset

 wedges of Klein space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{K}\cong\mathbf{R}^{2,2}$
\end_inset

,
 away from the null cone,
 by hyperbolic leaves,
 each of which is diffeomorphic to three-dimensional Lorentzian anti-de Sitter space with periodically identified time,
 given by the manifold 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}/\mathbf{Z}$
\end_inset

.
 In each wedge,
 this allows us to decompose the original Kleinian celestial amplitudes in four-dimensional space into a radial integral and three-dimensional integrals over the leaves of the hyperbolic foliation of Klein space.
 These three-dimensional integrals,
 together with the appropriate kinematical factors,
 define the celestial leaf amplitudes,
 which admit a representation as conformal correlators on the two-dimensional conformal boundary of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}/\mathbf{Z}$
\end_inset

,
 namely the Lorentzian celestial torus 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{CT}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 since our final objective is to reproduce those observables in terms of quantities naturally defined by the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model,
 we must further apply a second analytic continuation to the celestial amplitudes from the Lorentzian signature of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}/\mathbf{Z}$
\end_inset

 to arrive at the Euclidean signature of the three-dimensional Lobachevsky space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

,
 which is realised as the upper sheet of the unit hyperboloid embedded in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}^{1,3}$
\end_inset

.
 This second analytic continuation requires a careful consideration of the choices of branches,
 integration contours,
 phases and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathtt{i}\varepsilon$
\end_inset

-prescriptions.
 For clarity,
 the leaf amplitudes analytically continued to the Lobachevsky space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

 will be called 
\emph on
Euclidean leaf correlators
\emph default
.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Euclidean leaf correlators are thereby the observables that we desire to recover from the data appertaining to the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model.
 Before proceeding to the second part of the dictionary,
 let us recall from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "melton2023celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 that the original distributional Kleinian celestial amplitudes are reconstructed from their leaf counterparts by performing a radial integration of the leaf amplitudes and by summing the contributions originating from the timelike 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{W}^{\mathrm{T}}$
\end_inset

 and spacelike 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{W}^{\mathrm{S}}$
\end_inset

 wedges of Klein space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now we can discuss the second part of the dictionary,
 which consists in identifying the celestial vertex operators from the data belonging to the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model,
 and in introducing a procedure by means of which the Euclidean celestial amplitudes can be generated from their correlators.
 A spinless celestial vertex operator 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}_{\Delta}$
\end_inset

 with conformal dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta=1+\mathtt{i}\lambda$
\end_inset

 is constructed from a conformal primary field 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j}$
\end_inset

 of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model with isospin 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

,
 and the celestial conformal dimension is determined by the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 isospin by 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{j}=-2j=1-2\mathtt{i}s$
\end_inset

.
 The primary field of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model is subsequently dressed by a boundary Kac-Moody current algebra 
\begin_inset Formula $J^{\mathsf{a}}(x)$
\end_inset

,
 where the boundary means the boundary of the Lobachevsky space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

,
 which is diffeomorphic to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{S}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and charted by the holomorphic coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Furthermore,
 we also dress the conformal primaries by Grassmann-odd auxiliary variables modelling the helicity state of the boson under consideration.
 We implement this part of the proposed holographic dictionary in subsection II.B.2,
 where we identify the celestial gluon operators,
 and in subsection II.C.2,
 where we construct the celestial graviton operators.
 Finally,
 the desired Euclidean celestial leaf amplitudes for gluons and gravitons in the MHV sub-sectors of gauge theory and general relativity are generated by taking the minisuperspace limit of the correlators of the celestial vertex operators.
 It is also necessary to apply a projector operator to project the helicity states of the scattered bosons,
 which we encoded by the Grassmann-valued auxiliary variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can therefore summarise the limits in which the proposed holographic dictionary works as follows.
 From the viewpoint of four-dimensional bulk physics,
 we are working in the MHV sub-sectors of Yang-Mills theory and general relativity at tree level.
 In the case of gauge theory,
 we should also add that we are working in the large-
\begin_inset Formula $N_{c}$
\end_inset

 regime.
 From the viewpoint of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model,
 we are taking the large-level,
 or minisuperspace,
 limit,
 and applying a helicity projector operator.
 Let us close our outline of the character of the proposed dictionary by adding that,
 at present,
 we are currently finalising a forthcoming manuscript in which we have made a significant development according to which quantum-mechanical corrections to gauge-theory amplitudes,
 namely,
 loops in the planar regime of the theory,
 can be generated from the finite-
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 contribution of the correlators of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model,
 thus confirming our intuition in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "mol2024partial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Question of Unitarity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Note.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following section was originally written in response to my referee at 
\emph on
The European Physical Journal C
\emph default
,
 and I must thank the referee for encouraging me to include it in my manuscript.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\SpecialChar ligaturebreak

\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now,
 let me explain how the non-unitarity expected from the celestial CFT is compatible with the proposed holographic dictionary based on the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model.
 To make my argument unambiguous,
 I believe it should be helpful to begin by distinguishing some closely related but nonetheless distinct notions:
 the unitarity of the worldsheet-matter CFT appertaining to the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model;
 the absence of reflection positivity underlying the celestial CFT,
 in the sense expected from conventional radial quantisation;
 and the unitarity of the action of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbf{C})$
\end_inset

 on the zero-mode Hilbert space 
\begin_inset Formula $L^{2}(\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3})$
\end_inset

 of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model in the group-theoretic sense.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First,
 consider the following statement concerning the target space of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\cong\frac{\mathrm{SO}^{+}(1,3)}{\mathrm{SO}(3)}\cong\frac{\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbf{C})}{\mathrm{SU}(2)}\,.\label{eq:-8}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Geometrically,
 the meaning of the above assertion is that the target space underlying the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model is a homogeneous space.
 Nonetheless,
 we should emphasise that the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model belongs to a 
\emph on
different
\emph default
 class of conformal field theories than that defined by the conventional 
\begin_inset Formula $G/H$
\end_inset

 coset construction.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In fact,
 in the ordinary vector-gauged realisation of a 
\begin_inset Formula $G/H$
\end_inset

 coset CFT,
 we begin with a WZNW model with target space 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 and a global symmetry 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{\mathrm{L}}\times G_{\mathrm{R}}$
\end_inset

.
 Subsequently,
 we gauge a diagonally embedded subgroup 
\begin_inset Formula $H\subset G_{\mathrm{L}}\times G_{\mathrm{R}}$
\end_inset

.
 Now,
 by applying this construction with 
\begin_inset Formula $G=\mathrm{SO}^{+}(1,3)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $H=\mathrm{SO}(3)$
\end_inset

,
 we arrive at a model that does not have an 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SO}^{+}(1,3)$
\end_inset

 symmetry,
 because the symmetry described by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SO}^{+}(1,3)_{L}\times\mathrm{SO}^{+}(1,3)_{\mathrm{R}}$
\end_inset

 is broken by gauging the diagonally embedded 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SO}(3)$
\end_inset

 subgroup.
 In other words,
 
\emph on
the full original Lorentz symmetry does not survive as a global symmetry of the gauged model
\emph default
.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the other hand,
 the model defined by performing the analytic continuation of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SU}(2)$
\end_inset

 WZNW model is a sigma-model possessing 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

 as target space,
 which 
\emph on
does
\emph default
 have an 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SO}^{+}(1,3)$
\end_inset

 symmetry.
 Therefore,
 even if the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model is a sigma-model whose target space is a homogeneous space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SO}^{+}(1,3)/\mathrm{SO}(3)$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbf{C})/\mathrm{SU}(2)$
\end_inset

,
 it is not an 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SO}^{+}(1,3)/\mathrm{SO}(3)$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbf{C})/\mathrm{SU}(2)$
\end_inset

 coset model in the sense of the conventional vector-gauged realisation of the coset construction,
 explained,
 for example,
 in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "francesco1997conformal"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 See,
 for example,
 subsection 5.2 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "murthy2026localization"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 or the introduction of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "gawedzki1992non"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The physical significance of the preceding observations is that the familiar unitarity properties underlying the coset CFTs,
 originating from compact WZNW models at positive integer levels,
 should not be inferred merely from the fact that the target manifold is a homogeneous space.
 In especial,
 the worldsheet-matter CFT appertaining to the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model,
 on which our proposed holographic dictionary is based,
 is 
\emph on
non-unitary
\emph default
.
 The reason is that the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model can be understood as originating from the analytic continuation of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SU}(2)$
\end_inset

 WZNW model,
 and the procedure of analytic continuation multiplies the Wess-Zumino term by the imaginary unity,
 thereby making that contribution imaginary when the worldsheet has Lorentzian signature,
 a point emphasised in section 5 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "murthy2026localization"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The resulting worldsheet-matter CFT thereby lacks the reality properties required for conventional unitarity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that there exists no contradiction between the above remarks and the positivity of the zero-mode sector of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model.
 Recall that the Hilbert state-space of the zero-mode sector is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathscr{H}_{\text{zero-mode}}=L^{2}(\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3},\mathsf{d}h)\,,
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and carries a unitary 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbf{C})$
\end_inset

 representation.
 However,
 the contribution originating from the WZ term vanishes in the zero-mode truncation.
 Thus,
 positivity of the zero-mode inner product does not imply reflection positivity of the complete worldsheet-matter CFT.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Puzzle of the Central Charge
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now we should address the following puzzle.
 First,
 recall that the existence of the extended BMS algebra (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "BarnichTroessaert2010"
literal "false"

\end_inset

),
 possessing two centre-less Virasoro sub-algebras,
 served as a motivation (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "PasterskiShao2017"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) for the reformulation of the scattering amplitudes in perturbative quantum gravity as the correlation functions of a putative CFT residing on the celestial sphere at null infinity,
 referred to as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

celestial CFT.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 A significant consequence of this reformulation was the re-interpretation (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "KapecEtAl2014Virasoro"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "KapecEtAl2017StressTensor"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) of the sub-leading soft gravity theorem as a conformal Ward identity for the physical celestial stress-tensor 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{T}^{\mathrm{CCFT}}_{zz}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the other hand,
 in the present manuscript,
 we shall propose that the large-level limit of the correlation functions of a set of appropriately dressed vertex operators,
 constructed from the conformal primary fields of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model,
 reproduces the tree-level MHV leaf amplitudes for gravitons,
 thereby suggesting a candidate model for the perturbative celestial CFT approximating the tree-level MHV sub-sector of general relativity defined perturbatively around flat space.
 However,
 the boundary-spacetime Virasoro algebra realised by the string theory construction on Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}$
\end_inset

,
 which resides in the boundary of the conformal completion of the three-dimensional Lobachevsky space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

,
 has a non-zero central charge related to the three-dimensional Newton constant.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore,
 we must clarify how our proposal is consistent with the fact that,
 as argued by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "donnay2021bms"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 under a set of boundary conditions and flux prescriptions,
 the classical BMS flux algebra can be shown to close under the standard Peierls bracket without a central term,
 and that the corresponding celestial stress-energy tensor OPE has a vanishing Virasoro central charge.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our answer to this question is based on an observation due to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Nguyen2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and in order to explain it precisely,
 we should briefly recall some geometric notions about asymptotically flat spacetimes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 denote a coordinate function associated to a generator of null infinity 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{I}$
\end_inset

,
 and consider an oriented system of real coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{A}$
\end_inset

 charting the corresponding celestial cuts.
 We may accordingly represent the metric tensor on the celestial cuts by 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{AB}=\mathrm{e}^{2\psi}q_{0,AB}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{0,AB}$
\end_inset

 is a reference metric possessing constant curvature,
 whereas 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{u}\psi=0$
\end_inset

.
 In addition,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{D}_{A}$
\end_inset

 be the Levi-Civita covariant derivative operator,
 metrically compatible with 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{AB}$
\end_inset

,
 and for any given tensor 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{AB}$
\end_inset

,
 we define its trace-free part by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(X_{AB})^{\mathrm{TF}}\coloneqq X_{AB}-\frac{1}{2}q_{AB}q^{CD}X_{CD}\,.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We now introduce the lift to null infinity of a symmetric,
 trace-free tensor residing on the celestial cuts,
 given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{T}_{AB}\coloneqq2\,(\mathrm{D}_{A}\mathrm{D}_{B}\psi+\mathrm{D}_{A}\psi\,\mathrm{D}_{B}\psi)^{\mathrm{TF}}\,,\quad\mathcal{T}_{u\mu}\coloneqq0\,.
\]

\end_inset

Thus,
 given a local complex conformal coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 charting a coordinate neighbourhood of the celestial sphere,
 in terms of which 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{AB}\mathrm{d}x^{A}\mathrm{d}x^{B}=\mathrm{e}^{2\sigma}\mathrm{d}z\mathrm{d}\overline{z}$
\end_inset

,
 we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T}_{zz}=2\,(\partial^{2}_{z}\psi-2(\partial_{z}\sigma)(\partial_{z}\psi)+(\partial_{z}\psi)^{2})\,.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Next,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{N}^{zz}$
\end_inset

 denote the covariant news tensor with indices raised with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{AB}$
\end_inset

,
 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{N}^{zz}=q^{zA}q^{zB}\boldsymbol{N}_{AB}$
\end_inset

,
 which describes gravitational radiation through 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathscr{I}$
\end_inset

.
 In what follows,
 we shall specialise our discussion to the round-sphere conformal frame and set the super-translation mode to zero.
 We denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 the conformal factor appertaining to the round-sphere metric,
 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{AB}\mathrm{d}x^{A}\mathrm{d}x^{B}=\gamma^{2}\mathrm{d}z\mathrm{d}\overline{z}$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
 the physical celestial stress-tensor can be stated as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{T}^{\text{CCFT}}_{zz}=-\frac{1}{32\pi G_{\mathrm{N}}}\int_{\mathscr{C}}\frac{\mathrm{d}\overline{z}}{2\pi\mathrm{i}}\int_{\mathbf{R}}\mathrm{d}u\;u\gamma^{2}\,\bigl(\mathrm{D}^{3}_{z}-2\mathcal{T}_{zz}\mathrm{D}_{z}-(\mathrm{D}_{z}\mathcal{T}_{zz})\bigr)\,\boldsymbol{N}^{zz}\,.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Note that,
 in the preceding equality,
 the contour integral 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{\mathscr{C}}\frac{\mathrm{d}\overline{z}}{2\pi\mathrm{i}}$
\end_inset

 is utilised in order to extract the holomorphic celestial current density.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now,
 recall that the holographic dictionary which we carefully explained in the preceding subsection exists within the context of the uplifting of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}/\mathrm{CFT}_{2}$
\end_inset

 correspondence to flat-space holography,
 in the sense of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "BallEtAl2019"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Accordingly,
 following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Nguyen2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we shall argue that there is nothing in the geometry underlying this uplift which compels us to identify the physical celestial stress-tensor 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{T}^{\mathrm{CCFT}}_{zz}$
\end_inset

 with the boundary-spacetime Virasoro stress-tensor 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{t}_{\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}}$
\end_inset

 originating from the string theory construction on Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}$
\end_inset

 space.
 To see why,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}}$
\end_inset

 be the central charge of the boundary-spacetime Virasoro algebra of the underlying string-theory construction,
 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $|\Psi\rangle$
\end_inset

 be a boundary state whose associated semi-classical geometry belongs to the family of locally super-rotated vacua.
 Then,
 following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "BalasubramanianKraus1999"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Nguyen2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 it can be shown that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\langle\Psi|\boldsymbol{t}_{\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}}|\Psi\rangle}{\langle\Psi|\Psi\rangle}\sim\frac{c_{\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}}}{12}\mathcal{T}_{zz}[q_{AB},\psi]\,,\label{eq:-9}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is non-zero for the non-trivial Schwarzian profiles,
 in the family of states to which 
\begin_inset Formula $|\Psi\rangle$
\end_inset

 belongs.
 In Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:-9"

\end_inset

),
 we should evaluate 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}_{zz}$
\end_inset

 on the geometric data 
\begin_inset Formula $\{q_{AB},\psi\}$
\end_inset

,
 associated with the locally super-rotated vacuum corresponding to 
\begin_inset Formula $|\Psi\rangle$
\end_inset

,
 which we introduced above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore,
 as emphasised by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Nguyen2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the preceding result may be physically interpreted as saying that,
 under the uplifting of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}/\mathrm{CFT}_{2}$
\end_inset

 correspondence to the flat-space holography,
 the classical expectation value of the boundary stress tensor 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{t}_{\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}}$
\end_inset

,
 with respect to boundary states whose semiclassical geometry can be realised as a locally super-rotated vacuum,
 is mapped into 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}_{zz}$
\end_inset

 rather than the physical celestial stress-tensor 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{T}^{\mathrm{CCFT}}_{zz}$
\end_inset

 .
 Furthermore,
 the shear of the super-rotated vacuum is 
\begin_inset Formula $C^{\text{vac.}}_{zz}=u\mathcal{T}_{zz}$
\end_inset

,
 so that the covariant news becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{N}_{zz}=\partial_{u}C^{\text{vac.}}_{zz}-\mathcal{T}_{zz}=0$
\end_inset

,
 since 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{u}\mathcal{T}_{zz}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Hence 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}_{zz}$
\end_inset

 may be non-zero whereas the vacuum remains non-radiative.
 (Remember that we specialised our discussion to the round-sphere conformal frame,
 and that we assumed that the super-translation mode is zero.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore,
 we have no reason compelling us to identify the holographic uplifting of the stress-tensor underlying the boundary-spacetime Virasoro algebra,
 originating from the string-theory construction,
 with the physical celestial stress-energy tensor.
 Consequently,
 no contradiction follows merely from the difference between their central charges.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Holographic Construction of Tree-Level MHV Amplitudes
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Holographic-Reconstruction-of"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We shall start our discussion in Subsection 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:The-Holographic-Dictionary"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 by postulating the entries of our holographic dictionary,
 which maps correlation functions,
 vertex operators,
 and conformal primaries in Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 (bosonic) string theory to their counterparts in celestial CFT.
 This construction builds upon the model advanced by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ogawa2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Subsequently,
 in Subsections 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Yang-Mills-Theory"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Einstein's-Gravity"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 we will demonstrate that our dictionary enables the holographic derivation of tree-level MHV scattering amplitudes in both Yang-Mills theory and Einstein's gravity.
 The results presented herein build upon a series of works developed in the context of 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}/CFT_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 especially those by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "de1999string,giveon1998comments,maldacena2001strings,maldacena2001strings2,giribet2001correlators,bars1999string"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and celestial Liouville theory,
 including those by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "stieberger2023yang,stieberger2023celestial,giribet2024remarks,melton2024celestial,mol2024holographic"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Holographic Dictionary
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:The-Holographic-Dictionary"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first entry of our holographic dictionary draws inspiration from the analysis presented by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
after "Section 4"
key "de1999string"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and posits that to each sequence of worldsheet vertex operators 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{1}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 ...,
 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{n}\left(x_{n};z_{n}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 there corresponds a family of celestial vertex operators 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{V}_{1}\left(x_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 ...,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{V}_{n}\left(x_{n}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 such that the correlation function of the latter is expressed in terms of the correlation function of the former as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \mathcal{V}_{1}\left(x_{1}\right)...\mathcal{V}_{n}\left(x_{n}\right)\right\rangle =\frac{1}{W}\prod^{n}_{i=1}\int d^{2}z_{i}\left\langle \left\langle V_{1}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)...V_{n}\left(x_{n};z_{n}\right)\right\rangle \right\rangle ,\label{eq:Zero-Entry}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 represents a normalisation constant associated with the worldsheet ``area,'' ensuring that the worldsheet integrals are well-defined and finite.
 Here,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle ...\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 denotes the correlation function in celestial CFT,
 while 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle \left\langle ...\right\rangle \right\rangle $
\end_inset

 denotes the correlation function in the worldsheet CFT.
 This formulation is consistent with the conventional framework of string perturbation theory (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "d1988geometry"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "polchinski1998string"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 The worldsheet integrals in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Zero-Entry"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) represent a key distinction between our holographic dictionary and that proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ogawa2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In contrast to their approach,
 where the worldsheet variables 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i},\bar{z}_{i}$
\end_inset

 are omitted in the final form of the correlation function,
 we incorporate these variables by performing explicit integration over the worldsheet.
 As will be demonstrated in Subsection 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Operator-Product-Expansion"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 the product of gamma functions that arises from the worldsheet integrals in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Zero-Entry"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) plays an important role in determining the structure constants of the celestial operator product expansion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second entry of our holographic dictionary derives its inspiration from the model proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ogawa2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 yet it introduces a significant distinction arising from the split signature of Klein space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}^{2}_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 to which we shall analytically continue the scattering amplitudes of Yang-Mills theory and Einstein's gravity in Subsections 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Yang-Mills-Theory"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Einstein's-Gravity"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 We propose that each conformal primary 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j}\left(x;z\right)$
\end_inset

 with spin 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model corresponds to a celestial (scalar) wavefunction 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\Delta}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 with conformal weight 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

 in the celestial CFT,
 such that the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW spin and the celestial scaling dimension are related by the equation 
\begin_inset Formula $j+\Delta/2=0$
\end_inset

,
 as suggested by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ogawa2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 based on an analysis of the transformation properties of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j}\left(x;z\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\Delta}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 under conformal transformations.
 Thus,
 the principal distinction between our proposal and that of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ogawa2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 lies in the signature of the spacetime:
 while 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ogawa2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 works in Minkowski signature,
 where Lorentzian kinematics necessitated selecting a celestial conformal primary as a linear combination of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j}\left(x;z\right)$
\end_inset

 and its shadow transform–resulting in a vanishing three-point function–our model works in the split signature,
 freeing us from the constraint of the vanishing three-point function.
 In fact,
 as we shall demonstrate in Subsections 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Three-Point-Function"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Operator-Product-Expansion"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 the three-point function in our model is closely related to the structure constants of the celestial operator product expansion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The third and final postulate of our holographic dictionary takes its form from the construction proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "giveon1998comments"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 which establishes a correspondence between worldsheet and spacetime Kac-Moody and Virasoro currents in the context of 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 string theory,
 reviewed in detail in Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:First-Entry:-Kac-Moody"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 The third entry can be stated as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
First,
 we assert that,
 given a set of worldsheet level-
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{k}_{G}$
\end_inset

 Kac-Moody currents 
\begin_inset Formula $j^{a}\left(z\right)$
\end_inset

,
 transforming under the fundamental representation of a Lie group 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 with structure constants 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{abc}$
\end_inset

,
 and satisfying the OPE:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
j^{a}\left(z\right)j^{b}\left(w\right)\sim\frac{\hat{k}_{G}\delta^{ab}}{\left(z-w\right)^{2}}+\frac{if^{abc}j^{c}\left(w\right)}{z-w},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

there corresponds a family of celestial Kac-Moody currents 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)=-\frac{1}{k}\int d^{2}zj^{a}\left(z\right)\bar{J}\left(\bar{x};\bar{z}\right)\Phi^{j=1}\left(x;z\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is the level of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{J}\left(\bar{x};\bar{z}\right)$
\end_inset

 is the worldsheet current introduced in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Currents-2"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) of Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Symmetries"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 It is shown therein that these celestial CFT currents obey the OPE:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)\mathcal{O}^{b}\left(y\right)\sim\frac{\hat{k}_{G}\mathcal{I}\delta^{ab}}{\left(x-y\right)^{2}}+\frac{if^{abc}\mathcal{O}^{c}\left(y\right)}{x-y},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

with central extension:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}\coloneqq\frac{1}{k^{2}}\int d^{2}zJ\left(x;z\right)\bar{J}\left(\bar{x};\bar{z}\right)\Phi\left(x;z\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset

It is further demonstrated in the Appendix,
 following the work of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "giveon1998comments"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 that the above definition of the central extension is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x,\bar{x}$
\end_inset

,
 thereby confirming the mathematical consistency of the OPE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Second,
 we postulate that the celestial stress-energy tensor 
\begin_inset Formula $T\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 is expressed in terms of worldsheet currents and primaries as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
T\left(x\right)=\frac{1}{2}\oint\frac{dz}{2\pi i}\left(\partial_{x}J\partial_{x}\lambda+2\lambda\partial^{2}_{x}J\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and satisfies the Ward identities:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
T\left(x\right)\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)\sim\frac{h}{\left(x-y\right)^{2}}\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)+\frac{1}{x-y}\partial_{y}\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
T\left(x_{1}\right)T\left(x_{2}\right)\sim\frac{c}{\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)^{4}}+\frac{2T\left(x_{2}\right)}{\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)^{2}}+\frac{\partial_{x_{2}}T\left(x_{2}\right)}{x_{1}-x_{2}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

as expected from a generator of the Virasoro algebra in the celestial CFT.
 The celestial stress-energy tensor and the derivation of these Ward identities from the aforementioned definition will be discussed in detail in Subsection 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Virasoro-Current-Algebra"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Having now articulated the three entries of our holographic dictionary,
 we shall proceed to derive their implications.
 In the subsequent Subsections 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Yang-Mills-Theory"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Einstein's-Gravity"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 we will demonstrate that the first two entries allow for a holographic construction of tree-level MHV celestial amplitudes for gluons and gravitons.
 Thereafter,
 in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "sec:Correlation-Functions.-Operator"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 we will address the completeness and consistency of these postulates with the mathematical formalism of conformal field theory by computing the two- and three-point functions and determining the structure constants of the OPE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Yang-Mills Theory
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Yang-Mills-Theory"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this subsection,
 we begin by succinctly reviewing the formalism of celestial leaf amplitudes introduced by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "melton2023celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 which prescribes an analytic continuation of celestial amplitudes from Minkowski spacetime to the ultra-hyperbolic Klein space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}^{2}_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Following this,
 we discuss the holographic construction of these Klein space amplitudes for pure Yang-Mills theory within the framework of the holographic dictionary proposed in these notes,
 which establishes a correspondence between Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 (bosonic) string theory and celestial CFT.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Celestial Leaf Amplitudes for Yang-Mills Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Notation*
In what follows,
 we denote points on the celestial sphere by 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i},\bar{x}_{i}\in\mathbf{CP}^{1}$
\end_inset

,
 referred to as 
\emph on
celestial coordinates
\emph default
.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu^{A}\coloneqq\left(x,1\right)^{T}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{\nu}^{\dot{A}}\coloneqq\left(\bar{x},1\right)^{T}$
\end_inset

 be a pair of two-component spinors parametrised by the celestial coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $x,\bar{x}$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\emph on
standard null vector 
\emph default
is then defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $q^{\mu}\left(x,\bar{x}\right)\coloneqq(\sigma^{\mu})_{A\dot{B}}\nu^{A}\bar{\nu}^{\dot{B}}$
\end_inset

.
 For each family of two-component spinors 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu^{A}_{i}\coloneqq\sqrt{\omega_{i}}\left(x_{i},1\right)^{T}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{\mu}^{\dot{A}}_{i}\coloneqq\sqrt{\omega_{i}}\left(\bar{x}_{i},1\right)^{T}$
\end_inset

,
 indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,...,n$
\end_inset

,
 and describing the momenta of a sequence of gluons with frequencies 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{1},...,\omega_{n}$
\end_inset

,
 the associated four-momenta are given by 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\mu}_{i}=(\sigma^{\mu})_{A\dot{B}}\mu^{A}_{i}\bar{\mu}^{\dot{B}}_{i}=\omega_{i}q^{\mu}\left(x_{i},\bar{x}_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 The spinor-helicity brackets are then defined as 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle ij\right\rangle \coloneqq\varepsilon_{AB}\mu^{A}_{i}\mu^{B}_{j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[ij\right]\coloneqq\varepsilon_{\dot{A}\dot{B}}\bar{\mu}^{\dot{A}}_{i}\bar{\mu}^{\dot{B}}_{i}$
\end_inset

,
 such that,
 in our conventions,
 
\begin_inset Formula $2p_{i}\cdot p_{j}=\left\langle ij\right\rangle \left[ij\right]$
\end_inset

,
 which follows from 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{tr}\left(\sigma^{\mu}\bar{\sigma}^{\nu}\right)=2\eta^{\mu\nu}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The starting point of our analysis is the Parke-Taylor formula (cf.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "parke1986amplitude"
literal "false"

\end_inset

;
 for a contemporary pedagogical review,
 see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "elvang2013scattering,badger2024scattering"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) for the tree-level scattering amplitude 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{n}$
\end_inset

 of MHV gluons 
\begin_inset Formula $1^{-}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{-}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $3^{+}$
\end_inset

,
 ...,
 
\begin_inset Formula $n^{+}$
\end_inset

,
 which is expressed in spinor-helicity variables as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}_{n}=\frac{\left\langle 12\right\rangle ^{4}}{\left\langle 12\right\rangle \left\langle 23\right\rangle ...\left\langle n1\right\rangle }ig^{n-2}_{\text{YM}}\delta^{\left(4\right)}\left(\sum^{n}_{i=1}p^{\mu}_{i}\right),\label{eq:Parke-Taylor}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\mu}_{i}$
\end_inset

 denotes the four-momentum of the 
\begin_inset Formula $i^{\text{th}}$
\end_inset

 gluon and 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{\text{YM}}$
\end_inset

 is the Yang-Mills coupling constant.
 Utilising the celestial holography dictionary,
 as elaborated upon in detail by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pasterski2021lectures,raclariu2021lectures,strominger2018lectures,puhmcelestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the spinor-helicity variables are connected to the frequencies 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the complex parametrisation 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i},\bar{x}_{i}\in\mathbf{CP}^{1}$
\end_inset

 of the celestial sphere through the relation:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle ij\right\rangle =\sqrt{\omega_{i}\omega_{j}}x_{ij}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore,
 recalling the integral representation of the 
\begin_inset Formula $4d$
\end_inset

 Dirac delta function,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta^{\left(4\right)}\left(p\right)=1/\left(2\pi\right)^{4}\int d^{4}Ye^{ip\cdot Y}$
\end_inset

,
 and substituting the frequencies 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{i}$
\end_inset

 and celestial coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i},\bar{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 into Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Parke-Taylor"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 we arrive at the expression:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}_{n}=ig^{n-2}_{\text{YM}}\frac{x^{4}_{12}}{x_{12}x_{23}...x_{n1}}\int\frac{d^{4}Y}{\left(2\pi\right)^{2}}\prod^{n}_{i=1}\omega^{\alpha_{i}}_{i}e^{i\omega_{i}q\left(x_{i},\bar{x}_{i}\right)\cdot Y},\label{eq:Step-19}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where we introduce constants to streamline the notation:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}=\alpha_{2}=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{3}=...=\alpha_{n}=-1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using the prescriptions outlined in the celestial holography dictionary,
 the celestial amplitude corresponding to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{n}$
\end_inset

 is given by the 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

-regulated Mellin transform of Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-19"

\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mathcal{A}}_{n}=\prod^{n}_{i=1}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\omega_{i}\omega^{\Delta_{i}-1}_{i}e^{-\varepsilon\omega_{i}}\mathcal{A}_{n},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which results in:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mathcal{A}}_{n}=ig^{n-2}_{\text{YM}}\frac{x^{4}_{12}}{x_{12}x_{23}...x_{n1}}\int\frac{d^{4}Y}{\left(2\pi\right)^{4}}\prod^{n}_{i=1}\frac{\Gamma\left(2\rho_{i}\right)}{\left(\varepsilon-iq\left(x_{i},\bar{x}_{i}\right)\cdot Y\right)^{2\rho_{i}}},\label{eq:Step-21}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where we define 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{i}\coloneqq\left(\Delta_{i}+\alpha_{i}\right)/2$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By analytically continuing the spacetime integral in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-21"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) to Klein space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}^{2}_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 and employing the approach described by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "melton2023celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we derive the expression:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \int\frac{d^{4}Y}{\left(2\pi\right)^{4}}\prod^{n}_{i=1}\frac{\Gamma\left(2\rho_{i}\right)}{\left(\varepsilon-iq\left(x_{i},\bar{x}_{i}\right)\cdot Y\right)^{2\rho_{i}}}\\
 & =\frac{\delta\left(4-2\sum_{i}\rho_{i}\right)}{\left(2\pi\right)^{3}}\int_{\hat{y}^{2}=1}d^{3}\hat{y}\prod^{n}_{i=1}\frac{\Gamma\left(2\rho_{i}\right)}{\left(\varepsilon-iq\left(x_{i},\bar{x}_{i}\right)\cdot\hat{y}\right)}+\left(\bar{x}_{i}\leftrightarrow-\bar{x}_{i}\right).
\end{align}

\end_inset

Here,
 the notation 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\bar{x}_{i}\leftrightarrow-\bar{x}_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 denotes the repetition of the preceding term with the variables 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 substituted by 
\begin_inset Formula $-\bar{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Consequently,
 Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-21"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) can be reformulated as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mathcal{A}}_{n}=ig^{n-2}_{\text{YM}}\frac{\delta\left(4-2\sum_{i}\rho_{i}\right)}{\left(2\pi\right)^{3}}\frac{x^{4}_{12}}{x_{12}x_{23}...x_{n1}}\int_{\hat{y}^{2}=1}d^{3}\hat{y}\prod^{n}_{i=1}\frac{\Gamma\left(2\rho_{i}\right)}{\left(\varepsilon-iq\left(x_{i},\bar{x}_{i}\right)\cdot\hat{y}\right)}+\left(\bar{x}_{i}\leftrightarrow-\bar{x}_{i}\right).\label{eq:YM-Celestial-Amplitude}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This expression for the celestial amplitude 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\mathcal{A}}_{n}$
\end_inset

 is key in establishing that our holographic dictionary can reproduce the tree-level MHV scattering amplitude for gluons in an appropriate limit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Celestial Vertex Operators for Gluons
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Celestial-Vertex-Operators"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now turn our attention to the vertex operators within celestial CFT whose correlation functions generate the celestial amplitude 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\mathcal{A}}_{n}$
\end_inset

 as derived in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:YM-Celestial-Amplitude"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 To achieve this,
 it is necessary to introduce a ``dressing'' of the worldsheet conformal primaries 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j}\left(x;z\right)$
\end_inset

 in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model by the level-one Kac-Moody currents 
\begin_inset Formula $K^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $SO\left(2N\right)$
\end_inset

,
 residing on the boundary of 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

,
 which we identify with the celestial sphere.
 Hereafter,
 we shall refer to 
\begin_inset Formula $K^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 as the 
\emph on
celestial Kac-Moody currents
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The generators 
\begin_inset Formula $K^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 of the celestial Kac-Moody current algebra are characterised by the OPE:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
K^{a}\left(x_{1}\right)K^{b}\left(x_{2}\right)\sim\frac{\delta^{ab}}{x^{2}_{12}}+\frac{if^{abc}}{x_{12}}K^{c}\left(x_{2}\right),\label{eq:OPE-3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $f^{abc}$
\end_inset

 denote the structure constants of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{so}\left(2N\right)\simeq D_{N}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recalling that the dual Coxeter number of the Lie algebra 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{N}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $2N-2$
\end_inset

,
 we find that the KZ equations for the currents 
\begin_inset Formula $K^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 take the form:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{i}}+\frac{1}{2N-1}\sum_{j\neq i}\frac{T^{c}_{i}T^{c}_{j}}{x_{i}-x_{j}}\right)\left\langle K^{a_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)...K^{a_{n}}\left(x_{n}\right)\right\rangle =0,\label{eq:KZ-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $T^{a}$
\end_inset

 are the generators of the fundamental representation of 
\begin_inset Formula $SO\left(2N\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Decomposing the correlation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle K^{a_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)...K^{a_{n}}\left(x_{n}\right)\right\rangle $
\end_inset

 into the canonical 
\begin_inset Formula $SO\left(2N\right)$
\end_inset

 invariants,
 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{1}=\delta_{m_{1},m_{2}}\delta_{m_{3},m_{4}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $I_{2}=\delta_{m_{1},m_{3}}\delta_{m_{2},m_{4}}$
\end_inset

,
 and retaining only the terms 
\begin_inset Formula $T^{a}_{i}T^{a}_{j}I_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $T^{a}_{i}T^{a}_{j}I_{2}$
\end_inset

 proportional to 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

,
 we take the limit 
\begin_inset Formula $N\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 of the KZ equations.
 The asymptotic solution is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\left\langle \mathcal{O}^{a_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)...\mathcal{O}^{a_{n}}\left(x_{n}\right)\right\rangle =\text{tr}\left(T^{a_{1}}...T^{a_{n}}\right)\frac{1}{x_{12}x_{23}...x_{n1}}.\label{eq:Parke-Taylor-Correlation-Function}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 in the large-
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 limit,
 the correlation functions of the celestial Kac-Moody currents 
\begin_inset Formula $K^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 generate the colour-ordered Parke-Taylor factor (cf.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "parke1986amplitude"
literal "false"

\end_inset

),
 which is associated with the tree-level MHV scattering amplitudes of gluons.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We then define the level-one 
\begin_inset Formula $SO\left(2N\right)$
\end_inset

 vertex operators as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
V^{a}_{\rho}\left(x\right)\coloneqq\Gamma\left(\rho\right)K^{a}\left(x\right)\int d^{2}z\;\Phi^{\rho}\left(x;z\right),\label{eq:-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where,
 from the perspective of celestial CFT,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 is understood as a label associated with the vertex operators 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{a}_{\rho}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 rather than a scaling dimension.
 Consequently,
 the correlation function of the vertex operators in the large-
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 limit yields:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\left\langle V^{a_{1}}_{\rho_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)...V^{a_{n}}_{\rho_{n}}\left(x_{n}\right)\right\rangle =\frac{\text{tr}\left(T^{a_{1}}...T^{a_{n}}\right)}{x_{12}x_{23}...x_{n1}}\frac{1}{W}\int d^{2}z_{1}...d^{2}z_{n}\left\langle \left\langle \Phi^{\rho_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)...\Phi^{\rho_{n}}\left(x_{n};z_{n}\right)\right\rangle \right\rangle ,
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 is a renormalisation constant associated with the worldsheet ``area,'' required to render the worldsheet integrals well-defined and finite.
 Finally,
 in the mini-superspace limit (see Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Mini-Superspace-Limit"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)),
 wherein:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}\left\langle \left\langle \Phi^{\rho_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)...\Phi^{\rho_{n}}\left(x_{n};z_{n}\right)\right\rangle \right\rangle =\mathcal{N}\int\frac{d\rho d\gamma d\bar{\gamma}}{\rho^{3}}\prod^{n}_{i=1}\left(\frac{\rho}{\rho^{2}+\left|x_{i}-\gamma\right|^{2}}\right)^{2\rho_{i}},\label{eq:Step-B}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

we arrive at:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}\left\langle V^{a_{1}}_{\rho_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)...V^{a_{n}}_{\rho_{n}}\left(x_{n}\right)\right\rangle =\mathcal{N}\frac{\text{tr}\left(T^{a_{1}}...T^{a_{n}}\right)}{x_{12}x_{23}...x_{n1}}\int_{AdS_{3}}d^{3}\hat{y}\prod^{n}_{i=1}\frac{\Gamma\left(2\rho_{i}\right)}{\left(\varepsilon-iq\left(x_{i},\bar{x}_{i}\right)\cdot\hat{y}\right)^{2\rho_{i}}}.\label{eq:Step-C}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The right-hand side of the preceding equation may be identified with a scalar 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}$
\end_inset

 Feynman-Witten contact diagram,
 multiplied by the Chan-Paton factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\text{tr}(T^{a_{1}}...T^{a_{n}})$
\end_inset

,
 and the cyclic Parke-Taylor denominator.
 Nonetheless,
 in order to reproduce the full celestial leaf amplitude for gluons arranged in an MHV configuration,
 whose first two gluons have negative helicity and whose remaining gluons have positive helicity,
 it remains necessary to account for the helicity numerator 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{4}_{12}$
\end_inset

.
 The strategy that we now describe for reproducing this numerator is based on an observation due to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "nair2005note"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 namely a Berezin integral identity that we shall now recall.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\theta^{\alpha}_{A}\}^{4}_{\alpha=1}$
\end_inset

 be a family of Grassmann-valued undotted van der Waerden spinors,
 normalised so that their Berezin integrals satisfy
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int\mathrm{d}^{2}\theta^{\alpha}\;\theta^{\alpha}_{A}\theta^{\alpha}_{B}=\varepsilon_{AB}\,,\label{eq:}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=1,2,3,4$
\end_inset

.
 In what follows,
 we denote the contraction of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta^{\alpha}_{A}$
\end_inset

 with an undotted spinor 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu^{A}$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu\cdot\theta^{\alpha}=\nu^{A}\theta^{\alpha}_{A}$
\end_inset

.
 Let us further define a spinor 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu^{A}(x)=(x,1)$
\end_inset

,
 depending on the holomorphic coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 on the celestial sphere,
 and a family of Grassmann-valued variables
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\eta^{\alpha}(x)\coloneqq\nu(x)\cdot\theta^{\alpha}\,.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Therefore,
 Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:"

\end_inset

) implies the Berezin integral identity
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int\mathrm{d}^{8}\theta\;\prod^{4}_{\alpha=1}\eta^{\alpha}(x_{r})\,\prod^{4}_{\beta=1}\eta^{\beta}(x_{s})=x^{4}_{rs}\,.\label{eq:-2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This formula naturally suggests that,
 to obtain the helicity numerator,
 we may introduce the negative-helicity celestial gluon operators 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{O}^{a-}_{\rho}(x)$
\end_inset

 by dressing the stringy vertex operators 
\begin_inset Formula $V^{a}_{\rho}(x)$
\end_inset

,
 defined in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:-1"

\end_inset

),
 with the Grassmann-valued factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\prod^{4}_{\alpha=1}\eta^{\alpha}(x)$
\end_inset

.
 The positive-helicity celestial gluon operators 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{O}^{a+}_{\rho}(x)$
\end_inset

 are given simply by the original vertex operators.
 Thus,
 we set
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{O}^{a+}_{\rho}(x)\coloneqq V^{a}_{\rho}(x),\qquad\boldsymbol{O}^{a-}_{\rho}(x)\coloneqq V^{a}_{\rho}(x)\,\prod^{4}_{\alpha=1}\eta^{\alpha}(x)\,.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us observe that the anti-commuting quantities 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{\alpha}(x)$
\end_inset

,
 and the Grassmann-valued spinors 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta^{\alpha}_{A}$
\end_inset

,
 are nothing more than 
\emph on
auxiliary helicity bookkeeping variables
\emph default
.
 We emphasise that 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta^{\alpha}(x)$
\end_inset

 should not be interpreted as dynamical physical degrees of freedom,
 or as components of a superfield propagating on the worldsheet.
 Analogously,
 the anti-commuting spinors 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta^{\alpha}_{A}$
\end_inset

 should not be identified with the fermionic directions of a target supermanifold.
 In the present manuscript,
 we are working entirely within the framework of 
\emph on
bosonic
\emph default
 Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}$
\end_inset

 string theory.
 Therefore,
 the bookkeeping devices that we have just introduced may be physically interpreted as polarisation-like variables whose only purpose is to project the desired helicity information of the external states.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Keeping this in mind,
 and following Nair's suggestion,
 we identify the Berezin integration as the
\emph on
 helicity projector
\emph default
,
 defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Pi^{\text{YM}}_{\text{Nair}}[\Psi]\coloneqq\int\mathrm{d}^{8}\theta\;\Psi(\theta)\,,
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi$
\end_inset

 is any function of the Grassmann spinors 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta^{\alpha}_{A}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore,
 from Eqs.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:YM-Celestial-Amplitude"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-C"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:-2"

\end_inset

),
 the tree-level MHV celestial amplitude for gluon scattering in pure Yang-Mills theory,
 associated with the helicity configuration 
\begin_inset Formula $1^{-},2^{-},3^{+},\dots,n^{+}$
\end_inset

,
 may be expressed as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\widehat{\mathcal{A}}_{n} & =ig^{n-2}_{\text{YM}}\frac{\delta\left(4-2\sum_{i}\rho_{i}\right)}{\left(2\pi\right)^{3}\mathcal{N}}\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}\Pi^{\mathrm{YM}}_{\mathrm{Nair}}\left\langle \boldsymbol{O}^{-a_{1}}_{\Delta_{1}+\alpha_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)\boldsymbol{O}^{-a_{2}}_{\Delta_{2}+\alpha_{2}}\left(x_{2}\right)\prod^{n}_{k=3}\boldsymbol{O}^{+a_{k}}_{\Delta_{k}+\alpha_{k}}\left(x_{k}\right)\right\rangle \label{eq:-4}\\
 & \;+\left(\bar{x}_{i}\leftrightarrow-\bar{x}_{i}\right)\,.
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Remarks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us now observe that,
 in the preceding expression (a key entry in our dictionary,
 relating the tree-level MHV celestial amplitudes for gluons in gauge theory to the helicity projection of the leading-trace,
 large-level limit of appropriately dressed celestial vertex operators),
 the Yang-Mills coupling constant 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{\text{YM}}$
\end_inset

 enters only through the proportionality factor.
 Therefore,
 while Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:-4"

\end_inset

) shows that the kinematical structure and conformal dependence of the gluonic celestial amplitudes can be reproduced from data pertaining to the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model,
 at the present stage of our proposed holographic dictionary,
 the coupling parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{\text{YM}}$
\end_inset

 has a 
\emph on
phenomenological
\emph default
 character.
 We may,
 for example,
 absorb 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{\text{YM}}$
\end_inset

 into the normalisation of the positive-helicity celestial gluon operators 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{O}^{a+}_{\rho}(x)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Deriving 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{\text{YM}}$
\end_inset

 from the physical constants characterising the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model,
 such as the fundamental string length 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell_{0}$
\end_inset

 or the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}$
\end_inset

 radius 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell$
\end_inset

,
 however,
 lies beyond the scope of the present bottom-up approach to perturbative celestial CFT.
 It would certainly be very satisfactory and mathematically elegant to obtain the gauge-theoretic coupling constant 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{\text{YM}}$
\end_inset

 in the bulk Klein space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}^{2}_{2}$
\end_inset

 from critical string theory on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}\times X$
\end_inset

.
 We expect that such a derivation,
 if possible,
 would require detailed knowledge of the spacetime CFT associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

,
 and it remains a long-standing goal of our research programme.
