\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Einstein's Gravity
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Einstein's-Gravity"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this subsection,
 we shall explain how the preceding argument extends to gravity.
 The formulation by which tree-level celestial amplitudes for MHV gravitons can be represented as a correlation function of 
\emph on
Liouville
\emph default
 vertex operators,
 ``dressed'' by an 
\begin_inset Formula $SO\left(2N\right)$
\end_inset

 level-one Kac-Moody current algebra on the celestial sphere,
 has been thoroughly analysed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "mol2024holographic"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This construction is framed within the large-
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 semiclassical limit of Liouville field theory,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $b\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

.
 Our objective here is to demonstrate how this formalism may be adapted to the holographic dictionary introduced in Subsection 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:The-Holographic-Dictionary"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Mathematical Preliminaries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We must first undertake a brief preparatory discussion to introduce the mathematical objects needed for our construction.
 For further elaboration,
 the interested reader is referred to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "mol2024holographic"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 It is important to note that we shall employ a slightly different notation in this discussion:
 the variables 
\begin_inset Formula $z,\bar{z}$
\end_inset

 denote coordinates on the string worldsheet,
 while 
\begin_inset Formula $x,\bar{x}$
\end_inset

 correspond to coordinates on the celestial sphere.
 To begin,
 let us recall from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pasterski2017conformal"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 that the scalar celestial wavefunction with conformal weight 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta=2\sigma$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\phi_{2\sigma}\left(x;z\right)\coloneqq\frac{\Gamma\left(2\sigma\right)}{\left(\varepsilon-iq\left(x_{i},\bar{x}_{i}\right)\cdot x\right)^{2\sigma}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{P}_{i}$
\end_inset

 denote the operator acting on the space of celestial wavefunctions 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\phi_{2\sigma}\}$
\end_inset

 by shifting the conformal weights (with respect to the celestial CFT,
 and not to be confused with the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model) by one-half,
 such that:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{P}_{i}\phi_{2\sigma_{j}}\left(x_{i};z_{i}\right)\coloneqq\delta_{ij}\phi_{2\left(\sigma_{j}+1/2\right)}\left(x_{i};z_{i}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We must also introduce a pair of chiral quasi-primary free fermions,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi^{\dagger}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

,
 defined on the celestial sphere by their respective mode expansions:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\chi\left(x\right)\coloneqq\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}b_{n}x^{n},\,\,\,\chi^{\dagger}\left(x\right)\coloneqq\sum^{\infty}_{n=0}b^{\dagger}_{n}x^{-n-1},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{n}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b^{\dagger}_{n}$
\end_inset

 are fermionic annihilation and creation operators,
 satisfying the anti-commutation relations 
\begin_inset Formula $\{b_{m},b^{\dagger}_{n}\}=\delta_{mn}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $m,n\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 These operators act on the vacuum state 
\begin_inset Formula $\big|0\rangle$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{n}\big|0\rangle=0$
\end_inset

 for each non-negative integer 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

.
 The two-point function for this doublet is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\langle\chi\left(x_{1}\right)\chi^{\dagger}\left(x_{2}\right)\rangle\coloneqq\frac{1}{x_{1}-x_{2}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Finally,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu^{A}_{i}\coloneqq\left(x_{i},1\right)^{T}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{\nu}^{\dot{A}}_{i}\coloneqq\left(\bar{x}_{i},1\right)^{T}$
\end_inset

 be a pair of two-component spinors parametrising the four-momentum of the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

-th graviton,
 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda^{A}\coloneqq\left(\lambda,1\right)^{T}\in\mathbf{C}^{2}$
\end_inset

 be an auxiliary two-component spinor associated with the state 
\begin_inset Formula $\big|\lambda\rangle\coloneqq\chi^{\dagger}\left(\lambda\right)\big|0\rangle$
\end_inset

.
 We have shown in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "mol2024holographic"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 that,
 by defining the operators:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{A}_{i}\coloneqq\exp\left(q\left(x,\bar{x}\right)\cdot y\,\mathsf{P}_{i}\right)\chi^{\dagger}\left(x\right)\chi\left(x\right),\mathsf{B}_{i}\coloneqq\frac{\bar{\nu}^{\dot{A}}_{i}\lambda^{A}}{\left\langle \nu_{i},\lambda\right\rangle }\frac{\partial}{\partial y^{A\dot{A}}}\exp\left(q\left(x,\bar{x}\right)\cdot y\,\mathsf{P}_{i}\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the four-vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(y^{\mu}\right)\in\mathbf{R}^{4}$
\end_inset

 should be interpreted as an index rather than spacetime coordinates,
 the tree-level MHV celestial amplitude 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{\mathcal{M}}_{n}$
\end_inset

 for the scattering of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-gravitons can be expressed as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\widehat{\mathcal{M}}_{n} & =\left(\frac{\kappa}{2}\right)^{n-2}\frac{x^{8}_{12}}{x_{12}x_{23}...x_{n1}}\int\frac{d^{2}\lambda}{\pi}\int dy\delta\left(y\right)\langle\lambda\big|\mathsf{A}_{1}\left(\prod^{n-2}_{i=2}\mathsf{B}_{i}\right)\mathsf{A}_{n-1}\mathsf{A}_{n}\bar{\partial}\big|\lambda\rangle\\
 & \int\frac{d^{4}Y}{\left(2\pi\right)^{4}}\prod^{n}_{j=1}\phi_{2\sigma_{j}}\left(x_{j};Y\right)+\mathcal{P}\left(2,...,n-2\right),\label{eq:Celestial-1}
\end{align}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}\left(2,...,n-2\right)$
\end_inset

 denotes the permutation of the symbols within the set 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ 2,...,n-2\right\} $
\end_inset

,
 and we define the celestial conformal weights as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{i}\coloneqq\frac{1}{2}\left(\Delta_{i}+\alpha_{i}-1\right)\,\,\,\text{for \ensuremath{i\in\left\{ 1,n-1,n\right\} },}\label{eq:Sigma}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{i}\coloneqq\frac{1}{2}\left(\Delta_{i}+\alpha_{i}\right)\,\,\,\text{for \ensuremath{2\leq i\leq n-2}}.\label{eq:Sigma-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Furthermore,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\kappa^{2}=32\pi G_{N}\,,
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{N}$
\end_inset

 is the four-dimensional Newton's constant on the bulk Klein space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Celestial Vertex Operators for Gravitons
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We are now prepared to demonstrate that Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Celestial-1"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) can be reformulated using the celestial vertex operators constructed from 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 string theory,
 as detailed in Subsection 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:First-Entry:-Kac-Moody"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 We first define the ``dressed'' vertex operators in celestial CFT for gravitons:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
G_{i}\left(x_{i}\right)\coloneqq\Gamma\left(2\sigma_{i}\right)T^{a_{i}}K^{a_{i}}\left(x_{i}\right)\mathsf{A}_{i}\int d^{2}z_{i}\Phi^{2\sigma_{i}}\left(x_{i};z_{i}\right),\label{eq:Definition-2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,n-1,n$
\end_inset

,
 and:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
H_{j}\left(x_{j}\right)\coloneqq\Gamma\left(2\sigma_{i}\right)T^{a_{j}}K^{a_{j}}\left(x_{j}\right)\mathsf{B}_{j}\int d^{2}z_{j}\Phi^{2\sigma_{j}}\left(x_{j};z_{j}\right),\label{eq:Definition-3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for 
\begin_inset Formula $j=2,...,n-2$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $K^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 denotes the celestial Kac-Moody currents introduced in Subsection 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Celestial-Vertex-Operators"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 and obeying the OPE (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:OPE-3"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 We emphasise that the order of the operators 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{A}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{B}_{j}$
\end_inset

 in Eqs.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Definition-2"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Definition-3"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) is important,
 as the worldsheet conformal primaries 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{2\sigma_{i}}\left(x_{i};z_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 inherit the action of the operator 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{P}_{i}$
\end_inset

 due to their dependence on the celestial conformal weights,
 as given by Eqs.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Sigma"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Sigma-1"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As in the preceding subsection,
 it is also necessary to introduce auxiliary anti-commuting variables to generate the helicity numerator 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{8}_{12}$
\end_inset

 required by the BGK formula.
 In the gravitational case,
 we shall employ a collection 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\xi^{\hat{\alpha}}_{A}\}^{8}_{\hat{\alpha}=1}$
\end_inset

 of Grassmann-valued van der Waerden spinors normalised by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int\mathrm{d}^{2}\xi^{\hat{\alpha}}\;\xi^{\hat{\alpha}}_{A}\xi^{\hat{\alpha}}_{B}=\varepsilon_{AB}\,,\label{eq:-3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the indices 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\alpha}=1,\dots,8$
\end_inset

 in the gravitational case are adorned with a hat in order to distinguish them unambiguously from their gluonic counterparts.
 From these variables we define the auxiliary anti-commuting helicity variables
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\psi^{\hat{\alpha}}(x)\coloneqq\nu(x)\cdot\xi^{\hat{\alpha}}\,,
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu^{A}(x)=(x,1)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is the holomorphic coordinate on the celestial sphere,
 precisely as in the preceding subsection.
 Let us emphasise that 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi^{\hat{\alpha}}(x)$
\end_inset

 should not be regarded as a fermionic physical degree of freedom propagating on the worldsheet,
 nor should the underlying 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi^{\hat{\alpha}}_{A}$
\end_inset

 be identified with the anti-commuting coordinates parameterising the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

soul
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of a target superspace.
 These variables play an entirely 
\emph on
auxiliary role
\emph default
.
 Their only purpose is to project the helicity component of the external graviton states.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Accordingly,
 Nair's observation for gravitons follows from Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:-3"

\end_inset

) and reads
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int\mathrm{d}^{16}\xi\;\prod^{8}_{\alpha=1}\psi^{\alpha}(x_{r})\,\prod^{8}_{\beta=1}\psi^{\beta}(x_{s})=x^{8}_{rs}\,.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The helicity projector for gravitons is then defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Pi^{\mathrm{GR}}_{\text{Nair}}[\Psi]\coloneqq\int\mathrm{d}^{16}\xi\;\Psi(\xi)\,,
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for any function 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi=\Psi(\xi)$
\end_inset

.
 Now,
 as in the gluonic construction,
 the celestial graviton operators must be defined by dressing the vertex operators with the Grassmann-valued factors 
\begin_inset Formula $\prod^{8}_{\hat{\alpha}=1}\psi^{\hat{\alpha}}(x)$
\end_inset

.
 On the other hand,
 in the gravitational case,
 there are two kinds of vertex operators,
 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{j}$
\end_inset

,
 defined respectively in Eqs.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Definition-2"

\end_inset

) and (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Definition-3"

\end_inset

).
 Therefore,
 gravitons with positive helicity are associated with the operators
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{G}^{+}_{i}(x_{i})\coloneqq G_{i}(x_{i}),\qquad\boldsymbol{H}^{+}_{j}(x_{j})\coloneqq H_{j}(x_{j})\,,
\end{equation}

\end_inset

whereas gravitons with negative helicity are associated with
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{G}^{-}_{i}(x_{i})\coloneqq G_{i}(x_{i})\,\prod^{8}_{\alpha=1}\psi^{\alpha}(x_{i}),\qquad\boldsymbol{H}^{-}_{j}(x_{j})\coloneqq H_{j}(x_{j})\,\prod^{8}_{\alpha=1}\psi^{\alpha}(x_{j})\,.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 by employing reasoning analogous to that leading to Eqs.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-B"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-C"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) in the previous section,
 we obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\widehat{\mathcal{M}}_{n} & =\frac{1}{\left(2\pi\right)^{3}\mathcal{N}\gamma_{n}}\left(\frac{\kappa}{2}\right)^{n-2}\lim_{N\rightarrow\infty}\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}\Pi^{\text{GR}}_{\text{Nair}}\int\frac{d^{2}\lambda}{\pi}\int dy\delta\left(y\right)\label{eq:-5}\\
 & \quad\times\langle\lambda\big|\boldsymbol{G}^{-}_{1}\boldsymbol{H}^{-}_{2}\left(\prod^{n-2}_{i=3}\boldsymbol{H}^{+}_{i}\right)\boldsymbol{G}^{+}_{n-1}\boldsymbol{G}^{+}_{n}\bar{\partial}\big|\lambda\rangle+\left(\bar{x}_{i}\leftrightarrow-\bar{x}_{i}\right)+\mathcal{P}\left(2,...,n-2\right),
\end{align}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{n}\coloneqq\text{tr}\left(T^{a_{1}}...T^{a_{n}}\right)^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In conclusion,
 we have demonstrated that the tree-level celestial amplitude for MHV graviton scattering in Einstein gravity,
 in the helicity configuration 
\begin_inset Formula $1^{-},2^{-},3^{+},\dots,n^{+}$
\end_inset

,
 can be obtained as the large-
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

,
 large-level (
\begin_inset Formula $k\to\infty$
\end_inset

) limit of the correlation function of the vertex operators constructed from the Kac-Moody currents and conformal primaries of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model,
 appropriately dressed by auxiliary Grassmann-valued helicity variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Remark.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us note,
 as at the end of Subsection 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Celestial-Vertex-Operators"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 that the four-dimensional gravitational coupling constant 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

 in the bulk Klein space enters Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:-5"

\end_inset

) only through the proportionality factor.
 We may absorb the factor 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa/2$
\end_inset

 into the normalisation of the positive-helicity celestial graviton operators.
 However,
 a top-down derivation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

 in terms of the physical parameters describing the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model,
 such as the radius of three-dimensional anti-de Sitter space or the fundamental string length,
 remains a long-standing goal of our programme.
 Accordingly,
 at present,
 our candidate model for perturbative celestial CFT,
 which approximates the tree-level MHV sector of Einstein gravity,
 reproduces the kinematical structure and conformal dependence of the BGK formula when expressed in the celestial conformal-primary basis,
 but treats the gravitational coupling parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

 as a phenomenological ingredient.
\end_layout

