\begin_layout Subsection
Two-point function
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Two-point-function"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this subsection,
 we derive the celestial 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

-point function by employing the exact form of the corresponding correlation function in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model,
 studied in detail by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "teschner1997conformal"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 For readers seeking a comprehensive exposition of the mathematical subtleties inherent in the model,
 we direct them to Teschner's subsequent works (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "teschner1999mini,teschner1999structure,teschner2000operator"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our aim is to compute the correlation function of two scalar conformal primaries,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\Delta_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\Delta_{2}}\left(x_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 within the celestial CFT,
 characterised by conformal weights 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 respectively.
 According to our proposed holographic dictionary between Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 bosonic string theory and celestial CFT,
 these scalar wavefunctions correspond to the worldsheet conformal primaries 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j_{2}}\left(x_{2};z_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 In this correspondence,
 the spins in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model are related to the conformal weights in the celestial CFT by 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{1}=-\Delta_{1}/2$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{2}=-\Delta_{2}/2$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
According to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "teschner1999mini,teschner1997conformal"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

-point function of these conformal primaries is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \left\langle \Phi^{j_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)\Phi^{j_{2}}\left(x_{2};z_{2}\right)\right\rangle =\\
 & \frac{1}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{2\beta}}\left(\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)\delta\left(j_{1}+j_{2}+1\right)+\frac{B\left(j_{1}\right)}{\left|x_{12}\right|^{-2\left(j_{1}+j_{2}\right)}}\delta\left(j_{2}-j_{1}\right)\right),\label{eq:2-Point-Function}
\end{align}

\end_inset

where the exponents and proportionality constants are defined as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\beta\coloneqq\frac{b^{2}}{2}\left(2-\Delta_{1}\right)\left(2-\Delta_{2}\right),\,\,\,b^{2}=\frac{1}{k-2},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left(j_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

 denotes a normalisation constant that depends on the choice of normalisation of the ``plane-wave'' basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j}\left(x;z\right)$
\end_inset

.
 For the conventions adopted by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "giveon1998comments"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 which we adhere to in these notes,
 
\begin_inset Formula $B\equiv1$
\end_inset

.
 Nonetheless,
 to preserve generality and maintain consistency with 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "teschner1997conformal"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we retain the explicit dependence on the constant 
\begin_inset Formula $B\left(j_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

 throughout this subsection.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Applying our holographic dictionary,
 which maps correlation functions in celestial CFT to those in 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 string theory,
 we obtain:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \left\langle \phi_{\Delta_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)\phi_{\Delta_{2}}\left(x_{2}\right)\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}\\
 & =\frac{1}{N}\int d^{2}z_{1}\int d^{2}z_{2}\left\langle \Phi^{j_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)\Phi^{j_{2}}\left(x_{2};z_{2}\right)\right\rangle \\
 & =\left(\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)\delta\left(j_{1}+j_{2}+1\right)+\frac{B\left(j_{1}\right)}{\left|x_{12}\right|^{-2\left(j_{1}+j_{2}\right)}}\delta\left(j_{2}-j_{1}\right)\right)\frac{1}{N}\int d^{2}z_{1}\int d^{2}z_{2}\frac{1}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{2\beta}}\label{eq:Step-9}
\end{align}

\end_inset

Here,
 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 denotes the worldsheet ``area.'' The factor 
\begin_inset Formula $1/N$
\end_inset

 serves as a renormalisation of the worldsheet integration measure,
 ensuring that the correlation function remains well-defined and finite.
 This renormalisation is a standard procedure in string perturbation theory (cf.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "d1988geometry"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As demonstrated in the Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Generalised-Dirac-Delta"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Integral-4"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 the worldsheet integral evaluates to:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{N}\int d^{2}z_{1}\int d^{2}z_{2}\frac{1}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{2\beta}}=\frac{\pi^{2}}{2}\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(\beta-1\right),\label{eq:Identity-4}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where,
 following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "donnay2020asymptotic"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(i\left(\Delta-z\right)\right)$
\end_inset

 represents the generalised Dirac delta ``function,'' analytically continued to the complex plane via:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(i\left(\Delta-z\right)\right)\coloneqq\frac{1}{2\pi}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\tau\,\tau^{\Delta-z-1},\label{eq:Dirac-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

such that the following identity holds:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\varphi\left(\Delta\right)=-i\int_{\mathcal{C}}dz\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(i\left(\Delta-z\right)\right)\varphi\left(z\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for the contour 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{C}\coloneqq-1/2+i\mathbf{R}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Substituting this result into Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-9"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) and expressing the final result in terms of the conformal weights,
 we arrive at:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \left\langle \phi_{\Delta_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)\phi_{\Delta_{2}}\left(x_{2}\right)\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}=\\
 & \pi^{2}\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(\beta-1\right)\left(\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)\delta\left(2-\Delta_{1}-\Delta_{2}\right)+\frac{B\left(-\Delta_{1}/2\right)}{\left|x_{12}\right|^{\Delta_{1}+\Delta_{2}}}\delta\left(\Delta_{1}-\Delta_{2}\right)\right)\label{eq:Celestial-Two-Point-Function}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

-point function in our celestial CFT for the scalar conformal primaries 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\Delta_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\Delta_{2}}\left(x_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 takes the form of a linear combination involving a delta function localised at 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}=x_{2}$
\end_inset

 and a conventional 
\begin_inset Formula $1/\left|x_{12}\right|^{\Delta_{1}+\Delta_{2}}$
\end_inset

 power-law dependence on the conformal weights.
 This result stands in contrast to those reported by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "furugori2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 One might,
 as suggested by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ogawa2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 explore the possibility of constructing linear combinations of these conformal primaries to generate a 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

-point function that solely includes the delta function 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 the power-law term 
\begin_inset Formula $1/\left|x_{12}\right|^{\Delta_{1}+\Delta_{2}}$
\end_inset

,
 or their shadow transforms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 we prefer to follow an approach that avoids the imposition of intuition derived from conventional CFTs when examining celestial CFTs.
 Instead,
 we maintain that the mathematical structures we have uncovered merit serious consideration on their own terms,
 and their consequences should be rigorously examined.
 The ability of the mini-superspace limit of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model to holographically reconstruct the tree-level MHV scattering amplitudes for gluons and gravitons is a compelling reason for adhering to the mathematical framework of this model in deriving the properties of celestial CFT.
 Thus,
 in our view,
 one must be prepared to encounter correlation functions–and,
 as will be demonstrated in the subsequent sections,
 structure constants of celestial OPEs–manifesting in a non-conventional distributional form.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is also instructive to compute the shadow transform of the 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

-point function given by Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:2-Point-Function"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \int d^{2}y_{1}\frac{1}{\left|x_{1}-y_{1}\right|^{4\left(1+j_{1}\right)}}\int d^{2}y_{2}\frac{1}{\left|x_{2}-y_{2}\right|^{4\left(1+j_{2}\right)}}\left\langle \Phi^{j_{1}}\left(y_{1};z_{1}\right)\Phi^{j_{2}}\left(y_{2};z_{2}\right)\right\rangle \\
 & =\frac{1}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{2\beta}}\delta\left(j_{1}+j_{2}+1\right)\int d^{2}y_{1}d^{2}y_{2}\frac{\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(y_{1}-y_{2}\right)}{\left|x_{1}-y_{1}\right|^{4\left(1+j_{1}\right)}\left|x_{2}-y_{2}\right|^{4\left(1+j_{2}\right)}}\\
 & +\frac{1}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{2\beta}}B\left(j_{1}\right)\delta\left(j_{1}-j_{2}\right)\int d^{2}y_{1}d^{2}y_{2}\frac{\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(y_{1}-y_{2}\right)}{\left|x_{1}-y_{1}\right|^{4\left(1+j_{1}\right)}\left|x_{2}-y_{2}\right|^{4\left(1+j_{2}\right)}\left|x_{12}\right|^{-2\left(j_{1}+j_{2}\right)}}.\label{eq:Step-10}
\end{align}

\end_inset

In the Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Worldsheet-Integrals-Related"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 we gave a detailed analysis of the following integral over the complex plane:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{0}\left(\tau_{1},\tau_{2}\big|x_{1},x_{2}\right)\coloneqq\int d^{2}y\frac{1}{\left|x_{1}-y\right|^{2\tau_{1}}\left|y-x_{2}\right|^{2\tau_{2}}}.\label{eq:Integral-3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We found in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Integral-2"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) that this integral evaluates,
 in the distributional sense,
 to:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{0}=\frac{\pi}{\left|x_{1}-x_{2}\right|^{2\left(\tau_{1}+\tau_{2}-1\right)}}\frac{\Gamma\left(1-\tau_{1}\right)}{\Gamma\left(\tau_{1}\right)}\frac{\Gamma\left(1-\tau_{2}\right)}{\Gamma\left(\tau_{2}\right)}\frac{\Gamma\left(\tau_{1}+\tau_{2}-1\right)}{\Gamma\left(2-\tau_{1}-\tau_{2}\right)}.\label{eq:Identity-2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

From Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Identity-2"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 we demonstrated in Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Worldsheet-Integrals-Related"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 (cf.
 Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Integral-5"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)) that,
 by taking the limit 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{1}+\tau_{2}\rightarrow2$
\end_inset

,
 one recovers the well-known identity from 
\begin_inset Formula $2d$
\end_inset

 CFT (cf.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "simmons2014projectors"
literal "false"

\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int d^{2}y\frac{1}{\left|x_{1}-y\right|^{2\tau}\left|y-x_{2}\right|^{2\left(2-\tau\right)}}=\frac{4\pi^{2}}{\nu^{2}}\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right),\label{eq:Identity-3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau\eqqcolon1+i\nu$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consequently,
 using Eqs.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Identity-2"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Identity-3"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 we arrive at the following results:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{j_{1}+j_{2}+1\rightarrow0}\int d^{2}y_{1}d^{2}y_{2}\frac{\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(y_{1}-y_{2}\right)}{\left|x_{1}-y_{1}\right|^{4\left(1+j_{1}\right)}\left|x_{2}-y_{2}\right|^{4\left(1+j_{2}\right)}}=-\frac{4\pi^{2}}{\left(1+2j_{1}\right)^{2}}\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and,
 similarly,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \lim_{j_{1}-j_{2}\rightarrow0}\int d^{2}y_{1}d^{2}y_{2}\frac{\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(y_{1}-y_{2}\right)}{\left|x_{1}-y_{1}\right|^{4\left(1+j_{1}\right)}\left|x_{2}-y_{2}\right|^{4\left(1+j_{2}\right)}\left|x_{1}-x_{2}\right|^{-2\left(j_{1}+j_{2}\right)}}\\
 & =-\frac{\pi^{2}}{\left(1+2j_{1}\right)^{2}}\frac{1}{\left|x_{1}-x_{2}\right|^{2\left(2+j_{1}+j_{2}\right)}}.
\end{align}

\end_inset

Thus,
 Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-10"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) can be rewritten as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \frac{1}{2\pi}\int d^{2}y_{1}\frac{1}{\left|x_{1}-y_{1}\right|^{4\left(1+j_{1}\right)}}\frac{1}{2\pi}\int d^{2}y_{2}\frac{1}{\left|x_{2}-y_{2}\right|^{4\left(1+j_{2}\right)}}\left\langle \Phi^{j_{1}}\left(y_{1};z_{1}\right)\Phi^{j_{2}}\left(y_{2};z_{2}\right)\right\rangle \\
 & =-\frac{1}{\left(1+2j_{1}\right)^{2}}\frac{1}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{2\beta}}\left(\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)\delta\left(j_{1}+j_{2}+1\right)+\frac{B\left(j_{1}\right)}{4}\frac{1}{\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\left(2+j_{1}+j_{2}\right)}}\delta\text{\left(j_{1}-j_{2}\right)}\right).\label{eq:Step-11}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is then natural to define the shadow transform of the worldsheet conformal primary 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j}\left(x;z\right)$
\end_inset

 as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{\Phi}^{j}\left(x;z\right)\coloneqq\frac{1}{2\pi}\int d^{2}y\frac{1}{\left|x-y\right|^{4\left(1+j\right)}}\Phi^{j}\left(y;z\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so that Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-11"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) can be reorganised as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \left\langle \widetilde{\Phi}^{j_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)\widetilde{\Phi}^{j_{2}}\left(x_{2};z_{2}\right)\right\rangle \\
 & =-\frac{1}{\left(1+2j_{1}\right)^{2}}\frac{1}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{2\beta}}\left(\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)\delta\left(j_{1}+j_{2}+1\right)+\frac{B\left(j_{1}\right)}{4}\frac{1}{\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\left(2+j_{1}+j_{2}\right)}}\delta\text{\left(j_{1}-j_{2}\right)}\right).\label{eq:Step-12}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We conclude by extending the holographic dictionary,
 postulating that the shadow-transformed worldsheet conformal primaries 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde{\Phi}^{j}\left(x;z\right)$
\end_inset

,
 characterised by spin 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

,
 correspond to the celestial scalar conformal primaries 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde{\phi}_{\Delta}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

,
 with conformal weight 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta=-2j$
\end_inset

.
 Consequently,
 integrating Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-12"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) over the worldsheet coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1},z_{2}$
\end_inset

 yields:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \left\langle \widetilde{\phi}_{\Delta_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)\widetilde{\phi}_{\Delta_{2}}\left(x_{2}\right)\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}\\
 & =\frac{1}{N}\int d^{2}z_{1}\int d^{2}z_{2}\left\langle \widetilde{\Phi}^{j_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)\widetilde{\Phi}^{j_{2}}\left(x_{2};z_{2}\right)\right\rangle \\
 & =\frac{\pi^{2}}{\lambda^{2}}\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(\beta-1\right)\left(\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)\delta\left(2-\Delta_{1}-\Delta_{2}\right)+\frac{B\left(-\Delta_{1}/2\right)}{4}\frac{1}{\left|x_{12}\right|^{4-\Delta_{1}-\Delta_{2}}}\delta\left(\Delta_{1}-\Delta_{2}\right)\right),\label{eq:Celestial-Two-Point-Function-1}
\end{align}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{1}\eqqcolon1+i\lambda$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The celestial 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

-point function of the shadow-transformed conformal primaries exhibits a similar structure to that of Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Celestial-Two-Point-Function"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 namely,
 a linear combination of the delta function,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 and a standard power-law dependence,
 
\begin_inset Formula $1/\left|x_{12}\right|^{2-\Delta_{1}-\Delta_{2}}$
\end_inset

.
 However,
 the functional dependence on the conformal weights 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{1},\Delta_{2}$
\end_inset

 in Eqs.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Celestial-Two-Point-Function"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 ,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Celestial-Two-Point-Function-1"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)–aside from the prefactor 
\begin_inset Formula $1/\lambda^{2}_{1}=1/\left(1-\Delta_{1}\right)^{2}$
\end_inset

–is distinct.
 Furthermore,
 it is essential to recall that,
 within celestial CFT,
 the conformal weights 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{1},\Delta_{2}$
\end_inset

 are dynamical physical variables,
 corresponding to the Mellin conjugates of the frequencies of the particles involved in the scattering process.
 Therefore,
 the functional dependencies on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{1},\Delta_{2}$
\end_inset

 encode non-trivial physical information about the scattering events.
 However,
 as demonstrated by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "teschner1999mini"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the bases 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j}\left(x;z\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\widetilde{\Phi}^{-1-j}\left(x;z\right)$
\end_inset

 yield the same representation within the framework of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model,
 a result that becomes apparent from the structure of the shadow-transformed 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

-point function derived earlier.
 Consequently,
 this equivalence of bases in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model leads to non-trivial physical implications for celestial CFT,
 an important matter that we will leave unresolved for the time being,
 to be addressed in future investigations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Three-Point Function
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Three-Point-Function"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this subsection,
 we employ the exact form of the 
\begin_inset Formula $3$
\end_inset

-point function in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model,
 as determined by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "teschner1997conformal"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 to evaluate the celestial amplitude corresponding to the scattering of three massless scalar particles within the framework of our holographic dictionary.
 To structure our analysis,
 we adhere to the formalism introduced by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "teschner1999structure,teschner2000operator"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 in his subsequent works concerning the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model.
 For a more comprehensive account,
 we direct the reader to these works.
 It is important to note,
 however,
 that we adopt the normalisation conventions for the worldsheet primaries as defined by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "giveon1998comments"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us denote the conformal weights associated with the celestial scalar conformal primaries 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\Delta_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\Delta_{2}}\left(x_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\Delta_{3}}\left(x_{3}\right)$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{3}$
\end_inset

,
 respectively.
 Correspondingly,
 we introduce the quantities 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{1}\coloneqq-\Delta_{1}/2$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{2}\coloneqq-\Delta_{2}/2$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{3}\coloneqq-\Delta_{3}/2$
\end_inset

,
 which represent the spins of the worldsheet primaries within the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model.
 For the sake of notational simplicity,
 we temporarily omit the anti-holomorphic variables from our arguments,
 thereby denoting the worldsheet primaries as 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j_{2}}\left(x_{2};z_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j_{3}}\left(x_{3};z_{3}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is important to emphasise that the correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\Delta_{k}}\left(x_{k}\right)\leftrightarrow\Phi^{j_{k}}\left(x_{k};z_{k}\right)$
\end_inset

 of our holographic dictionary maps a 
\emph on
scalar
\emph default
 celestial wavefunction to a worldsheet conformal primary with 
\emph on
spin
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 The relation 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{k}\leftrightarrow-\Delta_{k}/2$
\end_inset

 summarises the mapping between the notion of spin in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-model and the conformal weight in the celestial CFT.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The scaling dimensions of the worldsheet primaries are related to their respective spins through the relations:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
h_{1}=-b^{2}j_{1}\left(j_{1}+1\right),\,\,\,h_{2}=-b^{2}j_{2}\left(j_{2}+1\right),\,\,\,h_{3}=-b^{2}j_{3}\left(j_{3}+1\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $b^{2}\coloneqq1/\left(k-2\right)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
According to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
after "Eq. (22)"
key "teschner2000operator"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $3$
\end_inset

-point correlation function in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \Phi^{j_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)\Phi^{j_{2}}\left(x_{2};z_{2}\right)\Phi^{j_{3}}\left(x_{3};z_{3}\right)\right\rangle =\frac{D\left(j_{1},j_{2},j_{3}\right)C\left(j_{1},j_{2},j_{3}\big|x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}\right)}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{2\sigma_{3}}\left|z_{23}\right|^{2\sigma_{1}}\left|z_{31}\right|^{2\sigma_{2}}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the Clebsch-Gordan distributional kernel is defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
C\left(j_{1},j_{2},j_{3}\big|x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}\right)=\frac{1}{\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\lambda_{3}}\left|x_{23}\right|^{2\lambda_{1}}\left|x_{31}\right|^{2\lambda_{2}}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The exponents associated with the worldsheet coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{3}$
\end_inset

 are defined as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{1}\coloneqq-h_{1}+h_{2}+h_{3},\,\,\,\sigma_{2}\coloneqq h_{1}-h_{2}+h_{3},\,\,\,\sigma_{3}\coloneqq h_{1}+h_{2}-h_{3},\label{eq:Exponents}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

while the exponents corresponding to the celestial coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{3}$
\end_inset

 are similarly given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lambda_{1}\coloneqq j_{1}-j_{2}-j_{3},\,\,\,\lambda_{2}\coloneqq-j_{1}+j_{2}-j_{3},\,\,\,\lambda_{3}\coloneqq-j_{1}-j_{2}+j_{3}.\label{eq:Exponents-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In accordance with the holographic dictionary proposed in Section 2,
 the celestial 
\begin_inset Formula $3$
\end_inset

-point amplitude corresponding to the scalar wavefunctions 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\Delta_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\Delta_{2}}\left(x_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\Delta_{3}}\left(x_{3}\right)$
\end_inset

 is expressed as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \left\langle \phi_{\Delta_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)\phi_{\Delta_{2}}\left(x_{2}\right)\phi_{\Delta_{3}}\left(x_{3}\right)\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}\\
 & =\frac{1}{N}\int d^{2}z_{1}\int d^{2}z_{2}\int d^{2}z_{3}\left\langle \Phi^{j_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)\Phi^{j_{2}}\left(x_{2};z_{2}\right)\Phi^{j_{3}}\left(x_{3};z_{3}\right)\right\rangle \\
 & =D\left(j_{1},j_{2},j_{3}\right)C\left(j_{1},j_{2},j_{3}\big|x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}\right)\frac{1}{N}\int d^{2}z_{1}\int d^{2}z_{2}\int d^{2}z_{3}\frac{1}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{2\sigma_{3}}\left|z_{23}\right|^{2\sigma_{1}}\left|z_{31}\right|^{2\sigma_{2}}}.\label{eq:Step-5}
\end{align}

\end_inset

Here,
 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 denotes the worldsheet area,
 and the factor 
\begin_inset Formula $1/N$
\end_inset

 serves as a renormalisation of the integration measure to ensure that the correlation function is well-defined and finite–a standard procedure in the computation of scattering amplitudes within string perturbation theory (cf.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "d1988geometry,polchinski1998string,polchinski2001string"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As demonstrated in Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Triple-Worldsheet-Integral"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 the integral over the worldsheet coordinates evaluates to:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \frac{1}{V}\int d^{2}z_{1}\int d^{2}z_{2}\int d^{2}z_{3}\frac{1}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{2\sigma_{3}}\left|z_{23}\right|^{2\sigma_{1}}\left|z_{31}\right|^{2\sigma_{2}}}\\
 & =2\pi^{3}\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(2-\sigma_{1}-\sigma_{2}-\sigma_{3}\right)\frac{\Gamma\left(1-\sigma_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(1-\sigma_{2}\right)\Gamma\left(1-\sigma_{3}\right)}{\Gamma\left(\sigma_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{2}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{3}\right)}.\label{eq:Integral}
\end{align}

\end_inset

It is noteworthy that the triple integral over the worldsheet yields the 
\emph on
generalised 
\emph default
delta ``function'' 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(2-\sum_{i}\sigma_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

 associated with the conformal weights.
 This form is characteristic of celestial amplitudes,
 as discussed in previous works (e.g.,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "arkani2021celestial,gu2022note,stieberger2018strings"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 Importantly,
 this delta function would not be present had we chosen to disregard the worldsheet coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{3}$
\end_inset

,
 as was done by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ogawa2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consequently,
 employing the previously defined equations (Eqs.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Exponents"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-5"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Integral"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)),
 we arrive at:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \left\langle \phi_{\Delta_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)\phi_{\Delta_{2}}\left(x_{2}\right)\phi_{\Delta_{3}}\left(x_{3}\right)\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}\\
 & =2\pi^{3}\frac{\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(2-h_{1}-h_{2}-h_{3}\right)D\left(j_{1},j_{2},j_{3}\right)}{\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\left(-j_{1}-j_{2}+j_{3}\right)}\left|x_{23}\right|^{2\left(j_{1}-j_{2}-j_{3}\right)}\left|x_{31}\right|^{2\left(-j_{1}+j_{2}-j_{3}\right)}}\frac{\Gamma\left(2h_{1}-1\right)\Gamma\left(2h_{2}-1\right)\Gamma\left(2h_{3}-1\right)}{\Gamma\left(2-2h_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(2-2h_{2}\right)\Gamma\left(2-2h_{3}\right)}.
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This exact form of the 
\begin_inset Formula $3$
\end_inset

-point celestial amplitude,
 derived as the correlation function within Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 string theory via our proposed holographic dictionary,
 explicitly exhibits the distributional structure anticipated in celestial amplitudes.
 Moreover,
 the presence of gamma functions of the scaling dimensions is indicative of an amplitude derived from worldsheet integrations,
 aligning with the general expectations from calculations in string perturbation theory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Celestial Operator Product Expansion
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Operator-Product-Expansion"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this subsection,
 we determine the celestial operator product expansion utilising the structure constants of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model as derived by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "teschner1999structure,teschner2000operator"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 However,
 for consistency,
 we adopt the normalisation conventions used by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "giveon1998comments"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{2}$
\end_inset

 denote the conformal weights associated with the scalar celestial wavefunctions 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\Delta_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\Delta_{2}}\left(x_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 respectively.
 According to the proposed holographic dictionary,
 these wavefunctions correspond to the worldsheet primaries 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j_{2}}\left(x_{2};z_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 where the spins in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model are related to the celestial conformal weights by 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{1}=-\Delta_{1}/2$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{2}=-\Delta_{2}/2$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
According to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "teschner2000operator"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the worldsheet OPE for the primaries 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j_{2}}\left(x_{2};z_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 is then expressed as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \Phi^{j_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)\Phi^{j_{2}}\left(x_{2};z_{2}\right)\sim\\
 & -\frac{1}{2}\int_{-\frac{1}{2}+i\mathbf{R}^{+}}dj_{3}\frac{\left(1+2j_{3}\right)^{2}}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{\sigma_{3}}}D\left(j_{1},j_{2},j_{3}\right)\int d^{2}x_{3}\frac{1}{\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\lambda_{3}}\left|x_{23}\right|^{2\lambda_{1}}\left|x_{31}\right|^{2\lambda_{2}}}\Phi^{-1-j_{3}}\left(x_{3};z_{2}\right).\label{eq:OPE-1}
\end{align}

\end_inset

Here,
 the exponents 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{3}$
\end_inset

 associated with the celestial coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{3}$
\end_inset

 are defined by Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Exponents"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{3}=h_{1}+h_{2}-h_{3}$
\end_inset

 as per Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Exponents-1"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our objective is to compute the celestial OPE involving two scalar conformal primaries.
 To achieve this,
 we adopt the following strategy:
 we investigate the behaviour of 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\Delta_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)\phi_{\Delta_{2}}\left(x_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 within a correlation function of the celestial CFT.
 Utilising Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:OPE-1"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 we express this as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \left\langle \phi_{\Delta_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)\phi_{\Delta_{2}}\left(x_{2}\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}\sim-\frac{1}{2}\int_{-\frac{1}{2}+i\mathbf{R}^{+}}\left(1+2j_{3}\right)^{2}D\left(j_{1},j_{2},j_{3}\right)\\
 & \int d^{2}x_{3}\frac{1}{\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\lambda_{3}}\left|x_{23}\right|^{2\lambda_{1}}\left|x_{31}\right|^{2\lambda_{2}}}\int d^{2}z_{1}d^{2}z_{2}\frac{1}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{\sigma_{3}}}\left\langle \Phi^{-1-j_{3}}\left(x_{3};z_{2}\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}.\label{eq:OPE-2}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Shadow-Transform-of"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Integral-6"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 we have demonstrated that the worldsheet integral evaluates to:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int d^{2}z_{1}\int d^{2}z_{2}\frac{1}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{\sigma_{3}}}\Phi^{-1-j_{3}}\left(x_{3};z_{2}\right)=\pi^{2}\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(h_{1}+h_{2}+h_{3}-2\right)\int d^{2}z_{2}\Phi^{-1-j_{3}}\left(x_{3};z_{2}\right).\label{eq:Integral-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Utilising the relations between the scaling dimensions 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{3}$
\end_inset

 and the spins 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{3}$
\end_inset

 in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
h_{1}=-b^{2}j_{1}\left(j_{1}+1\right),\,\,\,h_{2}=-b^{2}j_{2}\left(j_{2}+1\right),\,\,\,h_{3}=-b^{2}j_{3}\left(j_{3}+1\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $b^{2}\coloneqq1/\left(k-2\right)$
\end_inset

,
 we can decompose the generalised Dirac delta function of the scaling dimensions appearing in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Integral-1"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(h_{1}+h_{2}+h_{3}-2\right)=\frac{\delta\left(j_{3}-j^{+}_{3}\right)}{b^{2}\left(1+2j^{+}_{3}\right)}+\frac{\delta\left(j_{3}-j^{-}_{3}\right)}{b^{2}\left(1+2j^{-}_{3}\right)},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
j^{\pm}_{3}=-\frac{1}{2}\pm i\rho\left(j_{1},j_{2}\right),\,\,\,-\rho^{2}\left(j_{1},j_{2}\right)\coloneqq\frac{1}{4}+\frac{2}{b^{2}}+j_{1}\left(j_{1}+1\right)+j_{2}\left(j_{2}+1\right).\label{eq:Isospin}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Substituting these relations into Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:OPE-2"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) yields:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \left\langle \phi_{\Delta_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)\phi_{\Delta_{2}}\left(x_{2}\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}\sim\\
 & -\frac{\pi^{2}}{2b^{2}}\left(1+2j^{+}_{3}\right)D\left(j_{1},j_{2},j_{3}\right)\mathcal{I}_{2}\left(\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\lambda_{3}\right)\left\langle \phi_{-1-\Delta_{3}\left(j^{+}_{3}\right)}\left(x_{2}\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}},\label{eq:Step-8}
\end{align}

\end_inset

where we have introduced the new integral:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{2}\left(\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\lambda_{3}\right)\coloneqq\frac{1}{\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\lambda_{3}}}\int d^{2}x_{3}\frac{1}{\left|x_{23}\right|^{2\lambda_{1}}\left|x_{31}\right|^{2\lambda_{2}}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the Appendix 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Worldsheet-Integral-with"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Integral-7"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 we have shown that this integral evaluates to:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{2}=\frac{\pi}{\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\left(\lambda_{1}+\lambda_{2}+\lambda_{3}-1\right)}}\frac{\Gamma\left(\lambda_{1}+\lambda_{2}-1\right)}{\Gamma\left(2-\lambda_{1}-\lambda_{2}\right)}\frac{\Gamma\left(1-\lambda_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(1-\lambda_{2}\right)}{\Gamma\left(\lambda_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\lambda_{2}\right)}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Recalling that the exponents 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{3}$
\end_inset

 are related to the spins 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{3}$
\end_inset

 through the relations provided in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Exponents-1"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 we find:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{2}=\pi\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\left(j_{1}+j_{2}+j_{3}+1\right)}\frac{\Gamma\left(-1-2j_{3}\right)}{\Gamma\left(2+2j_{3}\right)}\frac{\Gamma\left(1-j_{1}+j_{2}+j_{3}\right)\Gamma\left(1+j_{1}-j_{2}+j_{3}\right)}{\Gamma\left(j_{1}-j_{2}-j_{3}\right)\Gamma\left(-j_{1}+j_{2}-j_{3}\right)}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This expression can be rewritten using Euler's beta functions as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{2}=-\frac{\pi}{1+2j_{2}}\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\left(j_{1}+j_{2}+j_{3}+1\right)}\frac{B\left(1-j_{1}+j_{2}+j_{3},1+j_{1}-j_{2}+j_{3}\right)}{B\left(j_{1}-j_{2}-j_{3},-j_{1}+j_{2}-j_{3}\right)}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 substituting this result into Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-8"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 we obtain the celestial OPE following from our proposed holographic dictionary:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \phi_{\Delta_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)\phi_{\Delta_{2}}\left(x_{2}\right)\sim\label{eq:Final-OPE}\\
 & \frac{\pi^{3}}{2b^{2}}\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\left(j_{1}+j_{2}+j^{+}_{3}+1\right)}D\left(j_{1},j_{2},j^{+}_{3}\right)\frac{B\left(1-j_{1}+j_{2}+j_{3},1+j_{1}-j_{2}+j_{3}\right)}{B\left(j_{1}-j_{2}-j_{3},-j_{1}+j_{2}-j_{3}\right)}\phi_{-1-\Delta\left(j^{+}_{3}\right)}.
\end{align}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta\left(j^{+}_{3}\right)=-2j^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $j^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

 given by Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Isospin"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To appreciate the physical significance of this OPE,
 we recall that,
 in celestial CFT,
 the conformal dimensions 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

 acquire the interpretation of dynamical physical quantities.
 This arises from the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

 are Mellin conjugates to the energies of scattered particles.
 Therefore,
 the product of gamma functions,
 which depends on the conformal weights–arising from the worldsheet integrals and manifesting both the worldsheet conformal invariance and the factorisation properties of the string vertex operators–is not merely a proportionality constant.
 Rather,
 it is a physical quantity that characterises the scattering processes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is noteworthy that even the much simpler celestial OPE for gluons in pure Yang-Mills theory,
 given by:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{G}^{a+}_{\Delta_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)\mathcal{G}^{b+}_{\Delta_{2}}\left(x_{2}\right)\sim\frac{if^{abc}}{z_{12}}\frac{\Gamma\left(-1-2j_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(-1-2j_{2}\right)}{\Gamma\left(-2j_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(-2j_{2}\right)}\mathcal{G}^{c+}_{\Delta_{1}+\Delta_{2}-1}\left(x_{2}\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{1}=-\Delta_{1}/2$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{2}=-\Delta_{2}/2$
\end_inset

,
 while considerably less intricate than Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Final-OPE"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 exhibits a similar structure.
 In this expression,
 the product of gamma functions that depends on the conformal weights encodes information about the scattering processes of gluons in flat space.
 This observation leads one to infer that the structure of the OPEs in the Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 string model for celestial CFT naturally encapsulates the physics of these scattering processes.
 We also note that this structure appears to deviate from that of conventional CFTs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Differential Equations for the Celestial CFT
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Differential-Equations-for"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the previous subsections,
 we have illustrated our holographic dictionary by computing the two- and three-point celestial amplitudes and extracting the structure constants of the celestial OPE,
 derived from the corresponding quantities in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model.
 The analytic structure of this model has been rigorously investigated by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ribault2005h3+,teschner1997conformal,teschner1999mini,teschner1999structure,teschner2000operator"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In this subsection,
 we conclude the exposition of our holographic dictionary between Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 bosonic string theory and celestial CFT in Klein space by deriving a system of partial differential equations that characterise the celestial amplitudes from the Knizhnik–Zamolodchikov (KZ) equations and the worldsheet Ward identities of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{G}_{n}=\widehat{G}_{n}\left(x_{1},...,x_{n};\Delta_{1},...,\Delta_{n};z_{1},...,z_{n}\right)$
\end_inset

 denote the 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-point function of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model,
 defined as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{G}_{n}\coloneqq\left\langle \Phi^{j_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)\Phi^{j_{2}}\left(x_{2};z_{2}\right)...\Phi^{j_{n}}\left(x_{n};z_{n}\right)\right\rangle ,\label{eq:N-Point-Function}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the spins in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model are related to the conformal weights in the celestial CFT by 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{k}=-\Delta_{k}/2$
\end_inset

.
 According to the holographic dictionary,
 the celestial amplitude corresponding to 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{G}_{n}$
\end_inset

 is obtained by integrating Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:N-Point-Function"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) over the worldsheet variables 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1},...,z_{n}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The KZ equations for the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model are deduced as follows.
 As discussed in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "giveon1998comments"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the Sugawara stress tensor 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{\text{S}}\left(z\right)$
\end_inset

 of the worldsheet CFT is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
T_{\text{S}}\left(z\right)=\frac{1}{k-2}\left(J^{+}J^{-}-\left(J^{3}\right)^{2}\right)=\frac{b^{2}}{2}\left(J\partial^{2}_{x}J-\frac{1}{2}\left(\partial_{x}J\right)^{2}\right).\label{eq:Sugawara}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Utilising the Sugawara construction (reviewed in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ginsparg1988applied,francesco1997conformal,ketov1995conformal"
literal "false"

\end_inset

),
 we find that the stress-energy tensor 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{\text{S}}\left(z\right)$
\end_inset

 satisfies the Ward identity:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \left\langle T_{\text{S}}\left(z\right)\Phi^{j_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)\Phi^{j_{2}}\left(x_{2};z_{2}\right)...\Phi^{j_{n}}\left(x_{n};z_{n}\right)\right\rangle \\
 & =\sum^{n}_{i=1}\left(\frac{1}{z-z_{i}}\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{i}}+\frac{h_{i}}{\left(z-z_{i}\right)^{2}}\right)\left\langle \Phi^{j_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)\Phi^{j_{2}}\left(x_{2};z_{2}\right)...\Phi^{j_{n}}\left(x_{n};z_{n}\right)\right\rangle \label{eq:Ward-3}
\end{align}

\end_inset

Substituting Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Sugawara"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) into the left-hand side of Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Ward-3"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) and applying Wick's theorem yields the KZ equations for the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 as 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ribault2005h3+"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 observed,
 it is more convenient to express the KZ equations in terms of the so-called 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

-basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j}\left(\mu;z\right)$
\end_inset

,
 defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Phi^{j}\left(\mu;z\right)\coloneqq\frac{\left|\mu\right|^{2j+2}}{\pi}\int d^{2}xe^{\mu x-\bar{\mu}\bar{x}}\Phi^{j}\left(x;z\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In this coordinate system 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

 on the celestial sphere,
 the KZ equations take the form (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ribault2005h3+"
literal "false"

\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\widehat{G}_{n}}{\partial z_{k}}+b^{2}\sum_{k\neq\ell}\frac{\mu_{k}\mu_{\ell}}{z_{k}-z_{\ell}}\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial\mu_{k}}-\frac{\partial}{\partial\mu_{\ell}}\right)^{2}\widehat{G}_{n}=b^{2}\sum_{k\neq\ell}\frac{\mu_{k}\mu_{\ell}}{z_{k}-z_{\ell}}\left[\frac{j_{k}\left(j_{k}+1\right)}{\mu^{2}_{k}}+\frac{j_{\ell}\left(j_{\ell}+1\right)}{\mu^{2}_{\ell}}\right]\widehat{G}_{n}.\label{eq:KZ}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Following the standard prescription in celestial holography (see reviews by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "pasterski2021lectures,raclariu2021lectures,strominger2018lectures,puhmcelestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

),
 the scattering amplitude 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}_{n}$
\end_inset

 associated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{G}_{n}$
\end_inset

 in the energy-momentum representation is obtained by performing an inverse Mellin transform of the worldsheet integral of 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{G}_{n}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}_{n}=\prod^{n}_{i=1}\int^{c+i\infty}_{c-i\infty}\frac{d\Delta_{i}}{2\pi i}\omega^{-\Delta_{i}}_{i}\prod^{n}_{j=1}\int d^{2}z_{j}\widehat{G}_{n}\left(x_{1},...,x_{n};\Delta_{1},...,\Delta_{n};z_{1},...,z_{n}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since our primary goal is to derive a system of partial differential equations (PDEs) characterising the celestial 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-point functions,
 we may omit the worldsheet integrals and define instead a quantity 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{n}\coloneqq\left(x_{1},...,x_{n};\omega_{1},...,\omega_{n};z_{1},...,z_{n}\right)$
\end_inset

 given by the inverse Mellin transform of 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{G}_{n}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
G_{n}=\prod^{n}_{i=1}\int^{c+i\infty}_{c-i\infty}\frac{d\Delta_{i}}{2\pi i}\omega^{-\Delta_{i}}_{i}\widehat{G}_{n}\left(x_{1},...,x_{n};\Delta_{1},...,\Delta_{n};z_{1},...,z_{n}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We aim to use the KZ equations to derive a system of PDEs for 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{n}$
\end_inset

.
 Assuming the resulting equations can be solved by traditional methods,
 the final amplitude is then given by integrating 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{n}$
\end_inset

 over the worldsheet coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1},...,z_{n}$
\end_inset

.
 Applying the inverse Mellin transform to Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:KZ"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 and using the identities:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\omega\frac{\partial}{\partial\omega}\int^{c+i\infty}_{c-i\infty}\frac{d\Delta}{2\pi i}\omega^{-\Delta}\hat{f}\left(\Delta\right)=-\int^{c+i\infty}_{c-i\infty}\frac{d\Delta}{2\pi i}\Delta\omega^{-\Delta}\hat{f}\left(\Delta\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left(\omega\frac{\partial}{\partial\omega}\right)^{2}\int^{c+i\infty}_{c-i\infty}\frac{d\Delta}{2\pi i}\omega^{-\Delta}\hat{f}\left(\Delta\right)=\int^{c+i\infty}_{c-i\infty}\frac{d\Delta}{2\pi i}\Delta^{2}\omega^{-\Delta}\hat{f}\left(\Delta\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

we obtain the following differential equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \frac{\partial G_{n}}{\partial z_{k}}+b^{2}\sum_{k\neq\ell}\frac{\mu_{k}\mu_{\ell}}{z_{k}-z_{\ell}}\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial\mu_{k}}-\frac{\partial}{\partial\mu_{\ell}}\right)^{2}G_{n}=\frac{b^{2}}{2}\sum_{k\neq\ell}\frac{\mu_{k}\mu_{\ell}}{z_{k}-z_{\ell}}\left(\frac{\omega_{k}}{\mu^{2}_{k}}\frac{\partial}{\partial\omega_{k}}+\frac{\omega_{\ell}}{\mu^{2}_{\ell}}\frac{\partial}{\partial\omega_{\ell}}\right)G_{n}\\
 & +\frac{b^{2}}{4}\sum_{k\neq\ell}\frac{\mu_{k}\mu_{\ell}}{z_{k}-z_{\ell}}\left[\frac{1}{\mu^{2}_{k}}\left(\omega_{k}\frac{\partial}{\partial\omega_{k}}\right)^{2}+\frac{1}{\mu^{2}_{\ell}}\left(\omega_{\ell}\frac{\partial}{\partial\omega_{\ell}}\right)^{2}\right]G_{n}.
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let us now consider the worldsheet Ward identity:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sum^{n}_{k=1}z_{k}\frac{\partial\widehat{G}_{n}}{\partial z_{k}}+\sum^{n}_{k=1}h_{k}\widehat{G}_{n}=0.\label{eq:Ward-4}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Recalling that the scaling dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{k}$
\end_inset

 in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model is related to the spin 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{k}$
\end_inset

 by the relation 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{k}=-b^{2}j_{k}\left(j_{k}+1\right)$
\end_inset

,
 and that the holographic dictionary prescribes that the spin is connected to the celestial conformal weight 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{k}$
\end_inset

 via 
\begin_inset Formula $j_{k}=-\Delta_{k}/2$
\end_inset

,
 Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Ward-4"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) can be reformulated as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sum^{n}_{k=1}z_{k}\frac{\partial\widehat{G}_{n}}{\partial z_{k}}+\frac{b^{2}}{2}\sum^{n}_{k=1}\Delta_{k}\widehat{G}_{n}-\frac{b^{2}}{4}\sum^{n}_{k=1}\Delta^{2}_{k}\widehat{G}_{n}=0.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Finally,
 by applying the inverse Mellin transform,
 we obtain the following differential equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sum^{n}_{k=1}z_{k}\frac{\partial G_{n}}{\partial z_{k}}=\frac{b^{2}}{2}\sum^{n}_{k=1}\omega_{k}\frac{\partial G_{n}}{\partial\omega_{k}}+\frac{b^{2}}{4}\sum^{n}_{k=1}\left(\omega_{k}\frac{\partial}{\partial\omega_{k}}\right)^{2}G_{n}.\label{eq:PDE}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Celestial Stress-Energy Tensor
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Virasoro-Current-Algebra"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this subsection,
 we shall discuss the celestial stress-energy tensor,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

.
 As a preliminary observation,
 note that,
 based on the anticipated scaling dimension of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

,
 it necessarily follows that it may be represented as a linear combination of the worldsheet currents,
 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{J}$
\end_inset

,
 together with the primary 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T}\left(x\right)\coloneqq\frac{1}{2k}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z\left(c_{1}J\partial^{2}_{x}\Phi+c_{2}\partial_{x}J\partial_{x}\Phi+c_{3}\Phi\partial^{2}_{x}J\right)\bar{J}.\label{eq:Stress-Tensor}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Since all terms within brackets are evaluated at the worldsheet coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(z,\bar{z}\right)$
\end_inset

 and the celestial coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x,\bar{x}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 we will temporarily omit the explicit arguments of the currents 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{J}$
\end_inset

,
 and the primary 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi$
\end_inset

.
 However,
 when analysing the behaviour of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 or its derivatives within correlation functions,
 we will restore the explicit dependencies on these variables to avoid ambiguity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given that the central extension 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}$
\end_inset

,
 introduced in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Central-Extension"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial^{2}_{x}\mathcal{I}=0$
\end_inset

.
 It follows that:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z\,J\bar{J}\partial^{2}_{x}\Phi=-\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z\,\left(\Phi\partial^{2}_{x}J+2\partial_{x}J\partial_{x}\Phi\right)\bar{J}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Substituting this into Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Stress-Tensor"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 we derive the expression:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T}\left(x\right)=\frac{1}{2k}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z\,\left(\alpha_{1}\partial_{x}J\partial_{x}\Phi+2\alpha_{2}\Phi\partial^{2}_{x}J\right)\bar{J},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the new constants 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}\coloneqq c_{2}-2c_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $2\alpha_{2}\coloneqq c_{3}-c_{1}$
\end_inset

 have been introduced.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using the identity 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{J}\Phi=\left(k/\pi\right)\partial_{\bar{z}}\lambda$
\end_inset

,
 and applying the complex-divergence theorem (Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Divergence-Theorem"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)),
 we can rewrite 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{T}\left(x\right) & =\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z\,\left(\alpha_{1}\partial_{x}J\partial_{x}\partial_{\bar{z}}\lambda+2\alpha_{2}\partial^{2}_{x}J\partial_{\bar{z}}\lambda\right)\\
 & =\frac{1}{2}\oint\frac{dz}{2\pi i}\left(\alpha_{1}\partial_{x}J\partial_{x}\lambda+2\alpha_{2}\lambda\partial^{2}_{x}J\right).
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To constrain the coefficients 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 we impose BRST invariance,
 requiring that the term inside the brackets in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Stress-Tensor"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) transforms as a worldsheet primary.
 A more efficient approach,
 however,
 stems from recognising that,
 by construction,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)$
\end_inset

 generates a level-
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{k}_{G}$
\end_inset

 celestial Kac-Moody current algebra and satisfies the Ward identities.
 In particular,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 can only function as a genuine stress tensor if the OPE 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}\left(x\right)\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)$
\end_inset

 includes the term:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T}\left(x\right)\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)\sim...+\frac{1}{x-y}\partial_{y}\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)+...
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 we compute this OPE and impose the above condition as a constraint.
 It is advantageous to work with the partial derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 with respect to the anti-holomorphic variable 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\partial_{\bar{x}}\mathcal{T}\left(x\right) & =\frac{1}{2}\oint\frac{dz}{2\pi i}\left(\alpha_{1}\partial_{x}J\partial_{x}\partial_{\bar{x}}\lambda+2\alpha_{2}\partial^{2}_{x}J\partial_{\bar{x}}\lambda\right)\\
 & =\frac{1}{2}\oint\frac{dz}{2i}\left(\alpha_{1}\partial_{x}J\partial_{x}\Phi+2\alpha_{2}\Phi\partial^{2}_{x}J\right),
\end{align}

\end_inset

and evaluate its contribution within correlation functions that include an insertion of the current 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To simplify our calculations,
 we divide 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 into the components:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T}_{1}\left(x\right)\coloneqq\frac{\alpha_{1}}{2}\oint\frac{dz}{2i}\partial_{x}J\partial_{x}\Phi,\,\,\,\mathcal{T}_{2}\left(x\right)\coloneqq\alpha_{2}\oint\frac{dz}{2i}\Phi\partial^{2}_{x}J.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The contribution of the OPE 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{\bar{x}}\mathcal{T}_{1}\left(x\right)\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)$
\end_inset

 to the correlation function is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \left\langle \partial_{x}\mathcal{T}_{1}\left(x\right)\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}\\
 & =-\frac{\alpha_{1}}{2k}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}w\oint\frac{dz}{2i}\langle j^{a}\left(w\right)\partial_{x}J\left(x;z\right)\partial_{x}\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)\bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right)...\rangle\\
 & \sim-\frac{\alpha_{1}}{2k}\partial_{x}\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x-y\right)\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}w\oint_{C_{\varepsilon}\left(w\right)}\frac{dz}{2i}\langle j^{a}\left(w\right)\partial_{x}J\left(x;z\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)\bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right)...\rangle.\label{eq:Step-X}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recalling (cf.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
after "Sec. 2"
key "giveon1998comments"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) that the worldsheet current operator 
\begin_inset Formula $J\left(x;z\right)$
\end_inset

 acts on the primary 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)$
\end_inset

 as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
J\left(x;z\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)\sim\frac{1}{z-w}\left[\left(y-x\right)^{2}\partial_{y}+2\left(y-x\right)\right]\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

we take the partial derivative with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 to obtain:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\partial_{x}J\left(x;z\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)\sim\frac{2}{z-w}\left[\left(x-y\right)\partial_{y}-1\right]\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right).\label{eq:Identity}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Therefore,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\langle\partial_{\bar{x}}\mathcal{T}_{1}\left(x\right)\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)...\rangle_{\text{CCFT}}\sim\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{x}}\left(\frac{\alpha_{1}}{\left(x-y\right)^{2}}\left\langle \mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}-\frac{\alpha_{1}}{x-y}\left\langle \partial_{y}\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}\right).\label{eq:Step-4}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Next,
 consider the contribution from 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}_{2}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Taking its partial derivative and inserting it into the correlation function with the Kac-Moody current 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)$
\end_inset

 yields:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \langle\partial_{\bar{x}}\mathcal{T}_{2}\left(x\right)\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)...\rangle_{\text{CCFT}}\\
 & \sim-\frac{\alpha_{2}}{k}\int d^{2}w\oint\frac{dz}{2i}\langle\partial^{2}_{x}J\left(x;z\right)\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)\bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right)j^{a}\left(w\right)...\rangle.\label{eq:Step-2}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Taking the partial derivative of Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Identity"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 gives:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\partial^{2}_{x}J\left(x;z\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)\sim\frac{2}{z-w}\partial_{y}\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Substituting this result into Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-2"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) gives:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\langle\partial_{\bar{x}}\mathcal{T}_{2}\left(x\right)\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)...\rangle_{\text{CCFT}}\sim\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{x}}\left(\frac{2\alpha_{2}}{\left(x-y\right)^{2}}\left\langle \mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}+\frac{2\alpha_{2}}{x-y}\left\langle \partial_{y}\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}\right).\label{eq:Step-3}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consequently,
 combining the results obtained in Eqs.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-4"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-3"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 and integrating with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x},$
\end_inset

 we finally obtain:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T}\left(x\right)\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)\sim\frac{2\alpha_{2}+\alpha_{1}}{\left(x-y\right)^{2}}\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)+\frac{2\alpha_{2}-\alpha_{1}}{x-y}\partial_{y}\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right).\label{eq:Ward-2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 a necessary condition for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 to be a valid stress tensor in the celestial CFT is that the coefficient of 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x-y\right)^{-1}\partial_{y}\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(y\right)$
\end_inset

 in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Ward-2"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) must equal one.
 This implies that the constants 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

 must satisfy the constraint 
\begin_inset Formula $2\alpha_{2}-\alpha_{1}=1$
\end_inset

.
 A convenient choice is 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}=\alpha_{2}=1$
\end_inset

,
 which leads to the final form of the celestial stress tensor:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{T}\left(x\right)=\frac{1}{2}\oint\frac{dz}{2\pi i}\left(\partial_{x}J\partial_{x}\lambda+2\lambda\partial^{2}_{x}J\right).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In conclusion,
 following the derivation presented in Section 3 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "kutasov1999more"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the Ward identity for the OPE of the celestial stress-energy tensor 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}\mathcal{T}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T}\left(x_{1}\right)\mathcal{T}\left(x_{2}\right)\sim\frac{c^{(\text{st})}/2}{\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)^{4}}+\frac{2\mathcal{T}\left(x_{2}\right)}{\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)^{2}}+\frac{\partial_{x_{2}}\mathcal{T}\left(x_{2}\right)}{x_{1}-x_{2}}.\label{eq:-6}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The constant 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{(\text{st})}$
\end_inset

 entering Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:-6"

\end_inset

) is the central charge of the spacetime Virasoro algebra associated with the stress-energy tensor 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}(x)$
\end_inset

.
 In the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}$
\end_inset

 string-theory construction of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "kutasov1999more"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 this central charge is determined by the so-called central-extension operator 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

,
 which is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
I=\frac{1}{k^{2}}\int\mathrm{d}^{2}z\;J(x;z)\overline{J}(\bar{x};\bar{z})\Phi_{1}(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z})\,.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We recall that 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "kutasov1999more"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 demonstrated that the 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

-operator is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

,
 and behaves as a spacetime identity operator up to a proportionality factor.
 Let us denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}(g_{s})$
\end_inset

 the eigenvalue such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\langle\langle IV_{1}\cdot\cdot\cdot V_{n}\rangle\rangle=\mathcal{P}(g_{s})\langle\langle IV_{1}\cdot\cdot\cdot V_{n}\rangle\rangle\,,\label{eq:-7}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{s}$
\end_inset

 denotes the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}$
\end_inset

 string coupling constant.
 The double-bracket notation in the preceding equation signifies that the expression holds when the correlators on each side of the equality receive corrections from all worldsheet topologies,
 including disconnected ones,
 and we refer the reader to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "de1999string"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for a careful discussion of this point.
 Furthermore,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "kutasov1999more"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 showed that the central charge 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{(\text{st})}$
\end_inset

 is related to the level 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 and the eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}(g_{s})$
\end_inset

 by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c^{(\text{st})}=6k\mathcal{P}(g_{s})\,.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

For clarity,
 we emphasise that 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{(\text{st})}$
\end_inset

 should not be confused with the worldsheet 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW central charge,
 which is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
c_{H^{+}_{3}}=\frac{3k}{k-2}\,.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now discuss a possible physical interpretation of Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:-6"

\end_inset

),
 and in particular of the central charge 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{(\text{st})}$
\end_inset

,
 from the point of view of the four-dimensional bulk theory.
 To contextualise,
 we first recall that 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "barnich2011bms"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 analysed the general structure of the BMS algebra,
 and the associated Dirac bracket,
 in three- and four-dimensional flat spacetimes by considering the classical symmetry transformations and charges.
 They found that the global sub-algebra in four dimensions has no central charges.
 This result was subsequently confirmed through the computation of the celestial 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{T}\mathcal{T}$
\end_inset

 OPE by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "fotopoulos2020extended"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "donnay2021bms"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Interestingly,
 however,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "barnich2017centrally"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "distler2019double"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 observed that a nontrivial extension of the classical algebra by a generalised 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

-cocycle exists when the BMS algebra is promoted to include local,
 singular superrotations.
 This 
\emph on
suggests
\emph default
 that,
 in our model for perturbative celestial CFT based on string theory on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}\times X$
\end_inset

,
 the central charge 
\begin_inset Formula $c^{(\text{st})}$
\end_inset

 of the boundary CFT should be related to the bulk theory through the action of superrotations.
 Formulating this statement precisely,
 and using it to constrain the kinds of critical string compactifications that are consistent with the behaviour of four-dimensional flat-space bulk theories under superrotations,
 remains a long-standing goal of our programme.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Discussion
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:Discussion"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this work,
 we have introduced a novel holographic dictionary connecting 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 string theory with celestial CFT.
 Building upon the foundational ideas of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "giveon1998comments,de1999string,maldacena2001strings,maldacena2001strings2"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and drawing inspiration from the model advanced by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ogawa2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we have demonstrated that the correlation function,
 stress-energy tensor,
 Kac-Moody currents,
 and vertex operators of the celestial CFT can be systematically derived from their counterparts in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model.
 Moreover,
 by applying the results rigorously established by the mathematical physicists 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ribault2005h3+,teschner1997conformal,teschner1999mini,teschner1999structure,teschner2000operator"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we have determined the two- and three-point functions,
 as well as the structure constants of the celestial OPE.
 Additionally,
 we have derived a system of partial differential equations that characterise celestial amplitudes in the frequency-momentum space,
 and have offered a brief analysis of the infrared and ultraviolet behaviour of these amplitudes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We should point out that the holographic dictionary developed in the present manuscript is consistent with the absence of standard reflection positivity and the associated non-unitarity expected in celestial conformal field theories.
 For,
 as explained by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "gawedzki1992non"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and recently reviewed in subsection 5.2 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "murthy2026localization"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the full worldsheet-matter CFT appertaining to the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model is itself non-unitary,
 notwithstanding the positiveness of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{SL}(2,\mathbf{C})$
\end_inset

-invariant inner product underlying the Hilbert state space 
\begin_inset Formula $L^{2}(\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3},\mathsf{d}h)$
\end_inset

 describing the quantum mechanics of the zero-mode truncation of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model.
 Independently,
 in the dictionary between celestial CFT and the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model originally proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ogawa2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and developed in our work,
 the conformal dimensions 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

 associated with the celestial CFT and the scaling dimensions 
\begin_inset Formula $(h_{\mathrm{ws}},\overline{h}_{\mathrm{ws}})$
\end_inset

 belonging to the worldsheet-matter CFT of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{H}^{+}_{3}\text{-WZNW}$
\end_inset

 model are distinct physical quantities:
 for isospin 
\begin_inset Formula $j=-\frac{1}{2}+\mathtt{i}s$
\end_inset

,
 the celestial conformal dimension assigned by our dictionary is 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{j}=-2j=1-2\mathtt{i}s$
\end_inset

,
 whereas the conformal weights of the worldsheet-matter CFT are 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{\mathrm{ws}}=\overline{h}_{\mathrm{ws}}=(\frac{1}{4}+s^{2})/(k-2)$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore,
 whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $s\neq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $k>2$
\end_inset

,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{\mathrm{ws}},\overline{h}_{\mathrm{ws}}>0$
\end_inset

 but 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta_{j}$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
complex
\emph default
,
 so that these celestial conformal primaries cannot create non-zero radial physical states of finite,
 positive norm in ordinary radial quantisation with a self-adjoint dilatation operator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Over the past decades,
 string theory has provided profound insights into quantum gravity,
 inspiring us to undertake a broader research programme,
 of which this paper constitutes the inaugural contribution.
 Our objective is to investigate certain aspects of celestial holography through the framework of string theory.
 The holographic dictionary proposed herein opens multiple avenues for further research.
 Notably,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "de2023celestial,de2023eikonal"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 have explored the derivation of various aspects of celestial holography from the 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS$
\end_inset

/CFT correspondence in a series of remarkable papers.
 We believe that our proposed holographic dictionary between 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 string theory and celestial CFT could serve as a valuable toy model to support these efforts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We further recall the well-known construction of black holes in 
\begin_inset Formula $d=2+1$
\end_inset

 gravity with a negative cosmological constant,
 introduced by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "banados1992black"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 and subsequently shown by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "cangemi1993gauge"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 to arise as orbifold solutions of string theory.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "strominger1998black"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 proposed a unified framework for all ``black objects'' with near-horizon geometries described by 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

,
 encompassing the BTZ solution.
 Building on these works,
 we are optimistic that insights into black hole solutions in string theory might be employed to study black holes in asymptotically flat spaces,
 informed by the ideas presented in this paper.
 In particular,
 these insights may intersect with the constructions recently discussed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "crawley2022black"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In our forthcoming note,
 we shall investigate the connection between 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{N}=2$
\end_inset

 string theory in Klein space and celestial conformal blocks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An important problem to be addressed in a forthcoming publication concerns the description of quantum,
 namely loop-level,
 corrections to celestial amplitudes in terms of the candidate models for perturbative celestial CFTs constructed from 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}$
\end_inset

 string theory proposed in our work.
 Therefore,
 let us close our discussion by outlining the strategy that we shall follow in order to incorporate quantum-theoretic corrections to celestial amplitudes.
 In 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "mol2024partial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we derived a set of partial differential equations (PDEs) governing gluonic celestial amplitudes for a model of perturbative celestial CFT based on Liouville theory.
 Following the proposal of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "stieberger2023yang"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 this model may be referred to as celestial Liouville theory.
 These PDEs are parameterised by the Liouville coupling constant 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

,
 and we solved them perturbatively to obtain corrections to the gluonic celestial leaf amplitudes in the MHV subsector of Yang-Mills theory up to order 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}(b^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
 following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "stieberger2023celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 these logarithmic corrections were identified with corrections expected in one-loop gauge-theory amplitudes.
 We confirmed this identification by showing that the celestial OPEs for gluons obtained from celestial Liouville theory at order 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}(b^{2})$
\end_inset

 match the expected one-loop corrected celestial OPEs obtained by the method of collinear singularities,
 employed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "fan2019soft"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for extracting celestial OPEs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now,
 recall that,
 in Section III of the present manuscript,
 we derived an analogous system of PDEs governing the celestial amplitudes for the models of (perturbative) celestial CFTs proposed in our work based on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}$
\end_inset

 string theory.
 These PDEs are parameterised by a constant 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 analogous to the Liouville coupling parameter.
 Here,
 however,
 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 is determined by the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW level 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 through 
\begin_inset Formula $b^{2}=1/(k-2)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In our forthcoming publication,
 we shall demonstrate that the PDEs obtained in Section III may also be solved perturbatively,
 and that the corresponding corrections at order 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}(b^{2})$
\end_inset

 may likewise be identified with corrections associated with one-loop gauge-theory and gravity amplitudes.
 In the case of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}$
\end_inset

 string theory,
 however,
 these one-loop corrections admit a particularly compelling physical interpretation.
 Remember that,
 in the present manuscript,
 we showed that tree-level MHV celestial amplitudes for gluons and gravitons can be expressed as the helicity projection of the leading-trace,
 mini-superspace limit of correlators of appropriately dressed celestial vertex operators.
 On the other hand,
 in the mini-superspace limit 
\begin_inset Formula $k\to\infty$
\end_inset

,
 whose mathematical analysis was carried out in detail by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "teschner1999mini"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model reduces to its zero-mode sector,
 which is identified with the quantum mechanics of a point particle on 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 if the finite-
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 contributions to the correlator can be interpreted,
 at least in the MHV sector,
 as loop corrections to celestial amplitudes,
 these corrections may be understood as arising from the stringy degrees of freedom of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model.
 By contrast,
 the tree-level celestial amplitudes arise when this model reduces,
 in the mini-superspace limit,
 to the quantum mechanics of a particle on 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To conclude,
 we would like to draw attention to the works of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "StiebergerTaylor2018Strings"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Jiang2022CelestialOPEs"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "Donnay2023CelestialOpenStrings"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "KervynStieberger2025HighEnergy"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 which,
 although differing in the particular way in which string theory is employed to investigate celestial CFT,
 belong,
 in our view,
 to the same broader and also ambitious research programme of using string theory as an intellectual laboratory for the study of celestial holography.
\end_layout

