\begin_layout Section
\start_of_appendix
Mini-Introduction to 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this section,
 we develop a holographic dictionary that maps the worldsheet vertex operators and generators of worldsheet Kac-Moody and Virasoro current algebras to celestial conformal primaries,
 allowing for the construction of the celestial conformal field theory (CFT) entirely from the data provided by string theory.
 To this end,
 we begin by reviewing the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model,
 which we identify as the analytic continuation of string theory on Lorentzian 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 to its Euclidean counterpart,
 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW Model Review
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "brown1986central"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 demonstrated that any theory of  
\begin_inset Formula $3d$
\end_inset

 gravity with a negative cosmological constant is endowed with an infinite-dimensional symmetry group that includes two commuting copies of Virasoro algebras,
 thus describing a 
\begin_inset Formula $2d$
\end_inset

 CFT.
 This observation was given a concrete form in the context of 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 string theory by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "giveon1998comments,de1999string,maldacena2001strings"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In this work,
 for simplicity,
 we focus on the analytically continued Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 string theory,
 though analogous considerations apply to the 
\begin_inset Formula $SL\left(2,\mathbf{R}\right)$
\end_inset

 model studied by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "maldacena2001strings"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Classical Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(u^{0},\vec{u}\right)$
\end_inset

 be Cartesian coordinates on 
\begin_inset Formula $4d$
\end_inset

 Minkowski space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}^{1}_{3}$
\end_inset

.
 Recall that Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 can be formulated as the hypersurface 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{3}\subset\mathbf{R}^{1}_{3}$
\end_inset

 defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $-(u^{0})^{2}+\sum^{3}_{i=1}(u^{i})^{2}=-\ell^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{3}$
\end_inset

 is a submanifold of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}^{1}_{3}$
\end_inset

 with constant negative scalar curvature 
\begin_inset Formula $R=-1/\ell^{2}$
\end_inset

 and an isometry group given by 
\begin_inset Formula $SL\left(2,\mathbf{C}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 This space can be parametrised by the coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(r,\tau,\theta\right)$
\end_inset

,
 given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
u^{0}=\sqrt{\ell^{2}+r^{2}}\cosh\tau,\,\,\,u^{1}=r\sin\theta,\,\,\,u^{2}=r\cos\theta,\,\,\,u^{3}=\sqrt{\ell^{2}+r^{2}}\sinh\tau,
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in[0,2\pi)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 The first fundamental form of 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{3}$
\end_inset

,
 inherited from the ambient space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}^{1}_{3}$
\end_inset

,
 is given by the line element:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
ds^{2}=\frac{1}{1+r^{2}/\ell^{2}}dr^{2}+\ell^{2}\left(1+\frac{r^{2}}{\ell^{2}}\right)d\tau^{2}+r^{2}d\theta^{2}.\label{eq:Line-Element}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

There are two disconnected components characterising the topology of 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{3}$
\end_inset

,
 one corresponding to 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{0}>0$
\end_inset

 and the other to 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{0}<0$
\end_inset

.
 We restrict our attention to the former,
 and the resulting hypersurface,
 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

,
 satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|u^{0}\right|>u^{3}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To connect this with the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model,
 it is more convenient to introduce the coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\phi,\gamma,\bar{\gamma}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 defined as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\phi=\log\ell^{-1}\left(u^{0}+u^{3}\right),\,\,\,\gamma=\frac{u^{2}+iu^{1}}{u^{0}+u^{3}},\,\,\,\bar{\gamma}=\frac{u^{2}-iu^{1}}{u^{0}+u^{3}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In these coordinates,
 the line element becomes:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
ds^{2}=\ell^{2}\left(d\phi^{2}+e^{2\phi}d\gamma d\bar{\gamma}\right).\label{eq:Line-Element-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Notice that the boundary of Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 corresponds to 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

,
 which is homeomorphic to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{CP}^{1}$
\end_inset

,
 parametrised by 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\gamma,\bar{\gamma}\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As explained by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "maldacena1999ads3"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 the equations of motion for strings propagating in a background with the line element given by Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Line-Element-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) can only be satisfied by turning on a Neveu-Schwarz two-form field 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{B}\coloneqq\left(1/2\right)B_{\mu\nu}dx^{\mu}\wedge dx^{\nu}$
\end_inset

.
 One way to understand this requirement is from the worldsheet perspective,
 where a non-vanishing 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{B}$
\end_inset

 is necessary to maintain conformal invariance.
 It turns out,
 as shown by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "giveon1998comments,maldacena1999ads3,de1999string"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 that the required background is characterised by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{B}=\ell^{2}e^{2\phi}d\gamma\wedge d\bar{\gamma}$
\end_inset

.
 Consequently,
 the action integral for the worldsheet becomes
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In these notes,
 we follow the conventions of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "francesco1997conformal"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 regarding the integral measure on the Riemann sphere,
 such that:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
d^{2}z\coloneqq\frac{1}{2i}d\bar{z}\wedge dz.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

As a result,
 the complex delta-function is represented as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\partial_{\bar{z}}\frac{1}{z-w}=\pi\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(z-w\right),\label{eq:Delta-Function}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and the divergence theorem takes the form:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z\left(\partial_{z}v^{z}+\partial_{\bar{z}}v^{\bar{z}}\right)=\frac{1}{2i}\oint_{\partial\Sigma}\left(dzv^{\bar{z}}-d\bar{z}v^{z}\right),\label{eq:Divergence-Theorem}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma\subset\mathbf{CP}^{1}$
\end_inset

 is a simply-connected region.
\end_layout

\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
I\left[\phi,\gamma,\bar{\gamma}\right]=2\left(\frac{\ell}{\ell_{0}}\right)^{2}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z\left(\partial_{z}\phi\partial_{\bar{z}}\phi+e^{2\phi}\partial_{\bar{z}}\gamma\partial_{z}\bar{\gamma}\right).\label{eq:Worldsheet-Action}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Here,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell_{0}$
\end_inset

 denotes the fundamental string length,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell^{2}_{0}=\alpha'$
\end_inset

.
 In this manuscript,
 we follow closely the notational conventions and normalisations introduced by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "giveon1998comments"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 according to which the level 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model is related to the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}$
\end_inset

 radius by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\ell^{2}=k\,\ell^{2}_{0}\,.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Consequently,
 the large-level limit 
\begin_inset Formula $k\to\infty$
\end_inset

,
 also known as the mini-superspace limit (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "teschner1999mini"
literal "false"

\end_inset

),
 may be identified with the regime in which the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{AdS}_{3}$
\end_inset

 radius is much larger than the fundamental string length,
 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell^{2}/\ell^{2}_{0}\to\infty$
\end_inset

.
 Let us further observe that the overall numerical factors in the sigma-model action in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Worldsheet-Action"

\end_inset

) depend on the conventions used for the worldsheet measure and for 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha'$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The action in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Worldsheet-Action"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) can be identified with the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model,
 an example of a non-compact Wess-Zumino-Novikov-Witten model.
 The Lagrangian formulation of this model was developed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "gawedzki1989coset"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and later expanded by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "gawedzki1992non"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 with a focus on path-integral methods.
 The pure WZNW action integral at level-
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
S\left[H\right]=-\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z\text{tr}\left(H^{-1}\partial_{z}H\right)\left(H^{-1}\partial_{\bar{z}}H\right)+k\Gamma,\label{eq:WZNW-Action}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Gamma$
\end_inset

 is the Wess-Zumino topological term.
 Parametrising the coset space 
\begin_inset Formula $SL\left(2,\mathbf{C}\right)/SU\left(2\right)$
\end_inset

,
 which consists of complex two-by-two Hermitian matrices with unit determinant,
 by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
H\left(\phi,\gamma,\bar{\gamma}\right)=\begin{pmatrix}\left|\gamma\right|^{2}e^{\phi}+e^{-\phi} & \gamma e^{\phi}\\
e^{\phi}\bar{\gamma} & e^{\phi}
\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

one can see that the worldsheet action in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:WZNW-Action"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) is 
\emph on
classically
\emph default
 equivalent to the action in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Worldsheet-Action"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) when 
\begin_inset Formula $H\left(\phi,\gamma,\bar{\gamma}\right)$
\end_inset

 is restricted to this form.
 As 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "gawedzki1989quadrature"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 demonstrated using path-integral techniques,
 these theories remain equivalent at the quantum level.
 This identification justifies the equivalence between (bosonic) string theory on Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 and the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Quantum Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Having reviewed the classical formulation of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model,
 we now turn to the construction of the quantum state space,
 following the approach of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "teschner1999mini"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In the Schrödinger representation,
 the Hilbert space of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}=L^{2}\left(H^{+}_{3},dh\right)$
\end_inset

,
 the Lebesgue space of square-integrable complex-valued functions on the hypersurface 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

 with respect to the measure:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
dh=d\phi d^{2}\gamma,\,\,\,h=\begin{pmatrix}e^{\phi}\sqrt{1+\gamma\bar{\gamma}} & u\\
\bar{u} & e^{-\phi}\sqrt{1+\gamma\bar{\gamma}}
\end{pmatrix}.\label{eq:Measure}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We shall call the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
wavefunctions
\emph default
 of the quantum theory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We also define an action of the symmetry group 
\begin_inset Formula $SL\left(2,\mathbf{C}\right)$
\end_inset

 on wavefunctions 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi\in\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

,
 given by:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
g\in SL\left(2,\mathbf{C}\right)\mapsto T_{g}\Psi\left(h\right)=\Psi\left(g^{-1}h\left(g^{-1}\right)^{\dagger}\right),\label{eq:Action}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for each point 
\begin_inset Formula $h\in H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The inner product on the quantum state space is defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\big|\Psi_{2}\right\rangle \coloneqq\int_{H^{+}_{3}}dh\,\Psi^{*}_{2}\left(h\right)\Psi_{1}\left(h\right).\label{eq:Inner-Product}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Using the Iwasawa decomposition,
 it can be shown that for all pairs of states 
\begin_inset Formula $\Psi_{1},\Psi_{2}\in\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

,
 the inner product becomes:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\big|\Psi_{2}\right\rangle =\frac{1}{\mathcal{V}_{SU\left(2\right)}}\int_{SL\left(2,\mathbf{C}\right)}dg\,\Psi^{*}_{2}(gg^{\dagger})\Psi_{1}(gg^{\dagger}).\label{eq:Iwasawa-Decomposed-Inner-Product}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{V}_{SU\left(2\right)}$
\end_inset

 denotes the volume of 
\begin_inset Formula $SU\left(2\right)$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $dg$
\end_inset

 represents the 
\begin_inset Formula $SL\left(2,\mathbf{C}\right)$
\end_inset

-invariant measure:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
dg=\left(\frac{i}{2}\right)^{4}d^{2}\zeta_{1}d^{2}\zeta_{2}d^{2}\zeta_{3}d^{2}\zeta_{4}\,\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(\zeta_{1}\zeta_{4}-\zeta_{2}\zeta_{3}\right),\,\,\,g=\begin{pmatrix}\zeta_{1} & \zeta_{2}\\
\zeta_{3} & \zeta_{4}
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rationale behind the choice of the inner product defined in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Inner-Product"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) is that it ensures the unitary realisation of the 
\begin_inset Formula $SL\left(2,\mathbf{C}\right)$
\end_inset

 action on the space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}$
\end_inset

 of wavefunctions,
 as introduced in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Action"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 This property arises from Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Iwasawa-Decomposed-Inner-Product"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) and the invariance of the measure 
\begin_inset Formula $dg$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $SL\left(2,\mathbf{C}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 which satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $d\left(g_{0}g\right)=dg$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now introduce a key element in bridging the abstract mathematical formalism of Hilbert spaces to physics through the specification of an appropriate basis.
 In non-relativistic quantum mechanics,
 a convenient choice of basis is provided by the plane waves of definite momentum,
 which afford a clear physical interpretation.
 Analogously,
 in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model,
 there exists a counterpart to the ``plane-wave'' basis,
 represented by the so-called conformal primaries with spin 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

,
 denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j}\left(x;z\right)$
\end_inset

,
 and defined as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Phi^{j}\left(x;h\right)\coloneqq\frac{1}{\pi}\left(\begin{pmatrix}x & 1\end{pmatrix}\cdot h\cdot\begin{pmatrix}\bar{x}\\
1
\end{pmatrix}\right)^{2j},\,\,\,h\in SL\left(2;\mathbf{C}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Here,
 we adopt the normalisation introduced by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "giveon1998comments"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Furthermore,
 by parametrising 
\begin_inset Formula $h\in SL\left(2;\mathbf{C}\right)$
\end_inset

 as in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Measure"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 and treating 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi,\gamma,\bar{\gamma}$
\end_inset

 as fields depending on the points 
\begin_inset Formula $z,\bar{z}$
\end_inset

 on the worldsheet,
 we simplify the notation by writing 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j}\left(x;z\right)\coloneqq\Phi^{j}\left(x;h\left(\phi,\gamma,\bar{\gamma}\right)\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Specifically,
 
\begin_inset Formula $x,\bar{x}$
\end_inset

 will denote coordinates on the celestial sphere,
 while 
\begin_inset Formula $z,\bar{z}$
\end_inset

 will serve as coordinates on the string worldsheet.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It was demonstrated by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
after "Appendix A"
key "teschner1999mini"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 through spectral decomposition that the set of functions 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\Phi^{j}\left(x;z\right):x\in\mathbf{C};i\in-1/2+i\mathbf{R}^{+}\}$
\end_inset

 forms a complete and 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

-function normalisable basis,
 such that:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \Phi^{j}\left(x;h\right),\Phi^{j'}\left(x';h\right)\right\rangle \propto\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x-x'\right)\delta\left(j-j'\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x,\bar{x}\in\mathbf{C}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $j,j'\in-1/2+i\mathbf{R}^{+}$
\end_inset

.
 In the following subsection,
 we will review how these wavefunctions can be employed to construct a Fourier-type transform,
 further reinforcing their role as analogues of ``plane-waves'' in the quantum theory of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Symmetries
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Symmetries"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
No discussion of quantum theory would be reasonable without emphasising the role played by symmetries,
 and the case of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model is no exception.
 Following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
after "Section 2"
key "giveon1998comments"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we observe that the classical theory described by the action integral in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Worldsheet-Action"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) possesses an infinite-dimensional affine 
\begin_inset Formula $SL\left(2;\mathbf{C}\right)\times\overline{SL\left(2;\mathbf{C}\right)}$
\end_inset

 symmetry,
 the global part of which admits the following generators:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
J^{-}_{0}\coloneqq\frac{\partial}{\partial\gamma},\,\,\,J^{3}_{0}\coloneqq\gamma\frac{\partial}{\partial\gamma}-\frac{1}{2}\frac{\partial}{\partial\phi},\,\,\,J^{+}_{0}\coloneqq\gamma^{2}\frac{\partial}{\partial\gamma}-\gamma\frac{\partial}{\partial\phi}-e^{-2\phi}\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{\gamma}},\label{eq:Currents}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and similarly:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bar{J}^{-}_{0}\coloneqq\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{\gamma}},\,\,\,\bar{J}^{3}_{0}\coloneqq\bar{\gamma}\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{\gamma}}-\frac{1}{2}\frac{\partial}{\partial\phi},\,\,\,\bar{J}^{+}_{0}\coloneqq\bar{\gamma}^{2}\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{\gamma}}-\bar{\gamma}\frac{\partial}{\partial\phi}-e^{-2\phi}\frac{\partial}{\partial\gamma}.\label{eq:Currents-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

A significant class of observables in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model consists of smooth functions on the hyperboloid 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

.
 A convenient method to analyse the decomposition of these functions in terms of representations of 
\begin_inset Formula $SL\left(2;\mathbf{C}\right)\times\overline{SL\left(2;\mathbf{C}\right)}$
\end_inset

 is to introduce a pair of complex variables 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(x,\bar{x}\right)$
\end_inset

 and realise the Lie algebra of 
\begin_inset Formula $SL\left(2;\mathbf{C}\right)\times\overline{SL\left(2;\mathbf{C}\right)}$
\end_inset

 by defining the following generators as linear differential operators acting on the germ of smooth functions on 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
J^{-}_{0}=-\frac{\partial}{\partial x},\,\,\,J^{3}_{0}=-\left(x\frac{\partial}{\partial x}+p\right),\,\,\,J^{+}_{0}=-\left(x^{2}\frac{\partial}{\partial x}+2px\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bar{J}^{-}_{0}=-\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{x}},\,\,\,\bar{J}^{3}_{0}=-\left(\bar{x}\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{x}}+2\bar{p}\right),\,\,\,\bar{J}^{+}_{0}=-\left(\bar{x}^{2}\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{x}}+2\bar{p}\bar{x}\right),\label{eq:Currents-2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is related to the ``spin'' of the 
\begin_inset Formula $SL\left(2;\mathbf{C}\right)$
\end_inset

 representation by 
\begin_inset Formula $p=j+1$
\end_inset

.
 Indeed,
 it should be recalled that the value of the (quadratic) Casimir in this representation is 
\begin_inset Formula $j\left(j+1\right)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We may now draw from the observation that,
 since 
\begin_inset Formula $J^{-}_{0}=-\partial/\partial x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{J}^{-}_{0}=-\partial/\partial\bar{x}$
\end_inset

,
 every observable 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{T}\left(x,\bar{x}\right)$
\end_inset

 is conjugate to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{T}\left(0,0\right)$
\end_inset

 by the relation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{T}\left(x,\bar{x}\right)=^{-xJ^{-}_{0}-\bar{x}\bar{J}^{-}_{0}}\mathsf{T}\left(0,0\right)e^{xJ^{-}_{0}+\bar{x}\bar{J}^{-}_{0}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

leading us to define the currents:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
J^{+}\left(x;z\right)\coloneqq e^{-xJ^{-}_{0}}J^{+}\left(z\right)e^{xJ^{-}_{0}},\,J\left(x;z\right)\coloneqq e^{-xJ^{-}_{0}}J^{3}\left(z\right)e^{xJ^{-}_{0}},\,J^{-}\left(x;z\right)=e^{-xJ^{-}_{0}}J^{-}\left(z\right)e^{xJ^{-}_{0}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

As observed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "giveon1998comments"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 since these currents are related through differentiation,
 our attention may be focused on:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
J\left(x;z\right)\coloneqq2xJ^{3}\left(z\right)-J^{+}\left(z\right)-x^{2}J^{-}\left(z\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and similarly for:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bar{J}\left(\bar{x};\bar{z}\right)\coloneqq2\bar{x}\bar{J}^{3}\left(\bar{z}\right)-\bar{J}^{+}\left(\bar{z}\right)-\bar{x}^{2}\bar{J}^{-}\left(\bar{z}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the quantum theory,
 the currents in Eqs.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Currents"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Currents-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) are generators of an 
\begin_inset Formula $\widehat{SL}\left(2;\mathbf{C}\right)\times\widehat{\overline{SL}}\left(2;\mathbf{C}\right)$
\end_inset

 algebra.
 Their operator product expansions may be succinctly expressed as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
J\left(x;z\right)J\left(y;w\right)\sim k\frac{\left(y-x\right)^{2}}{\left(z-w\right)^{2}}+\frac{1}{z-w}\left[\left(y-x\right)^{2}\partial_{y}-2\left(y-x\right)\right]J\left(y;z\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
J\left(x;z\right)\Phi^{j}\left(y;w\right)\sim\frac{1}{z-w}\left[\left(y-x\right)^{2}\partial_{y}+2j\left(y-x\right)\right]\Phi^{j}\left(y;w\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

with analogous expressions holding for the conjugates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We conclude our discussion of the quantum theory of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model by noting that 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{J}\left(\bar{x};\bar{z}\right)\Phi^{j=1}\left(x;z\right)$
\end_inset

 is an observable that will appear frequently in the following subsections,
 where the conformal primary 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j=1}\left(x;z\right)$
\end_inset

 will be denoted simply as 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi\left(x;z\right)$
\end_inset

.
 The observable 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{J}\left(\bar{x};\bar{z}\right)\Phi\left(x;z\right)$
\end_inset

 satisfies the identity:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bar{J}\left(\bar{x};\bar{z}\right)\Phi\left(x;z\right)=\frac{k}{\pi}\partial_{\bar{z}}\lambda\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lambda\coloneqq-\frac{1}{\gamma-x}\frac{\left(\gamma-x\right)\left(\bar{\gamma}-\bar{x}\right)e^{2\phi}}{1+\left(\gamma-x\right)\left(\bar{\gamma}-\bar{x}\right)e^{2\phi}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Similarly,
 these objects satisfy the identity:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Phi\left(x;z\right)=\frac{1}{\pi}\partial_{\bar{x}}\lambda\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

with analogous expressions holding for the conjugates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The Mini-Superspace 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(k\rightarrow\infty\right)$
\end_inset

 Limit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We are now in a position to discuss the mini-superspace limit of the correlation functions within the framework of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model.
 This limit holds particular significance for the developments presented in these notes,
 as it is in this case that we can holographically derive the tree-level MHV scattering amplitudes for gluons and gravitons analytically continued to Klein space,
 within the framework of celestial leaf amplitudes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From a heuristic perspective,
 the results we are about to describe can be motivated as follows.
 Recall that the conformal primaries 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{j}\left(x;z\right)$
\end_inset

 in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model can be viewed as analogues of the ``plane-wave'' basis for the Hilbert space of the model.
 In this basis,
 we can define the analogue of the Fourier transform as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F\left(j\big|x,\bar{x}\right)\coloneqq\int_{H^{+}_{3}}\Phi^{j}\left(x,\bar{x};h\right)f\left(h\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

whose inverse is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f\left(h\right)=\frac{i}{\left(4\pi\right)^{3}}\int_{-\frac{1}{2}+i\mathbf{R}^{+}}dj\left(2j+1\right)^{2}\int d^{2}x\left(\Phi^{j}\left(x,\bar{x};h\right)\right)^{*}F\left(j\big|x,\bar{x}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This transformation allows us,
 in particular,
 to decompose the correlation function:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}_{n}\coloneqq\left\langle \Phi^{j_{1}}\left(x_{1};z_{1}\right)...\Phi^{j_{n}}\left(x_{n},z_{n}\right)\right\rangle ,
\end{equation}

\end_inset

into the basis 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \Phi^{j}\left(x;z\right)\right\} $
\end_inset

,
 which now becomes the focus of our attention.
 However,
 as will be discussed in more detail in Subsection 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Differential-Equations-for"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 following an important observation by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ribault2005h3+"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 in the so-called 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

-basis given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Phi^{j}\left(\mu;z\right)\coloneqq\frac{\left|\mu\right|^{2j+2}}{\pi}\int d^{2}xe^{\mu x-\bar{\mu}\bar{x}}\Phi^{j}\left(x;z\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

the Knizhnik-Zamolodchikov (KZ) equations for the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model take the following form (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ribault2005h3+"
literal "false"

\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\mathcal{F}_{n}}{\partial z_{k}}+b^{2}\sum_{k\neq\ell}\frac{\mu_{k}\mu_{\ell}}{z_{k}-z_{\ell}}\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial\mu_{k}}-\frac{\partial}{\partial\mu_{\ell}}\right)^{2}\mathcal{F}_{n}=b^{2}\sum_{k\neq\ell}\frac{\mu_{k}\mu_{\ell}}{z_{k}-z_{\ell}}\left[\frac{j_{k}\left(j_{k}+1\right)}{\mu^{2}_{k}}+\frac{j_{\ell}\left(j_{\ell}+1\right)}{\mu^{2}_{\ell}}\right]\mathcal{F}_{n},\label{eq:KZ-2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $b^{2}\coloneqq1/\left(k-2\right)$
\end_inset

.
 In the mini-superspace (
\begin_inset Formula $k\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

) limit,
 Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:KZ-2"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) simplifies to:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial\mathcal{F}_{n}}{\partial z_{k}}=0.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus,
 in the large-
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 limit,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{F}_{n}$
\end_inset

 depends solely on the ``boundary spacetime'' coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $x,\bar{x}$
\end_inset

.
 It is therefore natural to expect that,
 in this limit,
 the Fourier representation of the correlation functions will be dominated by the convolution of these ``plane waves,'' such that:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{k\rightarrow\infty}\mathcal{F}_{n}=\frac{\mathcal{N}}{2\pi^{2}}\int_{H^{+}_{3}}dh\prod^{n}_{i=1}\Phi^{j_{i}}\left(x_{i},\bar{x}_{i};h\right)=\mathcal{N}\int\frac{d\rho d\gamma d\bar{\gamma}}{\rho^{3}}\prod^{n}_{i=1}\left(\frac{\rho}{\rho^{2}+\left|x_{i}-\gamma\right|^{2}}\right)^{2j_{i}},\label{eq:Mini-Superspace-Limit}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where we define the ``radial'' coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\coloneqq e^{-\phi}$
\end_inset

.
 Consequently,
 in the 
\begin_inset Formula $k\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset

 limit,
 the correlation functions of the primaries of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model can be interpreted as a Feynman-Witten contact diagram for massless scalars propagating on Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 (cf.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "penedones2017tasi"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We shall now briefly review how one could rigorously establish these arguments,
 drawing from the observation made by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ribault2005h3+"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 that the correlation functions of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model on the sphere are related to the correlation functions of the Liouville vertex operators 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{\alpha}\left(z\right)=:e^{2\alpha\phi\left(z\right)}:$
\end_inset

,
 which include degenerate fields,
 through the following relation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \left\langle \Phi^{j_{1}}\left(\mu_{1};z_{1}\right)...\Phi^{j_{n}}\left(\mu_{n};z_{n}\right)\right\rangle \\
 & =\frac{\pi\left(-\pi\right)^{-n}}{2}b\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(\sum^{n}_{i=1}\mu_{i}\right)\left|\Theta_{n}\right|^{2}\left\langle V_{\alpha_{1}}\left(z_{1}\right)...V_{\alpha_{n}}\left(z_{n}\right)V_{-1/2b}\left(y_{1}\right)...V_{-1/2b}\left(y_{n}\right)\right\rangle ,
\end{align}

\end_inset

where the function 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta$
\end_inset

 is defined by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Theta_{n}\coloneqq u\prod_{r<s\leq n}z^{1/2b^{2}}_{rs}\prod_{k<\ell\leq n-2}y^{1/2b^{2}}_{k\ell}\prod^{n}_{r=1}\prod^{n-2}_{k=1}\frac{1}{\left(z_{r}-y_{k}\right)^{1/2b^{2}}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and the variables 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1},...,y_{n},u$
\end_inset

 are related to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu_{1},...,\mu_{n}$
\end_inset

 via the equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{u}\sum^{n}_{i=1}\frac{\mu_{i}}{t-z_{i}}=\frac{\prod^{n-2}_{j=1}\left(t-y_{j}\right)}{\prod^{n}_{i=1}\left(t-z_{i}\right)}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Using these results,
 it was demonstrated in Section 4 of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "ribault2005h3+"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 that the large-
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 asymptotics of the correlation functions in the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-WZNW model are equivalent,
 up to a proportionality constant that we shall denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{N}$
\end_inset

,
 to the semiclassical limit 
\begin_inset Formula $b\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

 of the Liouville correlation functions.
 Furthermore,
 recalling from Appendix A of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "melton2024celestial"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 that the semiclassical limit of the Liouville vertex operators 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{2\sigma_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 ...,
 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{2\sigma_{n}}\left(x_{n}\right)$
\end_inset

 can be represented as a contact Feynman-Witten diagram for massless scalars on 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{b\rightarrow0^{+}}\left\langle V_{2\sigma_{1}}\left(x_{1}\right)...V_{2\sigma_{n}}\left(x_{n}\right)\right\rangle \propto\int_{AdS_{3}/\mathbf{Z}}d^{3}\hat{y}\frac{\Gamma\left(2\sigma_{i}\right)}{\left(\varepsilon-iq\left(x_{i},\bar{x}_{i}\right)\cdot\hat{y}\right)},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

we see that Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Mini-Superspace-Limit"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) follows from these considerations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Kac-Moody Current Algebras
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:First-Entry:-Kac-Moody"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We are now prepared to present the first entry of our holographic dictionary.
 Our objective is to describe the construction of Kac-Moody currents within the celestial CFT–henceforth referred to as 
\emph on
celestial Kac-Moody current algebras
\emph default
–derived from worldsheet Kac-Moody currents associated with gauge symmetries.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma$
\end_inset

 denote the worldsheet of a bosonic string embedded in the hyperboloid 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

 (times an arbitrary compact manifold 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{N}$
\end_inset

),
 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 be a Lie group with structure constants 
\begin_inset Formula $if^{abc}$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose we are given a set of generators 
\begin_inset Formula $j^{a}\left(z\right)$
\end_inset

 of a 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 level-
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{k}_{G}$
\end_inset

 Kac-Moody current algebra on the worldsheet 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma$
\end_inset

,
 satisfying the operator product expansion:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
j^{a}\left(z\right)j^{b}\left(w\right)\sim\frac{\hat{k}_{G}\delta^{ab}}{\left(z-w\right)^{2}}+\frac{if^{abc}j^{c}\left(w\right)}{z-w}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We recall from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "polchinski2001string"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 that,
 given such a set of worldsheet currents 
\begin_inset Formula $j^{a}\left(z\right)$
\end_inset

 obeying the above OPE,
 one obtains,
 in string theory,
 a gauge field 
\begin_inset Formula $A^{a}_{\mu}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 in the target space,
 whose associated vertex operator takes the form 
\begin_inset Formula $\int d^{2}zj^{a}\left(z\right)A^{a}_{\mu}\left(x\right)\partial_{\bar{z}}X^{\mu}\left(z,\bar{z}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $X^{\mu}\left(z,\bar{z}\right)$
\end_inset

 represents the embedding of the string in the target space.
 The gauge field transforms under infinitesimal gauge transformations as 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta A^{a}_{\mu}\left(x\right)=\partial_{\mu}\lambda^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
 a pure gauge field is described by a vertex operator of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $\int d^{2}zj^{a}\left(z\right)\partial_{\bar{z}}\lambda^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However,
 as is well known from the study of asymptotic symmetries and the infrared structure of gauge theories (cf.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "strominger2018lectures"
literal "false"

\end_inset

),
 an important class of vertex operators emerges from pure gauge configurations whose gauge function 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 does not have compact support in the target space.
 In the present context,
 this implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 does not vanish at the boundary of 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

,
 thereby generating an algebra of large gauge transformations.
 (Of course,
 in string theory,
 one must also impose worldsheet consistency conditions,
 e.g.,
 the integrands in the vertex operators must be primary under both left- and right-moving Virasoro symmetries.
 Furthermore,
 when the target space is flat,
 the gauge field must satisfy the gauge condition 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{\mu}A^{\mu a}=0$
\end_inset

,
 in addition to the massless Klein-Gordon equation 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{\mu}\partial^{\mu}A^{a}_{\nu}=0$
\end_inset

.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus,
 we are led to define the generators of the celestial Kac-Moody currents via the following vertex operators:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)\coloneqq-\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z\,j^{a}\left(z\right)\partial_{\bar{z}}\lambda\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right).\label{eq:Celestial-Kac-Moody-Current}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)$
\end_inset

 be a worldsheet operator depending on the celestial coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $x,\bar{x}\in\mathbf{CP}^{1}$
\end_inset

,
 transforming in a representation 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 with weight 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

,
 such that:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
j^{a}\left(z\right)\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(x,\bar{x};w,\bar{w}\right)\sim\frac{1}{z-w}t^{a}\left(R\right)\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(x,\bar{x};w,\bar{w}\right),\label{eq:OPE}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $t^{a}\left(R\right)$
\end_inset

 is the matrix associated with the representation under which 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{W}^{R,h}$
\end_inset

 transforms.
 To verify the consistency of our definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

,
 we must ensure it satisfies the celestial current algebra Ward identities:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(y,\bar{y};z,\bar{z}\right)\sim\frac{1}{x-y}t^{a}\left(R\right)\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right),\label{eq:Ward}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)\mathcal{O}^{b}\left(y\right)\sim\frac{\hat{k}_{G}\delta^{ab}}{\left(x-y\right)^{2}}+\frac{if^{abc}\mathcal{O}^{c}\left(y\right)}{x-y}.\label{eq:Ward-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We begin our discussion of the Ward identities with Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Ward"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 which is useful for introducing our regularisation scheme,
 ensuring that the celestial correlation functions are well-defined and finite.
 Using Eqs.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Celestial-Kac-Moody-Current"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:OPE"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 we arrive at the following expression for the correlation function of the operators 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(y,\bar{y}\right)$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
 & \left\langle \mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(y,\bar{y}\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}\\
 & =-\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z_{1}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z\,\frac{t^{a}\left(R\right)}{z-z_{1}}\left\langle \partial_{\bar{z}}\lambda\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(y,\bar{y};z_{1},\bar{z_{1}}\right)...\right\rangle .
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using integration by parts with respect to the anti-holomorphic variable 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{z}$
\end_inset

,
 and substituting the identity given by Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Delta-Function"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 we can rewrite the celestial correlation function as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \left\langle \mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(y,\bar{y}\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}\\
 & =-\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z_{1}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z\,\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{z}}\left(\frac{t^{a}\left(R\right)}{z-w}\left\langle \lambda\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(y,\bar{y};z_{1},\bar{z}_{1}\right)...\right\rangle \right)\\
 & +\lim_{z\rightarrow z_{1}}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z_{1}\,\left\langle \lambda\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(y,\bar{y};z_{1},\bar{z}_{1}\right)\right\rangle .
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As 
\begin_inset Formula $z\rightarrow z_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 the last term in this expression diverges,
 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{W}^{R,h}$
\end_inset

 are evaluated at the same insertion on the worldsheet.
 To address this,
 we introduce the following regularisation scheme:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \left\langle \mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(y,\bar{y}\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}\\
 & \stackrel{\text{reg.}}{\longrightarrow}-\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z_{1}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z\,\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{z}}\left(\frac{t^{a}\left(R\right)}{z-w}\left\langle \lambda\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(y,\bar{y};z_{1},\bar{z}_{1}\right)...\right\rangle \right).
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon>0$
\end_inset

 be a small real number,
 and split the integration over 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z=\int_{\Sigma\backslash D_{\varepsilon}\left(z_{1}\right)}d^{2}z+\int_{D_{\varepsilon}\left(z_{1}\right)}d^{2}z$
\end_inset

.
 As 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon\rightarrow0^{+}$
\end_inset

,
 applying the complex divergence theorem (see Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Divergence-Theorem"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)) yields:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \left\langle \mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(y,\bar{y}\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}\\
 & =\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z_{1}\oint_{C_{\varepsilon}\left(z_{1}\right)}\frac{dz}{2\pi i}\left\langle j^{a}\left(z\right)\lambda\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(y,\bar{y};z_{1},\bar{z}_{1}\right)...\right\rangle .
\end{align}

\end_inset

Here,
 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{\varepsilon}\left(z_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

 is a contour of radius 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

 centred at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}\in\Sigma$
\end_inset

.
 The sign change arises because,
 upon removing 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{\varepsilon}\left(z_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma$
\end_inset

,
 the contour integral traverses 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{\varepsilon}\left(z_{1}\right)$
\end_inset

 in the opposite orientation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By repeating this procedure iteratively for all worldsheet operator insertions at 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1},...,z_{n}$
\end_inset

,
 we obtain the following Cauchy integral representation of the celestial current 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 around all vertices attached to the worldsheet:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)=\sum^{n}_{i=1}\oint_{C_{\varepsilon}\left(z_{i}\right)}\frac{dz}{2\pi i}j^{a}\left(z\right)\lambda\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark*
The celestial correlation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\langle\mathcal{O}^{a}(x)\mathsf{W}^{R,h}(y,\bar{y})...\rangle_{\text{CCFT}}$
\end_inset

 also admits another integral representation that offers insight into the geometric nature of the regularisation scheme.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Sigma}$
\end_inset

 be the Riemann surface obtained from 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma$
\end_inset

 by removing small disks around all vertex operator insertions.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $j^{a}\left(z\right)$
\end_inset

 is a holomorphic current on 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\Sigma}$
\end_inset

,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{\bar{z}}j^{a}\left(z\right)\big|_{\hat{\Sigma}}\equiv0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
 & \left\langle \mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(y,\bar{y}\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}\\
 & =-\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z_{1}\int_{\hat{\Sigma}}d^{2}z\,\left\langle j^{a}\left(z\right)\partial_{\bar{z}}\lambda\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\mathsf{W}^{R,h}\left(y,\bar{y};z_{1},\bar{z}_{1}\right)...\right\rangle .
\end{align*}

\end_inset

Thus,
 the regularisation scheme can be understood as the removal of small discs centred around each operator insertion point on the worldsheet,
 which allow us to express the celestial current 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)=-\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{\hat{\Sigma}}d^{2}z\,\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{z}}\left[j^{a}\left(z\right)\lambda\left(z,\bar{z};x,\bar{x}\right)\right].
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We are now prepared to demonstrate that the celestial Kac-Moody current 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 satisfies the Ward identity,
 Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Ward-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 This can be established by analysing the behaviour of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 within a correlation function involving another operator 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}^{b}\left(y\right)$
\end_inset

.
 A more straightforward approach involves taking the partial derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

 with respect to the anti-holomorphic variable 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

,
 yielding:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\partial_{\bar{x}}\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)=\oint\frac{dz}{2i}j^{a}\left(z\right)\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right),\label{eq:Partial-Derivative-Kac-Moody-Current}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which follows from the identity 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{\bar{x}}\lambda=\pi\Phi$
\end_inset

.
 Consequently,
 we find:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \left\langle \partial_{\bar{x}}\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)\mathcal{O}^{b}\left(y\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}\\
 & =-\frac{1}{k}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}w\oint_{C_{\varepsilon}\left(z_{1}\right)}\frac{dz}{2i}\left(\frac{\hat{k}_{G}\delta^{ab}}{\left(z-w\right)^{2}}+\frac{if^{abc}j^{c}\left(z\right)}{z-w}\right)\left\langle \Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)...\right\rangle .\label{eq:OO-Correlation-Function}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two distinct contributions arise from the single pole in the above correlation function.
 The first originates from the OPE 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi\Phi\bar{J}$
\end_inset

,
 and the second from 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{J}\Phi\Phi$
\end_inset

.
 To compute the first,
 we use the asymptotic expansion:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{z\rightarrow w}\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)\sim\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x-y\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right),\label{eq:Asymptotics}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

from which we derive:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \lim_{z\rightarrow w}\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)\bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right)\\
 & \sim\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x-y\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)\bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right)+\left(z-w\right)\lim_{z'\rightarrow w}\partial_{z'}\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z',\bar{z}'\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)\bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right).
\end{align}

\end_inset

To compute the second contribution,
 recall (cf.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
after "Eq. (2.25)"
key "giveon1998comments"
literal "false"

\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right)\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)=\frac{1}{\bar{w}-\bar{z}}\left[\left(\bar{x}-\bar{y}\right)^{2}\partial_{\bar{x}}+2\left(\bar{x}-\bar{y}\right)\right]\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so that Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Asymptotics"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) implies:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \lim_{z\rightarrow w}\bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right)\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)\\
 & \sim\frac{1}{\bar{w}-\bar{z}}\left[\left(\bar{x}-\bar{y}\right)^{2}\partial_{\bar{x}}+2\left(\bar{x}-\bar{y}\right)\right]\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x-y\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)=0.
\end{align}

\end_inset

Thus,
 the contribution to 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle \partial_{\bar{x}}\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)\mathcal{O}^{b}\left(y\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}$
\end_inset

 from the single pole in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:OO-Correlation-Function"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & -\frac{if^{abc}}{k}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}w\oint_{C_{\varepsilon\left(w\right)}}\frac{dz}{2i}\left\langle \frac{j^{c}\left(w\right)}{z-w}\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x-y\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)\bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right)...\right\rangle \\
 & =\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{x}}\left(\frac{if^{abc}}{x-y}\left\langle \mathcal{O}^{c}\left(y\right)...\right\rangle \right)\label{eq:Step}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To compute the contribution from the double pole,
 we first integrate by parts with respect to the holomorphic variable 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
 & -\frac{1}{k}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}w\oint_{C_{\varepsilon}\left(w\right)}\frac{dz}{2i}\frac{\hat{k}_{G}\delta^{ab}}{\left(z-w\right)^{2}}\left\langle \bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right)\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)...\right\rangle \\
 & =-\frac{\hat{k}_{G}\delta^{ab}}{k}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}w\oint_{C_{\varepsilon}\left(w\right)}\frac{dz}{2i}\frac{1}{z-w}\left\langle \bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right)\partial_{z}\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)...\right\rangle \\
 & =-\frac{\pi\hat{k}_{G}\delta^{ab}}{k}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}w\lim_{z\rightarrow w}\left\langle \bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right)\partial_{z}\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)...\right\rangle .
\end{align*}

\end_inset

We recall that the limit 
\begin_inset Formula $z\rightarrow w$
\end_inset

 arises because 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon$
\end_inset

 is the radius of the small circle 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{\varepsilon}\left(w\right)$
\end_inset

 enclosing the insertion point 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 on the worldsheet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using the identities from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "giveon1998comments"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 namely 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{z}\bar{\lambda}=\left(\pi/k\right)J\Phi$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{x}\bar{\lambda}=\pi\Phi$
\end_inset

,
 we find the following result:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \lim_{z\rightarrow w}\left\langle \bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right)\partial_{z}\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)...\right\rangle \\
 & =\frac{1}{\pi}\lim_{z\rightarrow w}\left\langle \bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right)\partial_{z}\partial_{x}\bar{\lambda}\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)...\right\rangle \\
 & =\frac{1}{k}\lim_{z\rightarrow w}\left\langle \bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right)\partial_{x}\left[J\left(x;z\right)\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\right]\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)...\right\rangle \\
 & \sim\frac{1}{k}\partial_{x}\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x-y\right)\left\langle J\left(y;w\right)\bar{J}\left(\bar{y};\bar{w}\right)\Phi\left(y,\bar{y};w,\bar{w}\right)...\right\rangle .
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Defining a new operator 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}$
\end_inset

 as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}\coloneqq\frac{1}{k^{2}}\int d^{2}z\,J\left(x;z\right)\bar{J}\left(\bar{x};\bar{z}\right)\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right),\label{eq:Central-Extension}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

the contribution from the double pole to 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle \partial_{\bar{x}}\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)\mathcal{O}^{b}\left(y\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}$
\end_inset

 can be expressed as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{x}}\left(\frac{\hat{k}_{G}\delta^{ab}}{\left(x-y\right)^{2}}\left\langle \mathcal{I}...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}\right).\label{eq:Step-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 by combining Eqs.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 we arrive at:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \partial_{\bar{x}}\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)\mathcal{O}^{b}\left(y\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}}=\frac{\partial}{\partial\bar{x}}\left\langle \left(\frac{if^{abc}}{x-y}\mathcal{O}^{c}\left(y\right)+\frac{\hat{k}_{G}\mathcal{I}\delta^{ab}}{\left(x-y\right)^{2}}\right)...\right\rangle _{\text{CCFT}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which,
 upon integration with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

,
 gives the celestial Ward identity:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)\mathcal{O}^{b}\left(y\right)\sim\frac{\hat{k}_{G}\mathcal{I}\delta^{ab}}{\left(x-y\right)^{2}}+\frac{if^{abc}\mathcal{O}^{c}\left(y\right)}{x-y}.\label{eq:Ward-5}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We are led to the conclusion that the operator 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}$
\end_inset

 represents the central extension of the celestial Kac-Moody current algebra generated by 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}^{a}\left(x\right)$
\end_inset

.
 To establish that the definition of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}$
\end_inset

,
 as given in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Central-Extension"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 is consistent,
 it is necessary to verify that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}$
\end_inset

 is independent of the holomorphic 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and anti-holomorphic 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}$
\end_inset

 celestial coordinates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In fact,
 employing the identities:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\partial_{x}\bar{\lambda}=\pi\Phi,\,\,\,J\Phi=\frac{k}{\pi}\partial_{z}\bar{\lambda},\,\,\,\bar{J}\Phi=\frac{k}{\pi}\partial_{\bar{z}}\lambda,
\end{equation}

\end_inset

established in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
after "Sec. 2"
key "giveon1998comments"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we deduce the following result:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\partial_{x}\mathcal{I} & =\frac{1}{k^{2}}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z\,\bar{J}\left(\bar{x};\bar{z}\right)\partial_{x}\left[J\left(x;z\right)\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\right]=\frac{1}{\pi k}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z\,\bar{J}\left(\bar{x};\bar{z}\right)\partial_{x}\partial_{z}\bar{\lambda}\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\\
 & =\frac{1}{k}\int_{\Sigma}d^{2}z\,\partial_{z}\left[\bar{J}\left(\bar{x};\bar{z}\right)\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\right]=-\frac{1}{2ik}\oint d\bar{z}\,\bar{J}\left(\bar{x};\bar{z}\right)\Phi\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)\\
 & =-\oint\frac{d\bar{z}}{2\pi i}\partial_{\bar{z}}\lambda\left(x,\bar{x};z,\bar{z}\right)=0,
\end{align}

\end_inset

as claimed.
 This verifies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}$
\end_inset

 is indeed independent of the coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $x,\bar{x}$
\end_inset

,
 confirming its consistency as a central extension.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Worldsheet Integrals
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Worldsheet Integrals Related to the 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

-Point Function
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Worldsheet-Integrals-Related"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this Appendix,
 we undertake the computation of the integral over the worldsheet in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Integral-3"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 which is important for analysing the celestial 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

-point function discussed in Subsection 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Two-point-function"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 We shall proceed with detail,
 as this calculation exemplifies the basic reasoning employed to derive the worldsheet integral relations that emerge in the computations of the 
\begin_inset Formula $3$
\end_inset

-point function in Subsection 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Three-Point-Function"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 and the structure constants of the celestial OPE in Subsection 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Operator-Product-Expansion"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 Additionally,
 we shall derive the distributional limit of the resulting integral,
 thereby establishing the known 
\begin_inset Formula $2d$
\end_inset

 CFT identity (cf,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "simmons2014projectors,ketov1995conformal,vladimirov1971equations"
literal "false"

\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int d^{2}y\frac{1}{\left|x_{1}-y\right|^{2\tau}\left|y-x_{2}\right|^{2\left(2-\tau\right)}}=\frac{4\pi^{2}}{\nu^{2}}\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{1}\eqqcolon1+i\nu$
\end_inset

,
 which shall prove helpful in the derivations within Subsection 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Two-point-function"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 while also serving as a consistency check of our results.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We define the principal integral of interest in this Appendix as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{0}\left(\tau_{1},\tau_{2}\big|x_{1},x_{2}\right)\coloneqq\int d^{2}y\frac{1}{\left|x_{1}-y\right|^{2\tau_{1}}\left|y-x_{2}\right|^{2\tau_{2}}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We begin by simplifying 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}_{0}$
\end_inset

 through the translation 
\begin_inset Formula $y\mapsto\xi\coloneqq y-x_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 yielding:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{0}=\int d^{2}\xi\frac{1}{\left|\left(x_{2}-x_{1}\right)-\xi\right|^{2\tau_{1}}\left|\xi\right|^{2\tau_{2}}}.\label{eq:Step-13}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We proceed by employing the method of Feynman 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

-parameters.
 For a modern exposition,
 we refer the reader to 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "smirnov2004evaluating"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "weinzierl2022feynman"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Parametrising the integrand in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-13"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) gives:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\left|\left(x_{2}-x_{1}\right)-\xi\right|^{2\tau_{1}}}=\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\tau_{1}\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{1}\alpha^{\tau_{1}-1}_{1}e^{-\alpha_{1}\left|\left(x_{2}-x_{1}\right)-\xi\right|^{2}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\left|\xi\right|^{2\tau_{2}}}=\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\tau_{2}\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{2}\alpha^{\tau_{2}-1}_{2}e^{-\alpha_{2}\left|\xi\right|^{2}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Substituting these into Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-13"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) and denoting 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}-x_{1}\eqqcolon u_{0}+iv_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi\eqqcolon u+iv$
\end_inset

,
 we obtain:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{0}=\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\tau_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\tau_{2}\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{1}\alpha^{\tau_{1}-1}_{1}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{2}\alpha^{\tau_{2}-1}_{2}e^{-\alpha_{1}\left(u^{2}_{0}+v^{2}_{0}\right)}\mathcal{G}\left(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2}\right),\label{eq:Step-14}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the Gaussian integral 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{G}\left(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 is defined as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{G}\left(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2}\right)\coloneqq\int dudve^{-\left(\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}\right)u^{2}+2\alpha_{1}u_{0}u}e^{-\left(\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}\right)v^{2}+2\alpha_{1}v_{0}v}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Evaluating the Gaussian integral yields:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{G}=\frac{\pi}{\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}}e^{\left(u^{2}_{0}+v^{2}_{0}\right)\frac{\alpha^{2}_{1}}{\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus,
 Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-14"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) becomes:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{0}=\frac{\pi^{2}}{\Gamma\left(\tau_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\tau_{2}\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{1}\alpha^{\tau_{1}-1}_{1}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{2}\alpha^{\tau_{2}-1}_{2}\frac{1}{\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}}e^{-\left(u^{2}_{0}+v^{2}_{0}\right)\alpha_{1}}e^{\left(u^{2}_{0}+v^{2}_{0}\right)\frac{\alpha^{2}_{1}}{\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}}}.\label{eq:Step-15}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

To evaluate the integrals over 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 we introduce the variables 
\begin_inset Formula $t\coloneqq\alpha_{1}/\left(\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s\coloneqq\left(u^{2}_{0}+v^{2}_{0}\right)\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Recalling that 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{2}_{0}+v^{2}_{0}=\left|x_{1}-x_{2}\right|^{2}$
\end_inset

,
 we reorganise Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-15"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{0}=\frac{\pi}{\Gamma\left(\tau_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\tau_{2}\right)}\frac{1}{\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\left(\tau_{1}+\tau_{2}-1\right)}}\int^{\infty}_{0}dss^{\left(\tau_{1}+\tau_{2}-1\right)-1}\int^{1}_{0}dtt^{-\tau_{2}}\left(1-t\right)^{\tau_{2}-1}e^{-\left(1-t\right)s}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Finally,
 applying the rescaling 
\begin_inset Formula $s\mapsto r\coloneqq\left(1-t\right)s$
\end_inset

,
 we obtain:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{0}=\frac{\pi}{\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\left(\tau_{1}+\tau_{2}-1\right)}}\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\tau_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\tau_{2}\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}drr^{\left(\tau_{1}+\tau_{2}-1\right)-1}e^{-r}\int^{1}_{0}dtt^{-\tau_{2}}\left(1-t\right)^{-\tau_{1}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

leading to the final expression:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{0}=\frac{\pi}{\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\left(\tau_{1}+\tau_{2}-1\right)}}\frac{\Gamma\left(1-\tau_{1}\right)}{\Gamma\left(\tau_{1}\right)}\frac{\Gamma\left(1-\tau_{2}\right)}{\Gamma\left(\tau_{2}\right)}\frac{\Gamma\left(\tau_{1}+\tau_{2}-1\right)}{\Gamma\left(2-\tau_{1}-\tau_{2}\right)}.\label{eq:Integral-2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This expression must be interpreted in the sense of distributions;
 that is,
 it should be understood as making sense when applied to a test function.
 To clarify this concept and derive a useful 
\begin_inset Formula $2d$
\end_inset

 CFT identity,
 which will be employed in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Two-point-function"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 we now utilise Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Integral-2"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) to compute the limit 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{1}+\tau_{2}\rightarrow2$
\end_inset

 in the distributional sense.
 We begin by observing the following relation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(2-\tau_{1}-\tau_{2}\right)}=\frac{2-\tau_{1}-\tau_{2}}{\Gamma\left(3-\tau_{1}-\tau_{2}\right)},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which allows Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Integral-2"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) to be rearranged in the form:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \frac{\Gamma\left(\tau_{1}\right)}{\Gamma\left(1-\tau_{1}\right)}\frac{\Gamma\left(\tau_{2}\right)}{\Gamma\left(1-\tau_{2}\right)}\int d^{2}y\frac{1}{\left|x_{1}-y\right|^{2\tau_{1}}\left|y-x_{2}\right|^{2\tau_{2}}}\\
 & =2\pi\left(\frac{1}{2}\left(2-\tau_{1}-\tau_{2}\right)\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\left(1-\tau_{1}-\tau_{2}\right)}\right)\frac{\Gamma\left(\tau_{1}+\tau_{2}-1\right)}{\Gamma\left(3-\tau_{1}-\tau_{2}\right)}.
\end{align}

\end_inset

Introducing 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon\coloneqq2-\tau_{1}-\tau_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{1}\eqqcolon1+i\nu$
\end_inset

,
 we observe that:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\Gamma\left(\tau_{2}\right)}{\Gamma\left(1-\tau_{2}\right)}=-\left(\tau_{1}-1\right)\left(\tau_{1}+\varepsilon-1\right)\frac{\Gamma\left(1-\tau_{1}\right)}{\Gamma\left(\tau_{1}+\varepsilon\right)},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

yielding:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\Gamma\left(\tau_{1}\right)}{\Gamma\left(\tau_{1}+\varepsilon\right)}\int d^{2}y\frac{1}{\left|x_{1}-y\right|^{2\tau_{1}}\left|y-x_{2}\right|^{2\tau_{2}}}=\frac{2\pi}{\nu\left(\nu-i\varepsilon\right)}\left(\frac{1}{2}\varepsilon\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\left(\varepsilon-1\right)}\right)\frac{\Gamma\left(1-\varepsilon\right)}{\Gamma\left(1+\varepsilon\right)}.\label{eq:Step-16}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Recalling that (cf.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "vladimirov1971equations"
literal "false"

\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lim_{\varepsilon\rightarrow0^{+}}\frac{1}{2}\varepsilon\left|x_{12}\right|^{2\left(\varepsilon-1\right)}=\delta\left(\left|x_{12}\right|^{2}\right)=\delta\left(x_{12}\right)\delta\left(\bar{x}_{12}\right)=2\pi\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

we find that taking the limit 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon\rightarrow0^{+}$
\end_inset

 of Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-16"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) finally yields:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int d^{2}y\frac{1}{\left|x_{1}-y\right|^{2\tau}\left|y-x_{2}\right|^{2\left(2-\tau\right)}}=\frac{4\pi^{2}}{\nu^{2}}\delta^{\left(2\right)}\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right).\label{eq:Integral-5}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Generalised Dirac Delta Function on the Worldsheet
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Generalised-Dirac-Delta"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this Appendix,
 we recall how the generalised Dirac delta ``function,'' analytically continued in the complex plane,
 naturally arises in the context of worldsheet integration.
 This result proves useful in deriving the celestial 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

-point function,
 as described by our holographic dictionary in Subsection 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Two-point-function"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

,
 particularly Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Identity-4"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

).
 Moreover,
 it serves to illustrate some fundamental methods of worldsheet integration.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We begin by considering the integral defined as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}\left(\beta\big|x_{1},x_{2}\right)\coloneqq\frac{1}{N}\int d^{2}z_{1}d^{2}z_{2}\frac{1}{\left|z_{1}-z_{2}\right|^{2\beta}},\label{eq:Step-17}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 denotes the worldsheet ``volume,'' a regulator necessary for the integral measure.
 The method of Feynman 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

-parameters instructs us to introduce the following representation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\left|z_{1}-z_{2}\right|^{2\beta}}=\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\beta\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha\alpha^{\beta-1}e^{-\alpha\left|z_{1}-z_{2}\right|^{2}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so that Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-17"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) can be rewritten as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}=\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\beta\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha\alpha^{\beta-1}\frac{1}{N}\int d^{2}z_{1}d^{2}z_{2}e^{-\alpha\left|z_{1}-z_{2}\right|^{2}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

To perform the complex integrals,
 we introduce the parameterisations 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}\coloneqq x_{1}+iy_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{2}\coloneqq x_{2}+iy_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 leading to:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}=\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\beta\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha\alpha^{\beta-1}\frac{1}{N}\int dx_{1}dx_{2}e^{-\alpha\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)^{2}}\int dy_{1}dy_{2}e^{-\alpha\left(y_{1}-y_{2}\right)^{2}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which can be reorganised as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}=\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\beta\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha\alpha^{\beta-1}\frac{1}{N}\left(\mathcal{G}\left(\alpha\right)\right)^{2},\label{eq:Step-18}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the Gaussian integral is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{G}\left(\alpha\right)\coloneqq\int dx_{1}dx_{2}e^{-\alpha\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)^{2}}=\frac{\sqrt{\pi N}}{\alpha^{1/2}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus,
 Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-18"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) yields:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}=\frac{\pi}{4\Gamma\left(\beta\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha\alpha^{\left(\beta-1\right)-1}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Following 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "donnay2020asymptotic"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 this result can be expressed using the analytic continuation of the Dirac delta function (cf.
 Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Dirac-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)) as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(i\left(\Delta-z\right)\right)\coloneqq\frac{1}{2\pi}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\tau\,\tau^{\Delta-z-1},
\]

\end_inset

so that our final result reads:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{N}\int d^{2}z_{1}d^{2}z_{2}\frac{1}{\left|z_{1}-z_{2}\right|^{2\beta}}=\frac{\pi^{2}}{2}\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(\beta-1\right).\label{eq:Integral-4}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Triple Worldsheet Integral
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Triple-Worldsheet-Integral"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this Appendix,
 we shall compute the worldsheet integral that arises in the calculation of the 
\begin_inset Formula $3$
\end_inset

-point function of Euclidean 
\begin_inset Formula $AdS_{3}$
\end_inset

 bosonic string theory,
 as required for the continuation of the computations in Subsection 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Triple-Worldsheet-Integral"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2},\sigma_{3}$
\end_inset

 represent a sequence of exponents,
 and define the triple complex integral 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}\left(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2},\sigma_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 as follows:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}\left(\sigma_{1},\sigma_{2},\sigma_{3}\right)\coloneqq\frac{1}{N}\int d^{2}z_{1}\int d^{2}z_{2}\int d^{2}z_{3}\frac{1}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{2\sigma_{3}}\left|z_{23}\right|^{2\sigma_{1}}\left|z_{31}\right|^{2\sigma_{2}}},\label{eq:Definition}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 denotes the worldsheet area.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To evaluate this integral,
 we will employ the method of Feynman 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

-parameters,
 as reviewed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "smirnov2004evaluating,weinzierl2022feynman"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Beginning with the expressions for the distance between points on the complex plane,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}\eqqcolon x_{1}+iy_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{2}\eqqcolon x_{2}+iy_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{3}\eqqcolon x_{3}+iy_{3}$
\end_inset

.
 Consequently,
 we express the terms in the integrand as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{2\sigma_{3}}}=\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\sigma_{3}\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{3}\alpha^{\sigma_{3}-1}_{3}e^{-\alpha_{3}\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)^{2}-\alpha_{3}\left(y_{1}-y_{2}\right)^{2}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\left|z_{23}\right|^{2\sigma_{1}}}=\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\sigma_{1}\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{1}\alpha^{\sigma_{1}-1}_{1}e^{-\alpha_{1}\left(x_{2}-x_{3}\right)^{2}-\alpha_{1}\left(y_{2}-y_{3}\right)^{2}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\left|z_{31}\right|^{2\sigma_{2}}}=\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\sigma_{2}\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{2}\alpha^{\sigma_{2}-1}_{2}e^{-\alpha_{2}\left(x_{3}-x_{1}\right)^{2}-\alpha_{2}\left(y_{3}-y_{1}\right)^{2}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The integral in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Definition"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) can now be expanded as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
 & \mathcal{I}=\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\sigma_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{2}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{3}\right)}\frac{1}{N}\int dx_{1}dx_{2}dx_{3}\int dy_{1}dy_{2}dy_{3}\prod^{3}_{i=1}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{i}\alpha^{\sigma_{i}-1}_{i}\\
 & e^{-\alpha_{1}\left(x_{2}-x_{3}\right)^{2}-\alpha_{2}\left(x_{3}-x_{1}\right)^{2}-\alpha_{3}\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)^{2}}e^{-\alpha_{1}\left(y_{2}-y_{3}\right)^{2}-\alpha_{2}\left(y_{3}-y_{1}\right)^{2}-\alpha_{3}\left(y_{1}-y_{2}\right)^{2}}.
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our next goal is to reorganise this integral into the 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

-parameters and the worldsheet coordinates.
 We rewrite the integral as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}=\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\sigma_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{2}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{3}\right)}\frac{1}{N}\prod^{3}_{i=1}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{i}\alpha^{\sigma_{i}-1}_{i}\left(F\left(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\alpha_{3}\right)\right)^{2},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F\left(\alpha_{1},\alpha_{2},\alpha_{3}\right)\coloneqq\int dx_{1}dx_{2}dx_{3}e^{-\alpha_{1}\left(x_{2}-x_{3}\right)^{2}-\alpha_{2}\left(x_{3}-x_{1}\right)^{2}-\alpha_{3}\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\right)^{2}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Performing the Gaussian integrals,
 we find that:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F=\frac{\pi\sqrt{V}}{\left(\alpha_{1}\alpha_{2}+\alpha_{2}\alpha_{3}+\alpha_{3}\alpha_{1}\right)^{1/2}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus,
 the integral 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}$
\end_inset

 becomes:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}=\frac{\pi^{2}}{\Gamma\left(\sigma_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{2}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{3}\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{3}\alpha^{\sigma_{3}-1}_{3}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{2}\alpha^{\sigma_{2}-1}_{2}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{1}\frac{\alpha^{\sigma_{1}-1}_{1}}{\alpha_{1}\left(\alpha_{2}+\alpha_{3}\right)+\alpha_{2}\alpha_{3}}.\label{eq:Step-6}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We employ the following identity from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "gradshteyn2014table"
literal "false"

\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha\,\frac{\alpha^{\mu-1}}{\left(1+\beta\alpha\right)^{\nu}}=\beta^{-\mu}B\left(\mu,\nu-\mu\right),\label{eq:Identity-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which allows us to evaluate the integral over 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 yielding:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{1}\frac{\alpha^{\sigma_{1}-1}_{1}}{\alpha_{1}\left(\alpha_{2}+\alpha_{3}\right)+\alpha_{2}\alpha_{3}}=\frac{\alpha^{\sigma_{1}-1}_{2}\alpha^{\sigma_{1}-1}_{3}}{\left(\alpha_{2}+\alpha_{3}\right)^{\sigma_{1}}}B\left(\sigma_{1},1-\sigma_{1}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Substituting this result into Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-6"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) gives:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}=\pi^{2}\frac{B\left(\sigma_{1},1-\sigma_{1}\right)}{\Gamma\left(\sigma_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{2}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{3}\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{3}\alpha^{\left(\sigma_{1}+\sigma_{3}-1\right)-1}_{3}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{2}\frac{\alpha^{\left(\sigma_{1}+\sigma_{2}-1\right)-1}_{2}}{\left(\alpha_{2}+\alpha_{3}\right)^{\sigma_{1}}}.\label{eq:Step-7}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using the identity from Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Identity-1"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 we compute the integral over the Feynman parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

,
 yielding:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{2}\frac{\alpha^{\left(\sigma_{1}+\sigma_{2}-1\right)-1}_{2}}{\left(\alpha_{2}+\alpha_{3}\right)^{\sigma_{1}}}=\alpha^{\sigma_{2}-1}_{3}B\left(\sigma_{1}+\sigma_{2}-1,1-\sigma_{2}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Substituting this into Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Step-7"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 we find:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}=\pi^{2}\frac{B\left(\sigma_{1},1-\sigma_{1}\right)}{\Gamma\left(\sigma_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{2}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{3}\right)}B\left(\sigma_{1}+\sigma_{2}-1,1-\sigma_{2}\right)\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{3}\alpha^{\left(\sigma_{1}+\sigma_{2}+\sigma_{3}-2\right)-1}_{3}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now recall from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "donnay2020asymptotic"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 the definition of the 
\emph on
generalised 
\emph default
Dirac delta ``function,'' analytically continued to the complex plane:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(i\left(\Delta-z\right)\right)\coloneqq\frac{1}{2\pi}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\tau\,\tau^{\Delta-z-1},\label{eq:Dirac}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

such that the following identity holds:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\varphi\left(\Delta\right)=-i\int_{\mathcal{C}}dz\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(i\left(\Delta-z\right)\right)\varphi\left(z\right),
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for the contour 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{C}\coloneqq c+i\mathbf{R}$
\end_inset

.
 Consequently,
 the integral 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}$
\end_inset

 can be written in the form:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}=2\pi^{3}\frac{\Gamma\left(1-\sigma_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(1-\sigma_{2}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{1}+\sigma_{2}-1\right)}{\Gamma\left(\sigma_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{2}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{3}\right)}\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(2-\sigma_{1}-\sigma_{2}-\sigma_{3}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 by choosing the contour 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{C}=c+i\mathbf{R}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $c=\text{Re}\left(2-\sum_{i}\sigma_{i}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 we can write the following identity:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\Gamma\left(\sigma_{1}+\sigma_{2}-1\right)\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(2-\sigma_{1}-\sigma_{2}-\sigma_{3}\right)=\Gamma\left(1-\sigma_{3}\right)\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(2-\sigma_{1}-\sigma_{2}-\sigma_{3}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus,
 we arrive at the final form of the integral:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}=2\pi^{3}\frac{\Gamma\left(1-\sigma_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(1-\sigma_{2}\right)\Gamma\left(1-\sigma_{3}\right)}{\Gamma\left(\sigma_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{2}\right)\Gamma\left(\sigma_{3}\right)}\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(2-\sigma_{1}-\sigma_{2}-\sigma_{3}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset

As we see,
 the triple integral evaluates to a product of gamma functions,
 a common structure in worldsheet computations that exhibit conformal symmetry and factorisation properties of vertex operator correlation functions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Shadow Transform of Worldsheet Primaries
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Shadow-Transform-of"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this Appendix,
 we compute an integral involving the conformal primary 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{-1-j_{3}}\left(x_{3};z_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

 of the 
\begin_inset Formula $H^{+}_{3}$
\end_inset

-model.
 This calculation will prove helpful in deriving the operator product expansion in Subsection 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Operator-Product-Expansion"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

.
 We define the integral of interest as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{1}\left(j_{1},j_{2},j_{3}\big|x_{3}\right)\coloneqq\int d^{2}z_{2}\widetilde{\Phi}^{-1-j_{3}}\left(x_{3};z_{2}\right)=\int d^{2}z_{1}\int d^{2}z_{2}\frac{1}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{\sigma_{3}}}\Phi^{-1-j_{3}}\left(x_{3};z_{2}\right).\label{eq:Definition-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Before delving into the technical details of the computation,
 it’s interesting to first observe a geometric interpretation of this integral.
 The expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}_{1}$
\end_inset

 can be understood as representing the worldsheet average of the shadow transform of the conformal primary 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi^{-1-j_{3}}\left(x_{3};z_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 Recall that the shadow transform is defined as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{\Phi}^{-1-j_{3}}\left(x_{3};z_{2}\right)\coloneqq\frac{1}{N}\int d^{2}z_{1}\frac{1}{\left|z_{12}\right|^{\sigma_{3}}}\Phi^{-1-j_{3}}\left(x_{3};z_{2}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Therefore,
 the integral 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}_{1}$
\end_inset

 gives the averaged contribution of the shadow-transformed conformal primary across the worldsheet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To proceed with the calculation,
 we employ the method of Feynman 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

-parameters.
 First,
 we parametrise the complex coordinates as 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}\eqqcolon\xi_{1}+i\xi_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{2}\eqqcolon\eta_{1}+i\eta_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Reorganising Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Definition-1"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

),
 we obtain:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{1}=\frac{1}{N}\int d^{2}z_{1}d^{2}z_{2}\Phi^{-1-j_{3}}\left(x_{3};z_{2}\right)\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\sigma_{3}/2\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha\alpha^{\sigma_{3}/2-1}e^{-\alpha\left|z_{12}\right|^{2}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which simplifies to:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{I}_{1}= & \frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\sigma_{3}/2\right)}\int d\xi_{1}d\xi_{2}\int d\eta_{1}d\eta_{2}\Phi^{-1-j_{3}}\left(x_{3};\eta_{1}+i\eta_{2}\right)\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha\alpha^{\sigma_{3}/2-1}e^{-t\left(\xi_{1}-\eta_{1}\right)^{2}-t\left(\xi_{2}-\eta_{2}\right)^{2}}\\
 & =\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\sigma_{3}/2\right)}\int d\eta_{1}d\eta_{2}\Phi^{-1-j_{3}}\left(x_{3};\eta_{1}+i\eta_{2}\right)\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha\alpha^{\sigma_{3}/2-1}\left(\int d\xi_{1}e^{-t\left(\xi_{1}-\eta_{1}\right)^{2}}\right)^{2}.
\end{align}

\end_inset

Performing the Gaussian integration yields:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{1}=\frac{\pi}{\Gamma\left(\sigma_{3}/2\right)}\int d\eta_{1}d\eta_{2}\Phi^{-1-j_{3}}\left(x_{3};\eta_{1}+i\eta_{2}\right)\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha\alpha^{\left(\sigma_{3}/2-1\right)-1}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Using the definition of the generalised Dirac delta function,
 analytically continued in the complex plane (as given in Eq.
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:Dirac"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

)),
 we finally obtain:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{1}=\pi^{2}\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(\sigma_{3}-2\right)\int d^{2}z_{2}\Phi^{-1-j_{3}}\left(x_{3};z_{2}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{3}=h_{1}+h_{2}-h_{3}$
\end_inset

 gives the final expression for the integral:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{1}=\pi^{2}\boldsymbol{\delta}\left(h_{1}+h_{2}+h_{3}-2\right)\int d^{2}z_{2}\Phi^{-1-j_{3}}\left(x_{3};z_{2}\right).\label{eq:Integral-6}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Worldsheet Integral with Two Fixed Points
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Worldsheet-Integral-with"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this Appendix,
 we shall undertake the computation of a worldsheet integral which proves helpful for the evaluation of the operator product expansion as discussed in Subsection 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Operator-Product-Expansion"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{2}\left(\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2},\lambda_{3}\big|x_{1},x_{2}\right)=\frac{1}{\left|x_{1}-x_{2}\right|^{2\lambda_{3}}}\int d^{2}x_{3}\frac{1}{\left|x_{2}-x_{3}\right|^{2\lambda_{1}}\left|x_{3}-x_{1}\right|^{2\lambda_{2}}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To facilitate the computation,
 we introduce a translation 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{3}\mapsto\xi\coloneqq x_{3}-x_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}_{2}$
\end_inset

 can be recast in the form:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{2}=\frac{1}{\left|x_{1}-x_{2}\right|^{2\lambda_{3}}}\int d^{2}\xi\frac{1}{\left|\left(x_{2}-x_{1}\right)-\xi\right|^{2\lambda_{1}}\left|\xi\right|^{2\lambda_{2}}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Moreover,
 we implement a rescaling 
\begin_inset Formula $y\mapsto\xi=\left|x_{2}-x_{1}\right|y$
\end_inset

,
 which transforms our integral into:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{2}=\frac{1}{\left|x_{1}-x_{2}\right|^{2\left(\lambda_{1}+\lambda_{2}+\lambda_{3}-1\right)}}\int d^{2}y\frac{1}{\left|y-\hat{x}\right|^{2\lambda_{1}}\left|y\right|^{2\lambda_{2}}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}\coloneqq\left(x_{2}-x_{1}\right)/\left|x_{2}-x_{1}\right|$
\end_inset

.
 To proceed with the evaluation of this expression,
 we focus on the integral:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}\left(\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}\right)\coloneqq\int d^{2}y\frac{1}{\left|y-\hat{x}\right|^{2\lambda_{1}}\left|y\right|^{2\lambda_{2}}},
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so that the final result for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{I}_{2}$
\end_inset

 will be expressed as:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{2}=\frac{\mathcal{F}\left(\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}\right)}{\left|x_{1}-x_{2}\right|^{2\left(\lambda_{1}+\lambda_{2}+\lambda_{3}-1\right)}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At this stage,
 we introduce the Feynman 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

-parameters,
 leading to the following representations:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\left|y-\hat{x}\right|^{2\lambda_{1}}}=\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\lambda_{1}\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{1}\alpha^{\lambda_{1}-1}_{1}e^{-\alpha_{1}\left|y-\hat{x}\right|^{2}},\,\,\,\frac{1}{\left|y\right|^{2\lambda_{2}}}=\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\lambda_{2}\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{2}\alpha^{\lambda_{2}-1}_{2}e^{-\alpha_{2}\left|y\right|^{2}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We then parametrise 
\begin_inset Formula $y\eqqcolon y_{1}+iy_{2}$
\end_inset

 over the complex plane,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}\eqqcolon a_{1}+ia_{2}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $a^{2}_{1}+a^{2}_{2}=1$
\end_inset

),
 yielding:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}=\frac{1}{\Gamma\left(\lambda_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\lambda_{2}\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{1}\alpha^{\lambda_{1}-1}_{1}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{2}\alpha^{\lambda_{2}-1}_{2}\int dy_{1}dy_{2}e^{-\alpha_{1}\left(y_{1}-a_{1}\right)^{2}-\alpha_{1}\left(y_{2}-a_{2}\right)^{2}-\alpha_{2}y^{2}_{1}-\alpha_{2}y^{2}_{2}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Performing the Gaussian integrals gives:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}=\frac{\pi}{\Gamma\left(\lambda_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\lambda_{2}\right)}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{1}\alpha^{\lambda_{1}-1}_{1}e^{-\alpha_{1}}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{2}\alpha^{\lambda_{2}-1}_{2}\frac{1}{\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}}e^{\frac{\alpha^{2}_{1}}{\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}}}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

By introducing the variable 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta\coloneqq\alpha_{1}/\left(\alpha_{1}+\alpha_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 we obtain:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}=\frac{\pi}{\Gamma\left(\lambda_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\lambda_{2}\right)}\int^{1}_{0}d\zeta\zeta^{-\lambda_{2}}\left(1-\zeta\right)^{\lambda_{2}-1}\int^{\infty}_{0}d\alpha_{1}\alpha^{\left(\lambda_{1}+\lambda_{2}-1\right)-1}_{1}e^{-\alpha_{1}\left(1-\zeta\right)}.
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Finally,
 solving the integral over 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

 by rescaling 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha'_{1}\coloneqq\left(1-\zeta\right)\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 we arrive at:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}=\pi\frac{\Gamma\left(\lambda_{1}+\lambda_{2}-1\right)}{\Gamma\left(\lambda_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\lambda_{2}\right)}\int^{1}_{0}d\zeta\zeta^{-\lambda_{2}}\left(1-\zeta\right)^{-\lambda_{1}}=\pi\frac{\Gamma\left(\lambda_{1}+\lambda_{2}-1\right)}{\Gamma\left(2-\lambda_{1}-\lambda_{2}\right)}B\left(1-\lambda_{1},1-\lambda_{2}\right).
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus,
 the final form of our integral is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{I}_{2}=\frac{\pi}{\left|x_{1}-x_{2}\right|^{2\left(\lambda_{1}+\lambda_{2}+\lambda_{3}-1\right)}}\frac{\Gamma\left(\lambda_{1}+\lambda_{2}-1\right)}{\Gamma\left(2-\lambda_{1}-\lambda_{2}\right)}\frac{\Gamma\left(1-\lambda_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(1-\lambda_{2}\right)}{\Gamma\left(\lambda_{1}\right)\Gamma\left(\lambda_{2}\right)}.\label{eq:Integral-7}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


